const SA_QUESTIONS = [{"id":"2015-Q21","year":2015,"n":21,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.1","FA1.2.4","FA1.2.2"],"img":"2015_Q21_sa.png","w":1365,"h":155,"criteria":"• Sketches advantages of public health care in Australia                                       3\n    • Sketches an advantage of public health care in Australia\n    OR                                                                                              2\n    • Identifies advantages of the public health care system\n    • Identifies an advantage of public health care in Australia                                    1","sample":"Australia's public health care system is supported by Medicare. Medicare allows Australians\nto receive the basic level of health care, including their own choice of general practitioner, or\nwhether to seek private health cover. Medicare provides free or subsidised medical treatment\n(Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme) where costs for Medicare is subsidised or free for chronic\nillnesses. Medicare also provides free public hospital care. Medicare is a diverse system\nwhich applies for all Australians.\n\nAnswers could include:\n•     Access\n•     Free if GP bulk bills/subsidised GP visits\n•     Subsidised medication on PBS\n•     Free treatment in public hospitals\n\n                                                      -2-","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2015-Q24","year":2015,"n":24,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system","Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.2.1","FA1.1.2","FA1.2.4"],"img":"2015_Q24_sa.png","w":1365,"h":194,"criteria":"•   Makes a judgement of the degree of equity in access to health care\n        facilities/services\n    •   Makes evident the relationship between specific health care                                   8\n        facilities/services and equity of access for all Australians\n    •   Provides evidence to support the judgement\n    •   Makes a judgement about the equity of access to health care\n        facilities/services\n    •   Provides accurate reasons to support their judgements about the equity of                    6-7\n        access to health care facilities/services for all Australians\n    •   Provides relevant examples\n    •   Provides features about the equity of access to health care\n        facilities/services for all Australians                                                      4-5\n    •   Provides examples\n    •   Sketches in general terms about the equity of access to health care\n                                                                                                     2-3\n        facilities/services\n    •   Provides some relevant information about access to health care\n                                                                                                      1\n        facilities/services","sample":"It is to a moderate extent that health care facilities and services are equitable for all\nAustralians as there are significant concerns for subgroups of the population including\nAboriginal and Torres Strait Islanders (ATSIS), those of low socioeconomic status (SES),\nthose living in rural and remote areas, and migrant groups who experience a lower quality of\nhealth. ATSIS experience a significantly lower life expectancy (12 years less) than non-\nATSIS and are more likely to die from preventative causes of disease. Although there are\nprograms to enable them to access medical services, they still experience a significantly lower\nhealth status. This is shared by those of lower SES, who have limited access to health services\nbecause of lack of affordability. Although Medicare provides free treatment for all, the choice\nof doctor or hospital is not allowed and there are usually long waiting lists for elective\nsurgery. Similarly those living in rural and remote areas experience accessibility issues as a\nresult of distance and the generally limited amount of health services available. The\ngovernment, aware of this inequitable distribution, funds the Royal Flying Doctor service in\nan attempt to provide health care assistance to those in rural and remote areas with great\nsuccess. In addition, migrant populations generally experience inequitable services as a result\nof language barriers. Governments have made attempt to change this through provision of\ninformation in a multiplicity of languages. Thus, although there is significant difference\nbetween the accessibility and affordability of services the government is making efforts to\namend this.\n\nAnswers could include:\n•       Equity: Medicare, public health care services/facilities, Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme\n•       Inequities related to socioeconomic, sociocultural or environmental determinants\n•       Health promotion initiatives designed to address equity issues\n•       Groups experiencing health inequities, eg Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples,\n        people living in rural and remote areas, overseas born people, socioeconomically\n        disadvantaged people, the elderly, people with disabilities\n\n                                                        -5-","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2015-Q25","year":2015,"n":25,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.1","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2015_Q25_sa.png","w":1298,"h":145,"criteria":"•   Makes evident how flexibility can improve athletic performance\n                                                                                                  3\n•   Provides an example\n•   Sketches in general terms about how flexibility can improve performance                       2\n•   Provides relevant information about flexibility                                               1","sample":"Flexibility is extremely important for improving performance. Flexibility allows a wider\nrange of motion for muscle groups which can be used to the performer's advantage when\nparticipating in sport or exercise. Having good flexibility also reduces the chance of injury.\nHaving good flexibility means that muscles can stretch much more during activity without\noverstretching, eg a sprinter can take bigger steps giving him an advantage.\n\nAnswers could include:\n• Improved range of motion\n• Reduced risk of injury\n• Flexibility training","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2015-Q32(a)","year":2015,"n":32,"part":"(a)","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment","Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.2","FA2.3.1"],"img":"2015_Q32_a.png","w":1365,"h":83,"criteria":"•   Discusses the extent and differentiation of planning needed for elite and\n    amateur athletes\n•   Makes the relationship evident between differentiated planning and                            8\n    performance of elite and amateur athletes\n•   Provides relevant examples\n•   Provides reasons for the differing planning considerations for elite and\n    amateur athletes\n•   Recognises a relationship between planning and performance of elite and                      6-7\n    amateur athletes\n•   Provides relevant examples\n•   Provides characteristics and features of planning for elite and amateur\n    athletes                                                                                     4-5\n•   Provides an example\n•   Sketches in general terms planning considerations for elite or amateur\n                                                                                                 2-3\n    athletes\n•   Provides some relevant information about planning for performance                             1","sample":"Planning for performance will differ when comparing the training and preparation of elite and\namateur athletes. Elite athletes are often fully devoted to their sport and are full-time athletes,\nwhile amateur athletes may be required to balance work and other commitments with their\nsporting pursuits.\nThe performance and fitness needs of elite and amateur athletes while comparable in some\nsports, generally will differ due to the schedule of events and competitions. Elite athletes may\nparticipate in a wide variety of tournaments/competitions across the world, while amateur\nathletes may focus more on the local season of competition, possibly including national titles.\nAs such, elite athletes may be required to have a vastly different training plan and be required\nto peak several times a year for major events, while amateur athletes may be required to peak\nfor season-ending events as part of the local competition. This will result in the fitness needs\nof these athletes differing. While the amateur athletes can plan for a season of competition,\nelite athletes may be required to plan for short term goals such as a season of local\ncompetition, as well as longer term goals of international competitions, such as world cup\nevents and major world events such as the Olympics. As these major events occur every four\nyears, these elite athletes will need to manage their performance and fitness needs across a\nmuch longer time span.\nElite athletes may be required to prepare themselves for a variety of climatic conditions,\ndependent upon where they intend to compete. For example, an elite marathon runner in\nAustralia may be required to prepare for a race in the United States that would be occurring\n‘out of season' in Australia. As the summer months are opposite in the USA to Australia, the\nmarathon runner may have to adjust their training load to allow them to peak at the\nappropriate time for the race in the USA. They may have to base themselves overseas or in a\nwarmer climate to match the anticipated race conditions so that they are not at a disadvantage\nwhen they arrive for the race. Amateur athletes, faced with the same problem, may not be able\nto afford the same training environments and opportunities and are immediately at a\ndisadvantage.\n\n                                                   - 19 -","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2016-Q22","year":2016,"n":22,"part":"","marks":7,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2016_Q22_sa.png","w":1364,"h":235,"criteria":"• Makes evident the relationship between the determinants and the health\n  inequities in the context of ONE priority population group other than\n  Indigenous Australians                                                                         7\n• Provides examples that demonstrate the relationship between the\n  determinants and the health inequities experienced by the group\n• Provides characteristics and features of the determinants that create health\n  inequities experienced by ONE priority population group other than\n  Indigenous Australians                                                                        5-6\n• Provides relevant examples of the determinants that create the health\n  inequities experienced by the group\n• Provides characteristics and features of a determinant that creates health\n  inequities experienced by ONE priority population group other than\n  Indigenous Australians\nOR\n                                                                                                3-4\n• Sketches in general terms determinants that create the health inequities\n  experienced by ONE priority population group other than Indigenous\n  Australians\n• Provides relevant example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms about determinant(s) that create health\n  inequities OR a priority population group that experiences health                              2\n  inequities\n• Names a determinant of health inequities OR a priority population group\n                                                                                                 1\n  other than Indigenous Australians","sample":"Individuals from low socioeconomic backgrounds suffer higher rates of chronic diseases\nincluding CVD, cancer and diabetes type II, as well as higher rates of injury and mental health\nissues. Socioeconomic determinants of education, income and employment can contribute to\nthese inequities. Lower levels of education can lead to unhealthy lifestyle choices and\nincreased consumption of high fat diets, contributing to increased prevalence of obesity, CVD\nand cancer. Lower levels of education can also lead to unemployment or reduced employment\nopportunities, which then reduces income, and can limit the affordability of choices such as\ngetting a gym membership to engage in regular exercise. Environmental determinants\nincluding geographical location can limit an individual's access to technology and health\nservices. People of low socioeconomic status may live in rural areas with limited access to\nadvanced diagnostic testing and technology eg CT scans and breast screening, which can then\nincrease their chance of death from some cancers due to late diagnosis. Sociocultural factors\nincluding media, culture, religion, peers and family can have an impact on health inequities.\nPeople with low socioeconomic status are more likely to learn poor lifestyle decisions and\nrisk behaviours through peers and family which can contribute to higher risk-taking\nbehaviours eg, tobacco smoking which can lead to lung cancer or increased injuries resulting\nin hospitalisation. Furthermore, people from low socioeconomic backgrounds may lack\naccess to media health promotion initiatives which can limit their protective factors,\ncontributing to increased lifestyle diseases such as lung cancer.\n\n                                                   -3-","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2016-Q26","year":2016,"n":26,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.3.3"],"img":"2016_Q26_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"• Provides arguments to support the psychological strategies used by\n  athletes to enhance their motivation and manage anxiety\n                                                                                                  8\n• Relates cause and effect between the psychological strategies and their\n  ability to enhance motivation and manage anxiety\n• Provides characteristics and features of relevant psychological strategies\n  that enhance motivation and manage anxiety\n                                                                                                 6-7\n• Makes the relationships evident between psychological strategies and their\n  ability to enhance motivation and manage anxiety\n• Provides characteristics and features of psychological strategies that\n  enhance motivation and manage anxiety\n                                                                                                 4-5\n• Provides the effect of psychological strategies on their ability to enhance\n  motivation AND/OR manage anxiety\n• Sketches in general terms a psychological strategy(ies) that manages\n                                                                                                 2-3\n  anxiety AND/OR enhance motivation\n• Names a psychological strategy used to enhance motivation OR manage\n  anxiety\n                                                                                                  1\nOR\n• Provides some relevant information","sample":"Athletes enhance their motivation and manage anxiety by using the following psychological\nstrategies: concentration/attention skills, mental rehearsal, visualisation, imagery, goal setting\nand relaxation techniques.\n\nGoal setting is initially used at the start of the training year to set realistic objectives and\nperformance expectations. This increases levels of motivation resulting in adherence to\ntraining schedule and intensity required for improved performance. An athlete who is bored\nand lacking direction will benefit from goal setting, resulting in a greater interest and focus in\ntraining, which will then improve an athletes performance.\n\nRelaxation techniques such as progressive relaxation can assist an athlete whose performance\nsuffers due to high levels of anxiety. High levels of anxiety can lead to: tense muscles,\nmuscular fatigue and reduced focus. Progressive relaxation before an event helps to reduce\nmuscle tension and improve focus so that performance is improved or at an optimal level,\ninstead of being affected by a high level of anxiety.\n\nMental rehearsal involves recreating in your mind a mental image of yourself successfully\npractising a skill or movement performance. This increases an athlete's familiarity with the\nskill, increasing confidence and reducing anxiety. Mental rehearsal would definitely benefit\nan athlete at who lacks confidence and has a high level of anxiety, thus improving their\nperformance.\n\n                                                    -7-","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2016-Q30(a)","year":2016,"n":30,"part":"(a)","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.5"],"img":"2016_Q30_a.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"•   Makes evident the advantages and disadvantages related to drug testing of\n    elite athletes\n•   Makes evident the relationship between drug testing and the positive and                      8\n    negative outcomes\n•   Provides relevant examples\n•   Identifies advantages and disadvantages related to drug testing of elite\n    athletes\n•   Makes a link(s) between drug testing and the positive and negative                           6-7\n    outcomes\n•   May provide examples\n•   Identifies the advantages and disadvantages related to drug testing of elite\n    athletes                                                                                     4-5\n•   Provides characteristics and features of major issues related to drug testing\n•   Sketches in general terms issues related to drug testing of elite athletes                   2-3\n•   Names issues related to drug testing                                                          1","sample":"There are many pros and cons associated with drug testing. One of the problems with\nmandatory drug testing is that it directly violates an elite athlete's rights by deviating from the\nnotion of freedom and privacy. Even an elite athlete may freely make the choice to take\nperformance-enhancing drugs, with some understanding of the potential consequences.\nMandatory drug testing denies the option of not having to undergo a drug test.\n\nIn sport mandatory drug testing is considered ethically acceptable with the view that it can\nserve as a deterrent against use of performance-enhancing drugs. The use of performance-\nenhancing drugs such as anabolic steroids is associated with cardiovascular disease, cancer\nand premature death. Mandatory drug testing serves as a course of protection against harm,\nmeaning that drug testing in sport is actually in the best interest of elite athletes, should they\nchoose to abide by the World Anti Doping Association guidelines.\n\nElite athletes who fail mandatory drug tests should be disqualified and banned for repeat\noffences. The benefit of tough rules is that they create a level playing field in sport.\n\nA limitation of drug testing is the difference between what sports and sporting organisations\ndefine as ‘banned substance'. So, an elite athlete caught using a particular substance in one\nsport may be able to use it in another. There are different rules for different elite athletes.\nThis can also make it confusing for elite athletes who need to have a clear understanding of\nthe rules and sanctions for their sport's governing body. For example the IOC guidelines are\nonly relevant during the Olympic Games; outside this major event sporting bodies set their\nown rules.\n\n                                                   - 17 -","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2016-Q30(b)","year":2016,"n":30,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.3.1","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2016_Q30_b.png","w":1364,"h":233,"criteria":"•   Makes a judgement of the value of each element used to design a training                    11-12\n    session\n•   Makes evident the relationship between the elements of a training session\n    and improving performance\n•   Provides relevant examples to support response\n•   Makes evident the relationship between the elements of a training session                   8-10\n    and improving performance\n•   Provides relevant examples to support response\n•   Provides characteristics and features of the elements used to design a                       5-7\n    training session\n•   Provides relevant examples\n•   Sketches in general terms the elements used to design a training session                     3-4\n•   Recognises and names elements used to design a training session                              1-2","sample":"Every coach will develop their own approach to planning a session. However, there are\nspecific elements that are contained in any session for athletes. The first element is health and\nsafety. This involves the coach checking with the athlete if there are any medical problems or\nconcerns. In addition the playing space and equipment needs to be checked for suitability and\nsafety. By commencing the session in this manner, medical issues can be prevented or\nreduced, and therefore is important in looking after the wellbeing of the athlete.\n\nThe next element is providing an overview of the session. The overview should be brief and\nallow the athlete to prepare mentally for the session. This is important as it also allows the\nathlete to set session goals around the training sets or the overall session.\n\nWarm-up and cool down are essential parts of the session which assist the athlete to prepare\nmentally and physically for the session. The warm-up consists of a general gross motor\nactivity designed to work the specific muscular groups to be used during the session. The\nbenefit is that the body experiences increased blood flow and increased movement around the\njoints. To complement the warm-up is dynamic stretching which enhances flexibility slightly\nbut also enhances the response of the muscles eg increased muscle explosiveness.\n\nSkills instruction and practice are also elements of a training session. Skills instruction needs\nto be brief so as not to reduce the benefits attained by the warm-up. This provides the\nopportunity for the coach to instruct on what to do during skill development and practice.\nSkill practice is essential if athletes are to improve their biomechanical efficiency or correct\npoor technique.\n\nConditioning is a vital component of the training session. Physiological adaptations required\nfor competition are attained through the conditioning phase of the training session.\n\nThe evaluation element of the training session is important for providing and receiving\nfeedback. For example at the end of the session the coach will ask the athlete for perceived\nrate of exertion to assess the intensity of the training session.\n\n                                                   - 18 -","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2016-Q31(b)","year":2016,"n":31,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2016_Q31_b.png","w":1297,"h":105,"criteria":"•   Makes clear judgement(s) on how different factors create health inequities\n•   Makes evident the relationship between the inequities and the factors in\n                                                                                               11-12\n    relation to a population group\n•   Provides relevant examples linked to a population group\n•   Provides clear reasons showing how different factors create health\n    inequalities\n•   Provides characteristics and features of the inequities and the factors in                 9-10\n    relation to a population group\n•   Provides relevant examples\n•   Provides characteristics of different factors in relation to a population\n    group                                                                                       6-8\n•   May provide an example(s)\n•   Sketches in general terms the inequities of a population group                              3-5\n•   Provides some relevant information on health inequities of population\n                                                                                                1-2\n    group(s)","sample":"There are many factors that are out of an individual's control that hinder the chance of good\nhealth for some population groups. Socioeconomic status and rural and remoteness are\nconsidered to cause inequities.\nSocioeconomic status can contribute to their standard of living. People with high SES have\nincreased choices and opportunities, for example housing and areas in which they live and\nincreased choice in nutritional foods. Those with low SES tend to be unhealthier and can be\nlinked to a poorer attitude towards maintaining health. This is evident in epidemiological data\ncollected showing that they are more likely to access primary and secondary health facilities\nincluding doctors and hospitals rather than a preventative health service like immunisation\nprograms or dental check-ups. Their lifestyles are often riskier, with poor diets, lack of\nexercise and higher rates of smoking and drinking. All of these factors lead to an increase in\nthe morbidity and mortality of low SES from chronic lifestyle diseases including CVD,\ncancer and diabetes. Due to their lower levels of income people of low socioeconomic status\nare less likely to or be able to purchase medicines to treat minor illnesses and their\nnutritionally poor diets lower their immunity to many infections. Low SES people are less\nlikely to access and be able to afford private health insurance which increases their waiting\ntimes for elective preventative health procedures including breast screening or colonoscopy\nfor bowel cancer. This has a major impact on their health as prevention of diseases is more\nsuccessful than the treatment down the track. Ultimately a disparity in SES and health status\nwill lower overall health in a society as many diseases are not confined by households in\nwhich the diseases occur.\nDue to the lower levels of education and lower income occupations associated with low SES,\nboth education and occupation can be a significant risk factor for an individual's health.\nOccupations carry an element of risk that can affect a person's health. High stress, exposure\nto radiation, or heavy strain can lead to a debilitating injury or physical condition that can\nreduce a person's quality of life. Some blue-collar occupations can expose workers to toxins\nor carcinogens increasing their risk of developing cancers or respiratory dysfunction through\ninhaling vapours which have a significant negative impact on their health. Generally a\nperson's education has a large determining influence on the level of income earned. There is\nalso greater potential for people who stay in school to have an increased level of health\nliteracy. This is the ability to understand and interpret health information and use it to\npromote and maintain good health. Young people who leave school early and remain\n\n                                                  - 20 -\n\nunemployed are at greater risk of developing poor mental health and more likely to remain in\nlow SES throughout their life.\nTherefore, governments should actually invest heavily in the education of people if they want\nto reduce health inequities rather than spend money in treating disease in the health sector.\nWhen it comes to low-SES and health, prevention with education is better then hospital care.\n\n                                                  - 21 -\n\nPersonal Development, Health and Physical Education","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2017-Q22","year":2017,"n":22,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.4"],"img":"2017_Q22_sa.png","w":1364,"h":157,"criteria":"•   Makes evident the relationship between the risk factors and CVD\n•   Makes evident the relationship between protective factors and CVD\n                                                                                               5\n•   Provides examples of both risk and protective factors that demonstrate\n    their relationship with CVD\n•   Provides characteristics and features of the risk and/or protective factors of\n    CVD                                                                                       3-4\n•   Provides examples of risk and/or protective factors\n•   Sketches in general terms the risk and/or protective factors of CVD\n                                                                                               2\n•   Provides a relevant example\n•   Names risk or protective factors of CVD                                                    1","sample":"Risk factors are those behaviours that increase the chance of developing CVD. These include\nnon-modifiable factors (eg age, sex, family history) and modifiable factors (obesity, high\nblood pressure, smoking and alcohol consumption). The risk of CVD is increased for young\nmales compared to young females due to the protective nature of oestrogen women\nexperience pre-menopause. High blood pressure and smoking increase the risk of CVD due to\nthe increased stress these activities place on the heart and vascular tissues. Specifically these\nfactors result in atherosclerosis.\n\nGood nutrition and regular physical activity are protective factors of CVD. A diet high in\nfibre and unprocessed foods (especially vegetables) can reduce hypertension and plaque\nbuild-up on vascular walls, while regular physical activity increases cardiovascular fitness.\nThese both reduce the risk of developing CVD and can even be used as a means of treating\nthose with CVD.\n\n                                                    -3-","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2017-Q23","year":2017,"n":23,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians","The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.1.5","FA1.2.1","FA1.2.4"],"img":"2017_Q23_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"•   Makes judgement(s) about the extent to which having a healthy ageing\n    population benefits Australia's healthcare system\n•   Makes the relationship between healthy ageing and burden on Australia's                    8\n    healthcare system evident\n•   Provides examples that support the judgement made\n•   Makes the relationship between healthy ageing and burden on Australia's\n    healthcare system evident                                                                 6-7\n•   Provides example(s) that support the relationship\n•   Provides characteristics and features of healthy ageing and its affects on\n                                                                                              4-5\n    Australia's healthcare system\n•   Sketches in general terms healthy ageing in Australia and its relationship\n                                                                                              2-3\n    to healthcare\n•   Provides some relevant information about Australia's growing and ageing\n                                                                                               1\n    population","sample":"Aged people consume more health resources than any other sector of the Australian\npopulation. Healthy ageing is when people maintain good health into old age. This can have a\npositive impact on health care in Australia. Healthy ageing can result from positive health\nbehaviours and choices people have made throughout their life (eg participating in regular\nphysical activity, good dietary choices, maintaining strong social bonds). These choices\nthroughout life minimise the reliance on healthcare services as one ages as these individuals\nsignificantly reduce their risk of most non-communicable diseases and mental illness.\n\nEncouraging the aged to stay healthy and live in their own homes takes pressure off\nhealthcare infrastructure. Government initiatives support this by providing more community\nnurses to assess and treat elderly people in their homes if needed. By allowing these\ncommunity healthcare workers to also administer and monitor medication, healthy elderly\npeople can maintain largely independent lifestyles and not use hospitals and doctors' offices\nfor routine health concerns.\n\nHealthy ageing populations also become less dependent on volunteering organisations and\noften become a significant component of their workforce. Many healthy aged individuals\ncontribute to groups like Meals on Wheels and Community Transport.\n\nA healthy aged individual is therefore able to have access to programs that enable them to\nremain in their own home and socially connected to their community. With a growing aged\npopulation, ageing healthily reduces one's dependence on healthcare systems like hospitals\nand nursing homes and therefore reduces the use by this population of our healthcare system.\n\nAnswers could include:\n• Reduced occurrence of chronic/lifestyle diseases\n• Able to remain in the workforce therefore can remain in health care professionals\n• Remaining productive and independent decreases risk of mental health concerns which are\n  a major health issue in the population\n• Reduced money spent on healthcare of the elderly which may then be spent on other health\n  research areas of health promotion.\n\n                                                    -4-","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2017-Q24","year":2017,"n":24,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sleep, nutrition and supplements"],"sids":["FA2.4.3","FA2.4.1"],"img":"2017_Q24_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"•   Makes evident the relationship between specific vitamins and/or minerals\n        and athletic performance                                                                    5\n    •   Provides relevant examples of how vitamins/minerals affect performance\n    •   Provides characteristics and features of vitamins and/or minerals that\n        affect athletic performance                                                                 4\n    •   Provides relevant example(s) of how vitamins/minerals affect performance\n    •   Sketches in general terms how specific vitamin and/or mineral(s) affect\n        performance                                                                                2-3\n    •   Provides relevant example(s)\n    •   Recognises and names vitamin(s) or mineral(s) that affect athletic\n                                                                                                    1\n        performance","sample":"Iron and calcium are minerals which affect performance. Iron is found in the red blood cells\nand attaches itself to carry oxygen around the body. If an athlete does not have sufficient iron\nin their red blood cells, then the body's ability to carry oxygen is diminished leading to\npremature fatigue. For example, an anaemic marathon runner will fatigue earlier in an event\nthan one with optimal levels of blood iron.\n\nCalcium is important for bone density. If an athlete is deficient in calcium, they will be\nsusceptible to developing brittle bones, osteoporosis and hard tissue injuries. For example,\nfemale gymnasts with poor levels of dietary calcium are more prone to developing lower limb\nstress fractures.\n\nAnswers could include:\n•       Iron - improves oxygen carrying capacity for aerobic events\n•       Calcium - improves bone density\n•       Vitamin C - assists immune system\n•       Vitamin A - energy levels, immune system\n•       Vitamin B - energy levels\n•       Vitamin D - bone density\n•       Potassium - energy levels\n•       Magnesium - muscle soreness, cramping\n•       Sodium - cramping, may assist with hydration.\n\n                                                         -5-","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2017-Q26","year":2017,"n":26,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.3","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2017_Q26_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"• Makes evident how the physiological adaptations individuals develop\n  respond to a variety of principles of training\n                                                                                               8\n• Provides examples that demonstrate the relationship between the training\n  principles and the physiological adaptation\n• Provides characteristics and features of physiological adaptations in\n  relation to the principles of training                                                      6-7\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of principles of training AND/OR\n  physiological adaptations\nOR                                                                                            4-5\n• Sketches in general terms physiological adaptations to the principles of\n  training\n• Sketches in general terms a physiological adaptation to a principle of\n  training\nOR                                                                                            2-3\n• Sketches in general terms physiological adaptation(s) OR principle(s) of\n  training\n• Recognises and names physiological adaptations AND/OR principles of\n  training\n                                                                                               1\nOR\n• Sketches in general terms types of training","sample":"When an athlete undertakes a training program, a variety of physiological adaptations occur in\nresponse to the stress that the body is placed under. The different principles of training will\ndetermine what type of physiological adaptation occurs.\n\nWhen training aerobically, progressive overload and training thresholds are important. This\ninvolves the body working in the aerobic training zone (60−80%) while also gradually\nincreasing the working load of the athlete, eg increasing distance or time an athlete runs, in\norder to gradually increase the training load and performance. The physiological adaptations\nthat occur include a more powerful stroke volume and increases in cardiac output. The heart\nbecomes more effective as it is a stronger muscle and can pump more oxygen with each beat.\nThis also leads to a reduction in resting and working heart rate for a fit aerobic athlete.\nCardiac output is more effective as a result of these training principles as oxygen is delivered\nwith more efficiency to the working muscles through aerobic events.\n\nIn relation to strength training, specificity is important. By manipulating the number of\nrepetitions and size of the weight, an athlete can either develop muscular strength or muscular\nendurance. Both types of training result in muscle hypertrophy. However, the effect on fast\ntwitch or slow twitch development will be determined by the specificity of the training\nemployed. For example a high jumper will use specific power and plyometric exercises to\ndevelop their fast twitch muscle capabilities.\n\n                                                    -7-","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2017-Q29(a)","year":2017,"n":29,"part":"(a)","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.5.4","FA2.2.2","FA2.2.1"],"img":"2017_Q29_a.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"•   Makes the relationship evident between an athlete's physical preparation\n    and their prevention of injury\n                                                                                               8\n•   Provides relevant examples in a range of different sports that demonstrate\n    the relationship between physical preparation and injury prevention\n•   Identifies issues and provides points that support the physical preparation\n    of athletes in order to prevent injury                                                    6-7\n•   Provides relevant examples in a range of different sports\n•   Provides characteristics and features of how an athlete prepares physically\n    to prevent injury in different sports                                                     4-5\n•   Provides example(s)\n•   Sketches in general terms how athletes prevent injury\n                                                                                              2-3\n•   May provide examples\n•   Provides some relevant information about injury prevention                                 1","sample":"Correct skills and techniques are needed in all sports to ensure the safety of all players. These\nskills and techniques should always be taught correctly in training sessions prior to\ncompetition to enhance performance and reduce injury. For example, tackling in football. All\nplayers need to learn the correct technique to tackle safely to prevent head, neck or shoulder\ninjuries to them or their opposition. Practising the correct technique for tackling with the use\nof protective equipment and supervision is vital to prevent injury for the athlete and others.\n\nWhen an athlete has good physical fitness they reduce the likelihood of injury. This involves\na balance of both health-related and skill-related components of fitness because as the body\ngets tired, good skill and technique minimise the risk of injury. For example a tennis player is\nreturning to competition after having a few months off and he runs in towards the net to return\na volley. On execution he isn't quick enough to get there, so he over-stretches and injures his\nshoulder.\n\nWarm up, stretching and cool down are all important physical preparation to prevent injury.\nMost warm up sessions would include a combination of cardiovascular and stretching\nexercises to increase circulation, increase body temperature and increase the elasticity in the\nmuscles. For example, before a basketball game an athlete will move up and down the court\nto increase circulation and body temperature. Keeping her body warm will prevent acute\ninjuries and prepare the body steadily and safely. If she is a substitute she would also be\nencouraged to keep her body warm by continuing to run and stretch while she is waiting to\njoin the game.\n\n                                                   - 13 -","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2017-Q31(b)","year":2017,"n":31,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.4","FA1.2.2","FA1.2.1"],"img":"2017_Q31_b.png","w":1364,"h":118,"criteria":"•   Makes a judgement of how effective government funding is in improving\n    the health status of Australians\n•   Provides details on the nature of government funding in relation to\n    addressing the health status of Australians                                              11-12\n•   Provides relevant examples to support the judgement\n•   Provides a logical and cohesive response\n•   Makes evident the relationship between government funding and the\n    health status of Australians\n                                                                                             8-10\n•   Provides points relevant to the nature of government funding in Australia\n•   Provides examples that support the relationship that exists\n•   Provides characteristics and features of government funding and the nature\n    of health status in Australia                                                             5-7\n•   Provides relevant examples\n•   Sketches in general terms government funding strategies and/or the wider\n    implication on the health status of Australians                                           3-4\n•   May provide examples\n•   Provides relevant information about government funding of Australian\n                                                                                              1-2\n    health","sample":"State governments are the main providers of public access to health services that include\npublic hospitals, public health services and home and community care. The NSW\nGovernment will spend approximately $20.6 billion on health this year and this money was\nallocated around the state based on the number of people living in a particular area, priority\nhealth issues and specific health needs of a community. Money can help cure sick people by\nallowing them to use the public health system to visit a doctor or hospital. This is effective\nbecause it leads to higher life expectancy and decreased mortality rates in Australia.\n\nHowever, it is not that simple. Hospitals and doctors can help treat sick people but they\ncannot achieve their health potential until they are able to take control of the areas that\ninfluence their health. If we use an example, a 40-year-old male who doesn't speak English, is\na heavy drinker, a regular smoker, is physically inactive and who recently had an emergency\nheart-bypass operation. It was a success and he returned home, however he continued\nsmoking, drinking and did not engage in any exercise. The funding to assist this surgery\nresulted in him still being alive, but how long will this last before he needs another operation\nor loses his life? He needs to be educated on a healthy lifestyle because people need\nknowledge and skills to live a healthier life and funding alone cannot solve health inequities.\n\nIt is known that funding is provided for specific populations in Australia. The unemployed,\npeople with a disability and the aged are entitled to an allowance from the Department of\nHuman Services to enable them to satisfy their basic needs. This can be effective in\nimproving their health status as they are able to buy clothes, food and shelter. Federal funding\nhas also been allocated to improve healthcare for Indigenous communities in remote areas of\nAustralia. Yet, the aged, the unemployed, people with a disability and Indigenous\ncommunities are population groups in our society still experiencing health inequities.\n\nTo minimise health inequities, funding alone cannot help. People who are from disadvantaged\ncommunities need empowerment. This can be done through services responding to the\n\n                                                   - 20 -\n\npriorities identified by the relevant community. This gains the trust and respect of\ndisadvantaged communities. To be effective the service needs to ensure their approach is\nsustainable and effective in engaging socially excluded groups in health. An example could be\nwhen the doctors at the local Medical Centre notice a lot of their patients experiencing poor\nmental health. They organise a free monthly fun run to promote physical activity and increase\nsocial interaction. Everyone is encouraged to attend because they modify the event for the\naged (they can volunteer or take a shorter track), the disabled (it is wheelchair and visually\nimpaired friendly) and the unemployed (as it is free). This attempts to solve health inequities\nby empowering disadvantaged groups in the community.\n\nThe government provides funding to services where there is limited resources but this is often\ndone through prioritising where the money is best spent. For example, if you visit a public\nhospital and have a broken arm you are often waiting a very long time because more serious\ninjuries are seen first. Or in rural areas there are limited services such as hospitals and medical\ncentres. Therefore the local council tries to attract doctors and nurses to their community by\nfinancially supporting their salary, travel and accommodation. This is an effective initiative\nfor those disadvantaged groups living in rural and remote locations.\n\n                                                    - 21 -\n\nPersonal Development, Health and Physical Education","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2018-Q22","year":2018,"n":22,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.4","FA1.1.1","FA1.1.2"],"img":"2018_Q22_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"• Provides characteristics and features of the determinants of health that\n  affect a preventable condition other than cancer or CVD                                       4\n• Supports with relevant examples\n• Sketches in general terms the determinants of health that affect a\n  preventable condition other than cancer or CVD\n  OR\n                                                                                               2-3\n• Sketches in general terms the determinants of health that affect a\n  condition\n• May provide example(s)\n• Provides some relevant information on the determinants of health OR a\n                                                                                                1\n  preventable condition","sample":"Mental health is a preventable condition affecting many Australians.\n\nSociocultural determinants include the influence of peers, media and societal pressures. For\nmany women, body image influences their feelings of self-confidence and happiness. They\noften feel pressured to diet, as a result of the media images and expectations to look thin.\nThis need for society and peer acceptance leads to young women feeling anxious, and in\nsome cases leads to anorexia or bulimia as a result of their low sense of self-worth.\n\nYoung men living in isolated rural communities can also suffer from depression. Increasing\nrates of male suicide are in part due to the lack of appropriate support services located in\nrural areas. This environmental determinant is caused by living in a remote location leading\nto social isolation. Not being able to access appropriate health services (eg Headspace)\nwhen they are struggling to manage their mental health is detrimental for young rural men.\n\nAnswers may include:\nSociocultural, socioeconomic and environmental determinants of health.\n\n                                          CONFIDENTIAL                                        Page 3 of 28","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2018-Q23","year":2018,"n":23,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data","The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.3.2","FA1.3.1","FA1.2.7"],"img":"2018_Q23_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"• Identifies issues and provides positive and/or negative points regarding\n  the impact of emerging new treatments and technologies on health care\n  in Australia                                                                                  5\n• Supports with relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of the impact of emerging new\n  health treatments and technologies in Australia                                               4\n• Supports with example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms emerging new health treatments and\n                                                                                               2-3\n  technologies and/or their impact on health care in Australia\n• Provides relevant information on health care in Australia                                     1","sample":"Health care in Australia has seen the emergence of many new treatments and technologies.\nKeyhole surgery is a less invasive technology that assists surgeons in both detecting and\ntreating problems. This technology has meant that patients spend less time in hospital,\ngreatly reducing hospital bed costs. It is also a less invasive procedure, so patients recover\nquicker and are less at risk from complications such as infections of open wounds (eg open\nheart surgery).\n\nKeyhole surgery equipment is very expensive, and subsequently is not readily available in all\nhospitals. This means that patient access is limited. Public health patients will be at a huge\ndisadvantage, as they will have to go on waiting lists to access these procedures.\n\nTreatments that prevent or detect cancer early have also reduced the overall cost of health to\nthe Australian government. Implementing the free vaccine for the HPV virus in a national\nschool program has significantly reduced the incidence of cervical cancer in Australia.\n\n                                          CONFIDENTIAL                                        Page 4 of 28","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2018-Q25","year":2018,"n":25,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.3"],"img":"2018_Q25_sa.png","w":1364,"h":157,"criteria":"• Demonstrates why arousal differs in a variety of sports\n• Makes evident the relationship between arousal and a variety of sports                         5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of the required level of arousal\n  across a variety of sports                                                                    3-4\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms the arousal levels required in sport\n                                                                                                 2\n• May provide example(s)\n• Provides some relevant information on arousal found in sport                                   1","sample":"Arousal could either inhibit or enhance sporting performance. Optimal arousal is the required\nlevel of arousal for an athlete to perform at their very best. Arousal is an emotional and\nmental state that prepares and motivates the athlete to be ‘in the zone' and depending on the\ntype of performance it can be positive or negative.\n\nThe levels required for a sport vary depending on the type, level and/or intensity of that\nparticular sport. In sports that incorporate major muscle groups such as weightlifting, higher\nlevels of arousal are required to increase levels of intensity so that the appropriate skills may\nbe better executed (eg tackling).\n\nIn contrast, sports which incorporate finer skills (eg archery) may benefit from lower levels of\narousal to increase focus and concentration. If arousal levels are heightened in sports of this\nnature errors can increase and accuracy may suffer.\n\nAnswers could include:\nInverted U hypothesis.\n\n                                           CONFIDENTIAL                                        Page 6 of 28","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2018-Q30(a)","year":2018,"n":30,"part":"(a)","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2018_Q30_a.png","w":1364,"h":195,"criteria":"• Makes clearly evident the relationship between the injury and its\n  classification and management                                                                  8\n• Provides a range of relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of the classification and\n  management of the athlete's injury                                                            6-7\n• Provides a range of examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of the classification and/or\n  management of the athlete's injury                                                            4-5\n• May provide examples\n• Sketches in general terms the classification and/or management of the\n                                                                                                2-3\n  injury\n• Provides some relevant information about sports injuries                                       1","sample":"A hamstring strain suffered by a 400-metre runner as they sprint to the finish line would be\nclassified as soft tissue and indirect. This is because the hamstring is a muscle and a strain\nis a common injury that occurs to muscles when they are torn. The injury has occurred as a\nresult of the athlete working at maximum effort and the muscle fatiguing as they try to\nperform to their best at the end of the race. As a result an intrinsic force within the body puts\npressure on the hamstring, and an indirect injury has resulted.\n\nSoft tissue injuries such as a hamstring muscle strain are immediately managed by using the\nRICER method. This is a treatment regime that will ensure that a soft tissue injury is correctly\nmanaged, and reduces the impact that the inflammatory response may have on the injury if it\nis left untreated.\n\nRest is the first consideration in RICER, and refers to the immediate cessation of activity for\nthe athlete. This will prevent further injury by minimising further movement and hence\nreducing bleeding in the area. In this case, the athlete would be initially treated on the track\nand placed in a comfortable position. Their leg would be elevated and supported while the\ntrainer determines further action.\n\nIce is then administered to the injury site. The purpose of this is to reduce pain and swelling\nby cooling the area, and hence discouraging blood flow to the area. This will be carried out\nby placing a bag of crushed ice in a wet towel on the hamstring. This is administered for\n20 minutes at a time on the injury. This treatment should be repeated every hour for the next\n24-48 hours, to ensure that swelling is kept to a minimum.\n\nCompression is another consideration to ensure that bleeding is reduced and therefore\nswelling is kept at a minimum. This can be achieved initially at the track by securing the ice\nto the injured hamstring with a clear plastic wrap wound around the ice. Additionally, an\nelastic bandage covering both above and below the injured hamstring will also alleviate\nswelling. This also can be administered over the next 24-48 hours to supplement the icing of\nthe injury.\n\nElevation of the injury is a vital part of the process, and should be administered immediately\nwhen the athlete is rested once the injury has been sustained. This can be achieved by using\na sports bag, or a pillow placed under the athlete's foot and hamstring to support and raise\nthe leg. The purpose of elevation is to raise the injured hamstring above the level of the\nheart. This will assist in reducing the throbbing that may occur if swelling and blood flow to\n\n                                           CONFIDENTIAL                                    Page 19 of 28\n\nthe area are not minimised. This is again an action that the athlete can continue over several\ndays to provide comfort and assist with an efficient recovery process.\n\nReferral is the last phase of RICER, and is vital in the case of an indirect type tissue injury.\nAs the injury has resulted from an intrinsic force within the body, an athlete will not be able to\ndetermine the extent or nature of the cause of the injury without seeking expert advice.\nSeeing a doctor or physiotherapist will assist the athlete in determining how badly the\nhamstring muscle is strained. Once the grade of the strain is established, the physio will then\nrecommend the appropriate rehabilitation needed for the injury. In the case of grade one\nhamstring strain, this may involve further rest, while a more severe hamstring strain may\nneed surgical repair.\n\n                                           CONFIDENTIAL                                    Page 20 of 28","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2018-Q30(b)","year":2018,"n":30,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2018_Q30_b.png","w":1364,"h":125,"criteria":"• Demonstrates a clear understanding of policies and procedures that\n  regulate an athlete's return to play after sustaining an injury\n• Identifies ethical considerations that affect an athlete's return to play\n                                                                                              11-12\n• Makes a judgement on the effectiveness of policies and procedures to\n  address ethical considerations\n• Supports with relevant examples\n• Demonstrates an understanding of policies and procedures that regulate\n  an athlete's return to play after suffering an injury\n                                                                                              8-10\n• Identifies ethical considerations that affect an athlete's return to play\n• Supports with relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of policies and procedures and/or\n  ethical considerations that regulate an athlete's return to play after\n  suffering an injury                                                                          5-7\n• Provides some relevant examples\n• Sketches in general terms policies and procedures and/or ethical\n  considerations that regulate an athlete's return to play after suffering an                  3-4\n  injury\n• Provides relevant information on returning to play from injury                               1-2","sample":"The growing professionalism of sport and its huge popularity in our society has placed high\nexpectations and pressure on our sport stars to return to play from injury, often before they\nhave returned to full fitness and are match ready. This is also a pressure felt across amateur\nand junior sports.\n\nPressure on athletes is often derived from external sources such as the club administrators,\nmedia hype or desire of the sponsors and fans to see players back on the field. High profile\nplayers, who are crucial to the success of the team, will often feel obliged to return to play\nwell before their injury is fully recovered. For example, a rugby league halfback who has\nsuffered from a knee injury may be forced onto the sidelines for a number of weeks. If the\nteam is in danger of being eliminated from the final series, the pressure on that player from\nthe fans, media and sponsors will be intense. The athlete may return prematurely, risking\nfurther injury and ultimately leading to a longer period of time needed out of the game to\nrecover. Policies such as an independent medical clearance are now in place in most\nprofessional sports to remove the pressure from the player and coach to decide when to\nreturn to play. The club coach and medical staff have a duty of care towards the player's\nwellbeing and health. Hence, club officials must ensure player safety is paramount to all\nselection decisions regarding injured players returning to play.\n\nPressure can also be an internal motivation, whereby the players may fear losing their\nposition in the team, or may feel they are going to let their teammates down if they are\nunable to participate. A cricket fast bowler may suffer from a bruised heel from the repetitive\npounding of bowling excessive overs in a test match. If the team is to play a series of test\nmatches in a short space of time they may feel that they are jeopardising their career\nopportunities and letting their teammates down if they cannot recover in time to resume their\nplace in the team for the next match. Players in these circumstances will often consider the\nuse of pain killing injections to manage the pain of the injury and allow the player to\nparticipate. The danger with this practice is exacerbating the injury and actually making it\nworse. Pain killing injections numb the injured site, and in this case the bruised heel may\nthen lead to more serious damage in the form of stress fractures if not treated appropriately.\n\n                                          CONFIDENTIAL                                    Page 21 of 28\n\nThe team physio should monitor the player's progress using results from pre and post\ntesting. By making comparisons of fitness results in regards to the indicators of readiness (ie\nlevel of strength of the ankle joint, range of movement, pain-free sprinting action), an\nindependent assessment can be determined. These procedures ultimately remove the\ndecision and pressure from the player, and prioritise the player making a full recovery from\ninjury as the most significant factor.\n\nIn recent times, sports such as rugby league and union have introduced policies that address\nplayer safety in regards to head injuries. The nature of these sports is becoming more\npowerful as the size and strength of the players increases. Hence head injuries in the form of\nconcussion are occurring more regularly. Policy changes by the game administrators now\nrequire the referee to stop play and remove the injured player using a free head injury\ninterchange. A SCAT or COG test is conducted by a club doctor who assesses for signs of\ndizziness, memory loss or blackouts. These procedures are vital for the long-term health of\nthe rugby player. Repeated head knocks can lead to irreversible brain damage. Clubs are\nnow very conscious of long-term litigation concerns if they continue to play athletes who have\nrepetitive concussion injuries. It is therefore evident that player welfare is now a major\nconsideration in the decisions made by club administrators as to when to return a player to\nthe field from any form of concussive head injury.\n\n                                          CONFIDENTIAL                                    Page 22 of 28","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2018-Q31(a)","year":2018,"n":31,"part":"(a)","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations","Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.2.1","FA2.3.1","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2018_Q31_a.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Makes evident the relationship of both safe and potentially harmful\n  training procedures to TWO training types                                                     8\n• Provides accurate and relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of both safe and potentially harmful\n  training procedures related to TWO training types\n                                                                                               6-7\n• Shows some link between the training procedures and their outcomes\n• Provides example/s\n• Provides characteristics and features of safe and potentially harmful\n  training procedures related to training types                                                4-5\n• May provide examples\n• Sketches in general terms safe and/or harmful training procedures related\n                                                                                               2-3\n  to training types\n• Identifies information on safe/harmful training practices OR training types                   1","sample":"There are many safe and many potentially harmful training procedures related to the various\ntraining types. Strength training exposes athletes to a high risk of injury when executing\ntraining if certain procedures are not followed. Participants should be aware of all safety\nprecautions and potential harm from procedures before they commence a training program\nas incorrect forms and/or procedures will result in injury.\n\nIt is essential that an athlete uses only safe and well-maintained equipment as damaged or\npoorly-maintained equipment can result in injury. Precede specific strength-training programs\nwith a general program to ensure that all required muscles are adequately developed and\nresistant to injury. Warm up prior to all strength-training sessions to reduce the chance of\nmuscle strains or tears. Athletes must also use weights, sets and reps that are indicative of a\nperson at their stage or development. Using weight that is too heavy will result in injury with\npossible long recovery periods.\n\nUse spotters to ensure that technique is maintained as this will reduce cheat reps and\nexposure to injury. With the use of a spotter, an athlete can ensure controlled execution of\nmovement and avoid the jerky contracts that result in muscle injuries. Ensure exercises are\nequally balanced to avoid an imbalance in muscle hypertrophy, resulting in such conditions\nas kyphosis (rounded shoulders due to an imbalance of the chest and back muscles).\n\nTo avoid injuries an athlete must ensure that they progressively overload in small increments\nto ensure that the body has the required time to adapt. This will avoid injury in joints and\nmuscles and prevent long periods of recovery. To effectively and safely participate in\nstrength training, an athlete must ensure that they adequately cool down and allow\nappropriate rest days to allow muscle recovery and prevent injuries from fatigue.\n\nAlthough the level of intensity is not high in aerobic training, the duration and repetitive\nnature of movement can lead to both acute and chronic injuries. The safe training procedures\nfor aerobic training can include commencing a training program with realistic goals, and\nensuring that you have been given medical clearance to participate in the desired program.\nIn addition, athletes should be aware of the correct technique as inferior techniques can lead\nto overuse injuries and possible fractures or muscle soreness. Ensure that there is an\nappropriate warm up and cool down period to reduce muscle strains. The progressive\noverload principle should be applied to allow the body to make adaptations before increasing\nthe load.\n\n                                          CONFIDENTIAL                                    Page 23 of 28\n\nAvoid overtraining by allowing adequate rest periods after long duration exercises. It is also\nrecommended that cross training is implemented to avoid excessive fatigue of a specific\nmuscle group.\n\n                                          CONFIDENTIAL                                    Page 24 of 28","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2018-Q31(b)","year":2018,"n":31,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.5"],"img":"2018_Q31_b.png","w":1364,"h":86,"criteria":"• Demonstrates a clear understanding of the use of performance-\n  enhancing drugs by elite athletes\n• Makes detailed judgements about elite athletes' use of performance-                         11-12\n  enhancing drugs\n• Provides accurate and detailed supporting examples\n• Demonstrates an understanding of the use of performance-enhancing\n  drugs by elite athletes\n• Makes judgments about elite athletes' use of performance-enhancing                          8-10\n  drugs\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of issues surrounding elite athletes'\n  use of performance-enhancing drugs                                                           5-7\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms issues surrounding elite athletes' use of\n                                                                                               3-4\n  performance-enhancing drugs\n• Provides some relevant information related to the use of performance-\n                                                                                               1-2\n  enhancing drugs","sample":"Performance-enhancing drug use in sport carries serious health risks and can jeopardise an\nathlete's future in the sport. Some performance-enhancing drugs may result in serious health\nrisks including liver tumours, cancer, hypertension, kidney disease, fluid retention, mental\nhealth and fertility issues. Some physiological dangers can also include memory\ndisturbances, testicular atrophy, baldness and gynaecomastia all of which can have\nsignificant chronic effects. Medical treatment would be required for a long duration to treat\nand manage the variety of physical repercussions.\n\nThere can also be changes in the social and psychological behaviours of an athlete. Some\nperformance-enhancing drugs result in mood swings, irritability, aggression, sexual arousal,\nconfusion and guilt. In addition, there can be short-term effects including increased reaction\ntime and a reduction in focus, coordination and concentration. These repercussions can have\na significant negative impact on relationships with friends and families leading to relationship\nbreakdowns, alienation and isolation.\n\nAs a result of using drugs, the livelihood of an athlete can be dramatically affected. Athletes\nfound guilty can be banned from the sport, lose reputation and subsequently income and\nsponsorships/endorsements and cancellation of contracts. In addition to future loss of\nincome some athletes who use performance-enhancing drugs can be required to pay back\nincome and endorsements if found guilty. This can cause significant financial pressures (loss\nof home etc) and cause relationship stress.\n\nDespite the risk, many athletes still look towards using performance-enhancing drugs in the\npursuit of finding the edge over other athletes. The desire to win can sometimes make an\nathlete overlook the above-mentioned risks as the success that comes from winning can hold\nsignificant potential for life changes. There may be high levels of motivation for athletes from\ndisadvantaged backgrounds.\n\n                                          CONFIDENTIAL                                    Page 25 of 28","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2018-Q32(b)","year":2018,"n":32,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2018_Q32_b.png","w":1364,"h":242,"criteria":"• Draws out and relates the implications of multiple risk factors that may be\n  experienced by populations affected by health inequities\n• Makes evident the link between multiple risk factors and the management                       11-12\n  of health inequities\n• Provides accurate and relevant examples\n• Provides the characteristics and features of risk factors that may be\n  experienced by populations affected by health inequities\n• Provides the characteristics and features of the link between multiple risk                   8-10\n  factors and the management of health inequities\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Makes clear how more than one risk factor may contribute to health\n  inequities\n• Makes clear how management approaches may recognise multiple risk                              5-7\n  factors\n• May use examples\n• Sketches in general terms the relationship between risk factors and\n  health inequities\nOR                                                                                               3-4\n• Sketches in general terms the implications of risk factors for the\n  management of health inequities\n• Provides relevant information about risk factors or health status or\n                                                                                                 1-2\n  management of health inequities","sample":"Health risk factors seldom occur in isolation. There is a strong correlation between factors\nsuch as low socioeconomic status, isolation, access to services, education standards,\naccess to transport and inequity of health outcomes. When these conditions exist in\ncombination individuals are over-represented in all indicators of ill health.\n\nThe key implication for any management strategy is to adopt a multifaceted approach to\ndrive change. Simplistic approaches tend to be ineffective as they do not take into\nconsideration the variety of challenges facing the groups at risk.\n\nAttempts to increase the physical activity of a group through making aquarobic classes\navailable may be ineffective if the cost is prohibitive, if transport is not readily available, if the\nindividuals are non-swimmers and lack confidence in the water. A program design and\npromotion needs to think through the issues that people with multiple risk factors are faced\nwith and provide the support they need to participate.\n\nPrograms to improve the educational standards of young people may need to consider the\ndaily living standards of the young people and address these. If children arrive at school\nwithout breakfast their capacity to concentrate is reduced. An improved education program\nmay not realise its potential unless fundamental needs of food and health care are being\nmet.\n\nMaking health information leaflets available to populations may have limited impact if literacy\nlevels do not allow them to be read and understood. Cultural groups may be literate in other\nlanguages only and require translation into their native language.\n\n                                            CONFIDENTIAL                                    Page 28 of 28","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q22","year":2019,"n":22,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sleep, nutrition and supplements"],"sids":["FA2.4.1","FA2.4.3"],"img":"2019_Q22_sa.png","w":1364,"h":195,"criteria":"• Provides an argument to support why carbohydrate loading would not\n  improve performance for an athlete competing in a 100-metre running\n  race                                                                                         4\n• Demonstrates an accurate understanding of the relationship between\n  carbohydrate loading, fuel sources and energy system(s)\n• Provides reasons why carbohydrate loading would not improve\n                                                                                               3\n  performance for an athlete competing in a 100-metre running race\n• Sketches, in general terms, carbohydrate loading and/or energy systems                       2\n• Provides some relevant information about nutritional considerations for\n                                                                                               1\n  athletes","sample":"A 100-metre runner uses the anaerobic (ATP-CP) system, due to the duration and very high\nintensity of the race. The fuel source for the athlete is creatine phosphate not carbohydrates\nand therefore carbohydrate loading would be ineffective. Carbohydrates are a fuel source for\nthe lactic acid and aerobic energy systems. Carbohydrate loading involves a significant\nincrease in high carbohydrate foods for several days before a high intensity endurance\nactivity to increase glycogen stores in the body. Carbohydrate loading is most effective for\nimproving performance when used for aerobic events of more than ninety minutes. As such\ncarbohydrate loading would have no impact on improving the performance of a 100-metre\nrunner in the race.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q23","year":2019,"n":23,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2"],"img":"2019_Q23_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"• Makes evident the relationship between environmental determinants and\n  the health inequities experienced by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander                   5\n  peoples\n• Provides features of environmental determinants and relates how they\n  contribute to health inequities experienced by Aboriginal and Torres Strait                  4\n  Islander peoples\n• Provides features of environmental determinant(s) and relates this to\n  health inequity experienced by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander                         3\n  peoples\n• Sketches, in general terms, environmental determinants and/or health\n                                                                                               2\n  inequity experienced by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples\n• Provides some relevant information about environmental determinants or\n  health inequities experienced by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander                       1\n  peoples","sample":"When Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples live outside of major cities, this limits\naccess to primary health services, which can mean that treatment is received in the latter\nstages of a chronic condition like diabetes. This leads to higher mortality rates for Aboriginal\nand Torres Strait Islander peoples. Living in rural areas can limit employment opportunities,\nespecially for young people, which leads to higher levels of unemployment, isolation and\nloneliness. When combined with inadequate support services for mental health issues, this\nincreases morbidity for mental health issues like depression. A lack of access to health\nservices for mental health can lead to increased substance use, eg alcohol use.\n\nAnswers could include:\nEnvironmental determinants, eg geographical location, access to health services, access to\ntechnology and housing.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q27","year":2019,"n":27,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.2","FA2.2.3","FA2.2.1"],"img":"2019_Q27_sa.png","w":1364,"h":274,"criteria":"• Draws out and relates the implications of using progressive overload and\n  training thresholds in relation to the physiological adaptations for an                      8\n  athlete participating in a 12-week aerobic training program\n• Makes evident how the use of progressive overload and training\n  thresholds can result in physiological adaptations for an athlete in a                      6-7\n  12-week aerobic training program\n• Provides characteristics and features of progressive overload and training\n  thresholds and links these to physiological adaptations for an athlete in a                 4-5\n  12-week aerobic training program\n• Sketches, in general terms, physiological adaptations resulting from an\n  aerobic training program\nOR                                                                                            2-3\n• Sketches, in general terms, progressive overload and/or training\n  thresholds AND physiological adaptations\n• Provides some relevant information about a principle of training or a\n                                                                                               1\n  physiological adaptation","sample":"Progressive overload involves a gradual increase of frequency, duration and intensity of\ntraining, which should occur when an athlete adapts to the training, eg this athlete could run\nfor 20 minutes, three times per week at 65% MHR for the first four weeks, and then increase\nto 30 minutes four times per week at 75% MHR for the next four weeks. Training thresholds\nwill ensure the athlete exercises at an intensity above the aerobic threshold and below the\nanaerobic threshold (aerobic training zone), eg running continuously at 70-80% MHR. Using\nprogressive overload and training thresholds in this way for 12 weeks will result in\nphysiological adaptations including increasing oxygen uptake, stroke volume and cardiac\noutput as well as reducing resting heart rate. This is due to the increased stress placed on\nthe cardiovascular and respiratory systems, making them stronger and more efficient. This\nenables the athlete to train for a longer period of time. Stroke volume increases especially at\nmaximal levels of intensity, as the left ventricle increases in size, enabling a greater volume\nof blood to be pumped from the heart when it contracts. This, combined with increased\nhaemoglobin levels from aerobic training, enables working muscles to get more oxygen.\nBecause the heart is working more efficiently, resting heart rate will decrease, as more blood\nis pumped from the heart with every contraction.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q30(a)(i)","year":2019,"n":30,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2019_Q30_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Makes evident how the assessment procedures (TOTAPS) are used to\n  determine the nature and extent of this injury                                                4\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides features of the assessment procedures (TOTAPS) used to\n  determine the nature and extent of this injury                                                3\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches, in general terms, assessment procedures used to determine\n                                                                                                2\n  the nature and/or extent of injury\n• Provides some relevant information about assessment procedures                                1","sample":"The loud crack may indicate that the touch football player could have dislocated or fractured\ntheir shoulder which is a hard tissue injury. The assessment procedures to determine the\nnature and extent of this shoulder injury would be TOTAPS, which stands for talk, observe,\ntouch, active movement, passive movement and skills test. Talk to the athlete to gather as\nmuch information about the injury as possible, including symptoms of the injury, eg pain,\ngrating or cracking. Ask questions such as, where does it hurt? How painful is it out of 10? If\nthe athlete reports that they have immense pain or loss of function the TOTAPS procedure is\nstopped immediately as medical attention is required. Observe the injured shoulder by\ncomparing to the other shoulder in order to determine if there is an obvious abnormality, eg\nswelling, redness, blood. If there is an abnormality or the area is already inflammed, then the\nTOTAPS procedure is stopped and medical attention sought. Touch the casualty, to see\nwhere the pain begins moving around the shoulder and arm. Feel for abnormalities, heat and\nswelling. If an abnormality or excessive pain exists during touch, then the TOTAPS\nassessment procedure is stopped. If the shoulder injury is classified as a hard tissue injury\nthe TOTAPS assessment procedure would stop here as the touch player would not be able\nto participate in the remaining steps of the TOTAPS (active movement, passive movement\nand skills test) procedure as these steps would cause further injury to the shoulder.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q30(a)(ii)","year":2019,"n":30,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2019_Q30_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Provides characteristics and features of the management of fractures and\n  dislocations                                                                                  4\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Sketches, in general terms, the management of fractures and dislocations\n                                                                                                3\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches, in general terms, the management of fractures OR dislocations                       2\n• Provides some relevant information about managing hard tissue injuries                        1","sample":"Fractures and dislocations are classified as a hard tissue injury and will both require medical\nintervention.\n\nTo manage a fracture it is essential to immobilise the injured limb or area, eg using a sling for\na fractured forearm, or clavicle. If the fracture has pierced the skin, it is essential to reduce\nand control any external bleeding, using pressure, provided it does not cause further pain or\ninjury. The athlete should be monitored for shock. The athlete should then be taken to a\nmedical facility for treatment by a doctor, eg X-ray and plaster cast or splint if required. A\ndislocation is an injury to a joint where a bone has been displaced from the joint structure. To\nmanage this, do not move the joint. The injured joint should be supported in a comfortable\nposition and if possible a sling should be applied. The athlete or first aider should not attempt\nto relocate the joint, this must be done by a medical professional after an X-ray has been\ntaken. Applying ice may help to reduce the swelling and pain for a dislocation.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q31(a)(i)","year":2019,"n":31,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.1"],"img":"2019_Q31_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Provides characteristics and features of TWO different types of training\n  methods that are best suited to the performance requirements of an\n  athlete in a particular sport                                                                 4\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Sketches in general terms TWO different types of training methods best\n  suited to the performance requirements of an athlete in a particular sport                    3\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms a training method suited to the performance\n                                                                                                2\n  needs of an athlete\n• Identifies some relevant information about training methods of an athlete\n                                                                                                1\n  in a particular sport","sample":"A 50 m or 100 m butterfly swimmer would need to participate in flexibility training and\nstrength training to meet their performance needs as they require strength and flexibility to\nexecute the stroke consistently in a race. Dynamic flexibility training methods would be most\nbeneficial for increasing shoulder and hip flexibility. Dynamic flexibility training methods are\nsuited to a butterfly swimmer as it involves continuous movements of a joint, eg shoulder, in\na sustained way. This training method takes the shoulder through the full range of movement\npossible, and using all muscles of the shoulder ensures that flexibility is maximised. Strength\ntraining methods such as weight training would be most suited to the butterfly swimmer.\nWeight training involves the use of dumbbells and plates and this would be suited to a\nbutterfly swimmer as it allows a range of different exercises to be completed with ease of\nadding additional load by using heavier weights when the muscles increase in strength, eg\nadding a 10 kg plate to the lat pull down. The use of plates allows for progressive overload to\nbe applied easily. The butterfly swimmer would be focusing on both strength and power, so\nwould need to ensure that the load, sets, speed of lift, rest and number of repetitions are\nappropriate, eg 85% 1RM, 3-6 sets, 1-6 reps, 2-5 minutes rest between sets and slow\nspeed of repetitions. When adjusting the exercise to focus on power, the swimmer would\nneed to reduce the load and increase the speed of the repetition.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q31(a)(ii)","year":2019,"n":31,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations","Exercise assessment","Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.2.3","FA2.1.2","FA2.5.3"],"img":"2019_Q31_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Makes evident how the training adaptations for this athlete can be\n  measured and monitored                                                                        4\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of the ways training adaptations for\n  this athlete can be measured and monitored                                                    3\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms how training adaptation(s) for this athlete can\n                                                                                                2\n  be measured and/or monitored\n• Identifies some relevant information about measuring and/or monitoring\n                                                                                                1\n  training adaptations","sample":"When the swimmer participates in dynamic flexibility training methods, adaptations will occur\nto the muscles which should increase flexibility and range of movement in joints. To measure\nand monitor the adaptations from this flexibility training, the athlete will need to have\nmeasured their flexibility prior to the training, so that there is a starting baseline that the\nathlete or coach can use to monitor progress with improving flexibility. Measuring and\nmonitoring hamstring flexibility could be done using the sit and reach test. This should then\nbe repeated throughout the training program, with the results of the test recorded each time,\nso that improvements to hamstring flexibility can be seen. The weight training methods as\npart of the strength training program can result in a number of adaptations including\nincreased number of muscle fibres recruited and increased speed of recruitment of muscles.\nThese adaptations can be measured and monitored by completing a three repetition\nmaximum test for specific muscles or groups of muscles that have been trained, eg bench\npress, shoulder press, squats. The test should be completed before training commences and\nthen repeated throughout the strength training program with the same tests and test\nconditions. Recording the results of these tests enables improvements in muscle strength to\nbe monitored and then changes made to the weight training program, eg increased weight or\nrepetitions or methods used, eg resistance or isometric training to ensure ongoing muscle\nadaptations are made from strength training.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q31(b)","year":2019,"n":31,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.3.2","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2019_Q31_b.png","w":1364,"h":164,"criteria":"• Demonstrates a detailed understanding of an athlete's fitness and skill-\n  specific requirements in a particular sport\n• Draws out and relates the implications of an athlete's fitness and\n                                                                                              11-12\n  skill-specific requirements changing during each phase of competition in a\n  periodisation chart\n• Provides relevant and accurate examples\n• Demonstrates an understanding of an athlete's fitness and skill-specific\n  requirements\n• Makes evident the reasons why an athlete's fitness and skill-specific\n                                                                                              8-10\n  requirements change during each phase of competition in a periodisation\n  chart\n• Provides relevant and specific examples\n• Describes why an athlete's fitness and skill-specific requirements change\n  during each phase of competition in a periodisation chart\n• Provides examples\nOR                                                                                             5-7\n• Provides features of an athlete's fitness and skill-specific requirements\n  during the three phases of competition in a periodisation chart\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches, in general terms, why fitness and/or skill-specific requirements\n  change during the phases of competition\nOR                                                                                             3-4\n• Sketches, in general terms, the fitness and skill-specific requirements\n  during the phases of competition\n• Provides relevant information about the phases of competition and/or\n                                                                                               1-2\n  fitness and skill-specific requirements","sample":"The fitness and skill-specific requirements for an elite midfield soccer player in the A-League\nwill be different in each phase of competition. This is essential to ensure that the player has\nthe required skill level and physical conditioning so that they are physically ready for the\ncompetition, reduce the risk of injury and overtraining during the in-season and are ready to\npeak towards the end of the in-season in the finals of the competition.\n\nPre-season is up to three months and will include several macrocycles. A six-week\nmacrocycle of general fitness conditioning would include endurance fitness such as\ncontinuous running and aerobic interval training, as well as strength and flexibility to ensure a\nsoccer player being able to last the length of a game. This is followed by another six-week\nmacrocycle of specific conditioning which would include farlek training and interval training\nsuch as short prints with active recovery sessions that closely mimic a midfielder's running\npattern in a game. The core focus of the pre-season is for the athlete to regain his fitness\nthat was lost in the off-season and to adequately prepare them for the demands of the in-\nseason.\n\nThe pre-season will also include the practice of skill-specific requirements such as ball\ncontrol, passing, dribbling and shooting because they may not have played with a ball in the\noff-season. Some team tactical training will also be included such as general formation such\n\nas a 4, 4, 2 and defensive formations and where specifically the midfield soccer player needs\nto be when the opposition attacks from different positions.\n\nThe in-season has competition can be broken down into four macrocycles. The last\nmacrocycle prepares the athlete to peak for the finals. Prior to peaking, the midfielder's team\nwill taper their fitness session by reducing the workload, so that players can recover from\ninjury and minimise the chance of overtraining. Anaerobic fitness needs to be maintained in\nthe in-season phase so that the midfielder can meet the demands of the game and be in\noptimal position in defence and attack. Microcycles of fitness can be introduced if the coach\nbelieves a fitness aspect needs to be worked on such as speed and recovery.\n\nThe in-season requires a greater emphasis on skill and tactics. Rehearsal of these under\npressure, for example short passes in tight areas, ensure that the midfielder can make the\nplays despite pressure and fatigue. Microcycles can be introduced (within macrocyles) to\nensure the team is prepared specifically for opposition and new tactics can be developed and\npracticed; ie the midfielder's distribution to the forwards in a particular way and an\nintroduction of longer passes.\n\nIn the post-season, a period of rest is important to allow the body and mind to recover from\nthe demands of the season. The midfielder may maintain some fitness through cross-training\nor playing a similar sport like futsal. This will enable skill and fitness to be maintained without\nthe pressure of competition. This macrocycle will focus on general fitness with less emphasis\non skills.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2019-Q32(b)","year":2019,"n":32,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians","The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.2.1"],"img":"2019_Q32_b.png","w":1364,"h":125,"criteria":"• Makes an accurate judgement about how the inequity gap for TWO\n  population groups has changed as a result of government interventions                       11-12\n• Provides specific and relevant examples\n• Makes evident how the inequity gap for TWO population groups has\n  changed as a result of government interventions\n                                                                                              8-10\n• May provide a judgement\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Describes how the inequity gap for TWO population groups has changed\n  as a result of government interventions                                                      5-7\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches, in general terms, how the inequity gap for population group(s)\n  has changed as a result of government interventions                                          3-4\n• Provides examples\n• Provides some relevant information about how the inequity gap for\n                                                                                               1-2\n  population group(s) has changed and/or government interventions","sample":"Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people have a significantly higher burden of disease\ncompared to non-Aboriginal Australians. However with a combination of a range of\ngovernment Aboriginal health services, improvements have been made. Overall in the last\n10 years, the total burden of disease fell by 5%, with the death rate decreasing by 11%. The\ngovernment provides many specific Aboriginal health services to support mainstream health\nservices and target areas of health inequities in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander\npeoples. Examples of these Aboriginal health interventions are community support services,\nscreening programs, social and emotional wellbeing services, transport services and\naccommodation. These can be found in all major cities, as well as in most regional areas.\n\nThrough government funding many specific local Aboriginal Medical Services have been set\nup. These bring a range of health professionals, many Aboriginal, together in one area to\nservice the local Aboriginal population's health needs. The environment is less threatening\nand more trusted by the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community, so they are more\nlikely to use the service for their health needs, and follow up with treatment plans. This\nincreases the access to services and reduces barriers to using a health service, main factors\nthat can create inequities in health. This is mainly because the Aboriginal Medical Service is\nculturally sensitive and trusted. They also specialise in health issues most prevalent in the\nlocal community and provide screening and preventative treatments also as well as focusing\non the social wellbeing of the Aboriginal population. This has greatly increased the number of\nAboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people seeking health care, reducing especially the total\ndisease burden and death rate.\n\nThe government provides a Puggy Hunter Memorial Scholarship Scheme which funds\nAboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples who wish to take up health profession training.\nHaving health professionals who are Aboriginal increases trust in the health service and\nincreases the likelihood of greater participation in the health service from those in the\ncommunity due to the increased engagement of communities in their health care.\n\nPeople living in geographically remote populations have higher levels of health risk factors\nand suffer poorer health than those living in regional and metropolitan areas, resulting in\nhigher mortality and morbidity rates and lower life expectancy. The impact of these inequities\nincreases with remoteness. A high percentage of the people living in geographically remote\n\npopulations are Aboriginal. The government have many initiatives to potentially decrease the\ninequity gap in these areas. The Royal Flying Doctor service aims to increase the access to\nquality health care in an aim to reduce some of the inequity gap. They provide services for\nanyone living, working or travelling in geographically remote areas needing both primary and\nemergency health care. This increases the access to quality health services, decreases\nisolation and aims to reduce the large inequity gap in their health.\n\nThe National Rural and Remote Health Infrastructure Program aims to improve access to\nhealth services in rural and remote areas. The Rural eHealth Strategy is a NSW state\ngovernment initiative to connect rural and remote patients electronically with services and\nclinicians. This can allow people to remain in or close to their home while being treated and\nmonitoring for long-term conditions, eg diabetes and cardiovascular disease. This reduces\nadded stress and promotes healing as the individual is close to home and family and does\nnot have added costs of travelling to main regional areas or metropolitan hubs for regular\nmonitoring. It can occur online, and allows regular monitoring of their condition and therefore\nimproved access to services and decreased isolation.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q22","year":2020,"n":22,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.1","FA1.1.4"],"img":"2020_Q22_sa.png","w":1299,"h":1250,"criteria":"• Identifies three current leading causes of mortality for males and females\n  in Australia and all the corresponding trends in mortality rates over the                    4\n  last 10 years\n• Identifies three current leading causes of mortality for males and females\n  in Australia and at least four corresponding trends in mortality rates over                  3\n  the past 10 years\n• Identifies current leading causes of mortality for males and females in\n  Australia and at least two corresponding trends in mortality rates over the                  2\n  last 10 years\n• Identifies at least one leading cause of mortality for males and females                     1","sample":"Current leading cause of     Trend in mortality rate for          Trend in mortality rate for\n mortality                    males over the last 10 years         females over the last 10 years\n Coronary heart disease       Decreased                            Decreased\n Dementia and                 Increased                            Increased\n Alzheimer's disease\n Cerebrovascular disease      Decreased                            Decreased\nAnswers could include:\n• Lung cancer\n• Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease\n• Diabetes\n• Cardiovascular disease\n• Mental health - suicide","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q23(b)","year":2020,"n":23,"part":"(b)","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.3"],"img":"2020_Q23_b.png","w":1364,"h":155,"criteria":"• Shows how anxiety and arousal may have affected the athlete's\n  performance in this 100-metre final in similar or different ways                             4\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Sketches in general terms how anxiety and arousal may have affected\n  the athlete's performance in this 100-metre final                                            3\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms how anxiety OR arousal may affect\n                                                                                               2\n  performance\n• Provides some relevant information                                                           1","sample":"This athlete may have elevated anxiety and arousal levels and while their effects on the\nathlete differ, the impact on the athlete's overall performance could be the same. Anxiety is a\npsychological state characterised by fear when a situation is perceived as threatening, and\nthis Olympic final would cause heightened levels of anxiety. This may cause the athlete's\nconcentration to drift, they may have a slower reaction time out of the starting blocks and\ntheir form and technique may be affected, increasing their time. A high level of arousal can\nincrease the physiological effects for this athlete, such as increasing their heart rate, blood\npressure and muscle tension. If not managed, this can lead to a false start, or reduced stride\nlength, which can lead to a loss of power. This will increase their time as well.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q24","year":2020,"n":24,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.1"],"img":"2020_Q24_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"• Makes clearly evident the relationship between two flexibility training\n  methods and improved performance of an athlete in one sport                                  5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Describes how two flexibility training methods can improve the\n  performance of an athlete in one sport                                                       4\n• Provides examples\n• Outlines how flexibility training method/s can improve performance in one\n  sport\n• Provides example(s)\n                                                                                               3\nOR\n• Describes two flexibility training methods and their effects on the athlete\n• Provides example(s)\n• Outlines flexibility training method(s)\n• Provides some relevant information                                                           1","sample":"PNF stretching involves lengthening a muscle against resistance, often provided by a\npartner. It involves static stretching then an isometric contraction and another static stretch.\nThis flexibility training method is appropriate for soccer. When a player participates in regular\nPNF stretching of their hamstrings with a partner, this increases the muscle length and range\nof movement possible at the hip joint. This can improve performance when the player is\nkicking the ball by enabling them to generate more power so the ball travels further. Dynamic\nstretching uses continuous movement at speed and with momentum to gradually warm up\nmuscle fibres and extend them through the range of movement required. A soccer player can\nuse leg swings to increase the flexibility of muscles around the hip joint eg hamstrings,\nquadriceps. This will improve performance as a player can more effectively dribble and pass\nthe ball, change direction and make tackles without sustaining injury.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q25","year":2020,"n":25,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.4"],"img":"2020_Q25_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"• Provides characteristics and features of the advantages and\n                                                                                               5\n  disadvantages of private health insurance for the individual\n• Provides characteristics and features of the advantages and a\n  disadvantage of private health insurance for the individual\nOR                                                                                             4\n• Provides characteristics and features of the disadvantages and an\n  advantage of private health insurance for the individual\n• Sketches in general terms the advantage(s) and/or the disadvantage(s) of\n                                                                                              2-3\n  private health insurance for the individual\n• Provides some relevant information about private health insurance                            1","sample":"Private health insurance has advantages for an individual as it can provide more health care\noptions. Private health insurance covers most of the costs associated with treatment in a\nprivate hospital. It also covers some of the costs for a number of ancillary services eg\nphysiotherapy and dental. Another advantage of having private health insurance is that an\nindividual can choose their own specialist and access private accommodation in hospital.\nIndividuals can also have shorter waiting times for elective treatments/surgery if they have\nprivate health insurance. There are disadvantages of having private health insurance,\nincluding the significant cost to the individual each year, especially for individuals with low\nsocioeconomic status. Another disadvantage is that individuals may not access the full\namount of entitlement available to them for their level of cover.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q30(a)(i)","year":2020,"n":30,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2020_Q30_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Sketches in general terms how the indicators of readiness to return to\n  play are used following an ankle injury                                                       3\n• Provides examples related to the ankle\n• Sketches in general terms how an indicator of readiness to return to play\n  is used following an ankle injury                                                             2\n• Provides an example\n• Provides some relevant information about readiness to return to play                          1","sample":"Athletes who have had an ankle injury should meet specific standards of function before\nreturning to play. This will reduce the athlete's chance of re-injury to the ankle. Before\nreturning to play, the athlete should ensure that the ankle is pain free for all movement,\nparticularly the movements specific to their sport, eg a netball player should be able to run,\njump and change directions without any pain in the ankle. The athlete must also ensure that\nthe degree of mobility or range of movement at the ankle is at least the same as or,\npreferably, better than it was before the injury occurred. For example, the netball player\nshould have full range of movement of their ankle, such as being able to write the alphabet\nwith their foot.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q30(a)(ii)","year":2020,"n":30,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4","FA2.5.5"],"img":"2020_Q30_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Provides points for and/or against the ethical issues to consider before an\n  athlete returns to play following an injury                                                   5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Describes the ethical issues to be considered before an athlete returns to\n  play following an injury                                                                      4\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Sketches in general terms ethical issue(s) to be considered before an\n                                                                                               2-3\n  athlete returns to play following an injury\n• Provides relevant information about an ethical issue for an athlete\n                                                                                                1\n  returning to play","sample":"There are a range of ethical issues to consider when deciding if an athlete is ready to return\nto training and competition.\n\nPressure to participate can come from both within the athlete as well as external sources.\nMany athletes may consider themselves as indispensible or have feelings of guilt about\nletting down teammates or fans. Pressure from within may help the athlete focus more on\nrecovery and better adhere to programs and professional advice. External pressure may\ncome from coaches, teammates, sponsors or the media. This pressure to participate may\nhave a negative effect in causing an athlete to return to play prematurely, increasing the risk\nof re-injury.\n\nThe use of painkillers is another ethical issue when an athlete is returning to play. Painkillers\nare drugs used to mask the pain associated with injury. As such, their use can be warranted\nin the short-term for minor injuries. However, the ongoing use of painkillers may lead to\nincreased healing time or re-injury. The use of painkillers for more serious injuries may lead\nto increased or irreparable damage. For example, a player with a Grade 1 hamstring tear\nmay return to play following a painkilling injection and suffer a complete rupture of the\nmuscle.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q31(a)(i)","year":2020,"n":31,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.3"],"img":"2020_Q31_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Sketches in general terms how equipment advances have been used to\n  improve performance                                                                           3\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms how equipment advances have been used to\n  improve performance\n                                                                                                2\nOR\n• Sketches in general terms equipment advances in sport\n• Provides some relevant information about equipment advances                                   1","sample":"Equipment advances have significantly improved performance. Equipment such as swimsuits\nhave seen world records fall due to the reduction in drag through the water. Golf clubs made\nfrom graphite and titanium possess a larger sweet spot accuracy, which increases\nconsistency with each stroke. Golf balls made from synthetic fibres, eg tungsten, provide\ngreater durability, improving distance, spin and control. In athletics, the rubber compound\nused in synthetic tracks increases the forward motion efficiency, which improves running\ntime.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q31(a)(ii)","year":2020,"n":31,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.3"],"img":"2020_Q31_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Makes clearly evident the relationship between training innovations and\n  improved performance for an athlete                                                           5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of training innovations and how they\n  can improve an athlete's performance                                                          4\n• Provides examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of training innovations that can\n  improve an athlete's performance                                                              3\n• Provides example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms training innovations that can improve an\n                                                                                                2\n  athlete's performance\n• Provides some relevant information about training                                             1","sample":"Training innovation has seen athletics performance make considerable gains. Although the\ninnovations are expensive, it provides medical /coaching staff with an abundance of\ninformation. Lactate threshold testing procedures mean blood samples are taken once an\nathlete accumulates a level of lactic acid similar to that achieved in competition. This point is\ncharacterised by increased ventilation rate and deteriorating performance. Another type of\ntraining innovation is ‘biomechanical analysis'. This technique uses video analysis,\nphotography or motion replays in order to fine tune movements like throwing or running and\nmake movement more efficient. The athlete's technique is manipulated in order to execute at\na high level, hence making an improvement in performance.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q31(b)","year":2020,"n":31,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.3.1","FA2.3.2","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2020_Q31_b.png","w":1364,"h":311,"criteria":"• Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of the elements of a\n  training session, activities relevant to these elements and suitable for a\n  group of athletes for the sport selected\n• Provides an argument for the selection of ONE activity the coach could                      11-12\n  use for most elements of the training session\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Presents a logical and cohesive response\n• Demonstrates a sound understanding of elements of a training session,\n  activities relevant to these elements and suitable for a group of athletes\n  for the sport selected\n• Provides reasons for the selection of activities the coach could use for                    8-10\n  elements of the training session\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides a logical response\n• Describes a suitable activity for some of the elements of the training\n  session for the sport selected\n• Provides examples\n                                                                                               5-7\nOR\n• Provides reasons for different elements of a training session\n• Provides examples\n• Outlines an activity for some of the elements of the training session\n                                                                                               3-4\n• Provides examples\n• Provides some relevant information about training sessions                                   1-2","sample":"For a netball team preparing to play three days out from competition, the coach's 60-minute\nsession should be designed around every element of a training session, but with activities\nchosen to sharpen the team while protecting them from fatigue and injury this close to the\ngame - reflecting the tapering phase of the yearly training program.\n\nThe first element is health and safety. The coach should conduct a brief individual check-in,\nasking each athlete about any niggles, soreness or carry-over strains from the previous\nsession, and inspect the court surface and footwear for hazards. Justification: with only three\ndays to recover, identifying and managing a minor issue now prevents it from becoming a\ncompetition-ending injury, and reduces the risk of a new injury being aggravated by training\nload this late in the cycle.\n\nThe overview/aim of the session should be goal-specific and clearly communicated: 'today we\nare sharpening combinations and reducing training volume so you arrive fresh.' Justification:\ntelling athletes the purpose keeps them mentally focused on quality over quantity, consistent\nwith tapering, and prevents athletes pushing themselves into unnecessary fatigue.\n\nFor warm-up and cool-down, a dynamic, sport-specific warm-up (footwork ladders, dynamic\nlunges, lead-up passing at increasing pace) should be used, followed by a light jog and static\nstretching to cool down. Justification: dynamic movement increases blood flow, joint range\nand muscle temperature without the fatigue caused by long aerobic warm-ups, while an\neasy cool-down and stretch promotes recovery so the athlete is fresher for the next session\nand for competition.\n\nSkill instruction and practice should focus on short, high-quality passing and shooting drills\nperformed at match pace but low volume (eg 3 sets of centre-pass patterns). Justification:\nthis applies the principle of specificity, reinforcing correct technique and team combinations\nthrough movements that mirror the competition, without the volume that would cause\nresidual fatigue.\n\nConditioning three days out should be brief and match-intensity rather than aerobic-volume\nbased, such as four short, sharp 20-metre sprints with full recovery between efforts.\nJustification: this maintains the athletes' anaerobic sharpness and guards against\nreversibility (a rapid loss of fitness) without the accumulated fatigue of a full conditioning\nsession, which would compromise recovery before the game.\n\nStrategies and tactics should be rehearsed through a walk-through or video-based review of\nset plays, such as centre-pass and defensive-zone structures, at low physical intensity.\nJustification: this consolidates the team's game plan and builds confidence without adding\nfurther physical load this close to competition.\n\nFinally, athlete reflection and coach evaluation should occur through a short debrief where\nathletes rate their own readiness (eg a simple 1-10 rating of soreness and confidence) and\nthe coach records observations on execution. Justification: this feedback informs any final\nadjustments to team selection or tactics before the game, and lets the coach confirm the\nteam is ready rather than overtrained. Overall, selecting one purposeful, low-fatigue activity\nfor each element allows the coach to keep the team sharp, confident and injury-free heading\ninto competition.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q32(a)(i)","year":2020,"n":32,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2020_Q32_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Sketches in general terms how the quality of the early years of life can\n  contribute to health inequities in Australia                                                  3\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Identifies some ways that the quality of the early years of life can\n                                                                                                2\n  contribute to health inequities\n• Provides some relevant information about the early years of life                              1","sample":"Some people have limited access to health care and education about health. This can result\nin poorer pre-natal health care of the mother which can lead to a low birth weight baby,\npremature birth and/or higher rate of infant mortality. Some mothers smoke or drink alcohol\nduring pregnancy, placing the baby at risk of health complications. Infants or young children\ncan miss out on having scheduled immunisations, which can increase their risk of illnesses in\ntheir school years, or when socialising.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2020-Q32(a)(ii)","year":2020,"n":32,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2020_Q32_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Makes the relationship evident between socioeconomic factors and the\n  health inequities experienced by different population groups in Australia                     5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Describes how socioeconomic factors can contribute to the health\n  inequities experienced by different population groups in Australia                           3-4\n• Provides examples\n• Outlines socioeconomic factors that can contribute to health inequities\n                                                                                                2\n  experienced by a population group\n• Provides some relevant information about health inequities                                    1","sample":"Socioeconomic factors that can contribute significantly to health inequities for different\npopulation groups include a person's income and their level of education. This is very evident\nfor homeless people who can find it very difficult to obtain employment or to maintain\nemployment. If the homeless person is young, this can also affect their ability to be engaged\nin secondary or tertiary education, which further reduces their ability to gain employment. A\nlower level of education for homeless people can also lead to increased health risk taking\nbehaviours, because they may be unaware of the dangers eg smoking or alcohol use, which\ncan place them at greater risk of illnesses such as cancer and cardiovascular disease. Being\nunemployed for an extended period of time has a huge impact on a person's financial\nresources, and they will then find it very difficult to find affordable housing and to access\neducation. People who are homeless and/or unemployed have a much poorer health status,\nas they often need to prioritise basic needs such as food, water and shelter over their health\ncare needs. This can place them at greater risk of chronic diseases such as asthma and\ncancer. They can also have higher rates of communicable diseases like influenza, from living\nin overcrowded shared housing facilities. As homeless or unemployed people may not have\nthe financial resources to access preventative health screening, this can increase their\nmortality rate from diseases such as breast or colon cancer.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q21(a)","year":2021,"n":21,"part":"(a)","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.4"],"img":"2021_Q21_a.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Sketches in general terms two types of cardiovascular disease in\n                                                                                         3\n  Australia\n• Sketches in general terms cardiovascular disease in Australia                          2\n• Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"Coronary heart disease is the disruption of blood flow back to the heart. This can cause a\nsudden blockage to the heart's blood supply. Stroke is another type of cardiovascular\ndisease and occurs when there is a blockage in the vessels that supply blood to the brain or\nthose vessels rupture and begin to bleed.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q21(b)","year":2021,"n":21,"part":"(b)","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.4"],"img":"2021_Q21_b.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Makes evident the relationship between two protective factors and the\n                                                                                         4\n  reduction in the risk of cardiovascular disease\n• Provides characteristics and features of how two protective factors can\n                                                                                         3\n  reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease\n• Sketches in general terms protective factors for cardiovascular disease\nOR\n                                                                                         2\n• Provides characteristics and features of how one protective factor can\n  reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease\n• Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"Regular physical activity or exercise is a protective factor for cardiovascular disease because\nit will help to strengthen the heart muscle, maintain a healthy body weight and also maintain\nblood pressure within a healthy range. Making the decision to not smoke tobacco is another\nprotective factor for cardiovascular disease. Smoking tobacco or being exposed to tobacco\nsmoke is a major risk factor for cardiovascular disease because it can increase blood\npressure and the potential for developing blood clots.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q22","year":2021,"n":22,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sleep, nutrition and supplements"],"sids":["FA2.4.3"],"img":"2021_Q22_sa.png","w":1364,"h":196,"criteria":"• Provides why an athlete competing in long jump would consider using\n                                                                                         3\n  creatine supplementation to improve performance\n• Outlines creatine supplementation for improved performance                             2\n• Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"An athlete would consider using creatine supplementation to produce more ATP by\nincreasing the stores of creatine phosphate, the fuel source for the ATP−PC energy system.\nThe increased fuel stores produce faster ATP resynthesis. This can help an athlete\ncompeting in a long jump event as they need to perform a short run and an explosive jump.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q24","year":2021,"n":24,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.2","FA2.2.1"],"img":"2021_Q24_sa.png","w":1364,"h":235,"criteria":"• Provides characteristics and features of how specificity and progressive\n  overload can be applied to a strength training program to improve\n  performance                                                                            5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Sketches in general terms how specificity and progressive overload can\n  be applied to a strength training program to improve performance                       4\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms how specificity and/or progressive overload\n  can be applied to a strength training program                                          3\n• Provides example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms specificity and/or progressive overload and/or\n                                                                                         2\n  strength training\n• Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"Specificity involves an athlete targeting the muscle groups that are needed in their sport,\neg a swimmer concentrating on lat pull-down exercises to develop hypertrophy of their\nlatissimus dorsi. By strengthening this muscle, the swimmer can have a stronger pull in the\nwater, increasing their speed within a race, resulting in a quicker time.\n\nProgressive overload can be achieved by an athlete increasing the resistance they are lifting,\neg a rower bench-pressing 90 kg in one session and 100 kg in the next. This increase in\nresistance helps develop muscle hypertrophy and strength of the pectoralis major, enabling\nthe rower to pull stronger through their stroke and row faster in short distance races. Athletes\nmay also increase the volume of their training, eg more sessions per week, or increase the\nnumber of repetitions or sets of an exercise. This would make the rower stronger, generating\nmore force with each stroke.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q30(a)(i)","year":2021,"n":30,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2021_Q30_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Sketches in general terms how to reduce the risk of overuse injuries in\n  children and young athletes when they are engaged in sport                              3\n• Provides example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms how to reduce the risk of overuse injuries in\n                                                                                          2\n  children and young athletes when they are engaged in sport\n• Provides some relevant information                                                      1","sample":"Sporting activities that have a high impact on joints and/or sustained repetitive actions can\ncause overuse injuries in children and young athletes so it is important to take steps to\nreduce the forces/impact on children's developing joints and muscles. For example, fast\nbowlers in cricket are more prone to stress fractures from overuse. As a result, allowing a\nlimited number of overs that each bowler can play during the developmental years of cricket\nshould help reduce this risk of injury.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q30(b)","year":2021,"n":30,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4","FA2.5.2"],"img":"2021_Q30_b.png","w":1364,"h":194,"criteria":"• Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of rehabilitation\n  procedures that should be used one week after experiencing a soft tissue\n  injury\n• Draws out the implications of the rehabilitation procedures and relates               11-12\n  them to the athlete recovering from a soft tissue injury\n• Provides a logical and cohesive response\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Demonstrates a sound understanding of rehabilitation procedures that\n  should be used one week after experiencing a soft tissue injury\n• Makes evident the relationship between the rehabilitation procedures and\n  the athlete's recovery from a soft tissue injury                                      8-10\n• Provides a logical response\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Describes rehabilitation procedures an athlete should use one week after\n  experiencing a soft tissue injury                                                      5-7\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms rehabilitation procedure(s) for a soft tissue\n                                                                                         3-4\n  injury\n• Provides some relevant information                                                     1-2","sample":"One week after a soft tissue injury such as a grade II lateral ankle ligament sprain, the acute\ninflammatory response from the initial 48-72 hours has largely settled, so rehabilitation shifts\nfrom protecting the joint towards gradually restoring its movement, strength and function\nbefore a safe return to play.\n\nProgressive mobilisation should begin with gentle, pain-free range-of-motion exercises such\nas ankle circles and alphabet tracing, gradually increasing range as pain allows. This prevents\nthe stiffness and scar tissue adhesions that form when a joint is immobilised for too long,\nwhich would otherwise restrict flexibility long after the tissue has healed. However, moving\ntoo far or too fast at this stage risks re-tearing tissue that has not yet regained tensile\nstrength, so mobilisation must be closely graded to the athlete's pain response.\n\nGraduated exercise follows, beginning with light static stretching of the surrounding\nmuscles (calf, peroneals) to maintain flexibility, then conditioning such as partial\nweight-bearing balance work on a wobble board to rebuild proprioception, which is\ncommonly lost after ligament injury and is a major cause of re-injury if neglected. Total body\nfitness is maintained through low-impact alternatives such as pool running or stationary\ncycling, so that cardiovascular fitness is not lost through reversibility while the joint itself is\nprotected from load.\n\nThe use of heat and cold continues to play a role: heat packs or a warm bath before a\nmobilisation session increase blood flow and tissue elasticity, making movement easier and\nless painful, while ice is applied after exercise sessions if any swelling reappears, to control\nthe inflammatory response triggered by the new loading. Alternating heat and cold\n(contrast/hydrotherapy) can further stimulate blood flow to speed the delivery of nutrients\nneeded for tissue repair.\n\nTraining is only reintroduced once pain-free range and strength are close to normal,\nprogressing from light jogging in a straight line to gradual change-of-direction and\nsport-specific drills. Before any return to full training or competition, the athlete should be\nreassessed using TOTAPS (Talk, Observe, Touch, Active movement, Passive movement, Skill)\nto confirm the joint can tolerate sport-specific demands, linking this rehabilitation directly to\nreturn-to-play policy.\n\nFinally, psychological recovery strategies such as relaxation techniques help manage any\nanxiety or fear of re-injury that commonly develops after a soft tissue injury, since an athlete\nwho is tense or hesitant is more likely to move incorrectly and re-injure the area. Together,\nthese procedures show that effective rehabilitation is a staged, evidence-based progression\nrather than a single action, with each stage building the physical and psychological\nreadiness needed before the athlete safely returns to competition.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q31(a)(i)","year":2021,"n":31,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.5"],"img":"2021_Q31_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Sketches in general terms the dangers to an athlete of using performance\n  enhancing drugs                                                                         3\n• Provides example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms dangers of using performance enhancing\n                                                                                          2\n  drugs\n• Provides some relevant information                                                      1","sample":"There are physical dangers to an athlete from taking performance enhancing drugs. For\nexample, using anabolic steroids can cause kidney and liver damage and an enlarged heart,\nwhich increases the risk of heart attack. Specifically, in males there is a chance of shrinking\ntesticles, and in females the growth of facial hair or excess body hair.\n\nLoss of reputation can also occur to an athlete if caught taking performance enhancing\ndrugs. The athlete may be a role model, therefore being caught taking performance\nenhancing drugs can result in the loss of respect from peers, spectators and the general\npublic, which may in turn affect their mental health and financial status.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q31(a)(ii)","year":2021,"n":31,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.5"],"img":"2021_Q31_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":195,"criteria":"• Provides detailed points for and/or against the introduction of drug testing\n  at this level of the competition                                                        5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides points for and/or against the introduction of drug testing at this\n  level of the competition                                                                4\n• Provides examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of the introduction of drug testing at\n  this level of the competition                                                           3\n• Provides example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms the introduction of drug testing                              2\n• Provides some relevant information                                                      1","sample":"Drug testing at the finals of these championships can help provide an environment to prevent\ncheating, so ensuring that every athlete has an equal opportunity to win. It also recognises\nthe importance of looking after the health and safety of all athletes, regardless of their age.\nSporting bodies must be seen to promote drug-free sport so testing should be introduced at\nthis level to create a level playing field, especially as Australian championships are often\nused for the selection of national and international teams. An issue with this testing beginning\nat Australian championships is that young athletes may not know they could be required to\ntake a drug test.\n\nThe World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) publishes a list of banned substances across each\nsport. Introducing drug testing at the Australian championships will help educate athletes\nabout banned substances so they can ensure they remain drug free and their performance is\na reflection of their training and ability. An issue with drug testing is that some small amounts\nof a banned drug may be found in other products that are consumed as part of everyday life,\neg pseudoephedrine in cough mixtures. Athletes could accidently consume a performance\nenhancing drug through medications prescribed by a doctor, and suffer the consequences of\na positive drug test at this championship.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2021-Q32(a)(ii)","year":2021,"n":32,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians","The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.2.1","FA1.2.4"],"img":"2021_Q32_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Makes evident the relationship between a government intervention and its\n  effects for ONE population group that experiences health inequities                     5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of a government intervention and\n  makes links to the effects for ONE population group that experiences\n  health inequities                                                                       4\n• Provides examples\n• Sketches in general terms the effects of a government intervention for\n  ONE population group that experiences health inequities                                 3\n• Provides example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms a government intervention aimed at ONE\n  population group that experiences health inequities and/or the needs of a               2\n  group that experiences health inequities\n• Provides some relevant information                                                      1","sample":"The NDIS, introduced in 2013, has supported people with a disability to gain greater choice\nand control to help reach their goals and have more say in the planning and delivery of their\nsupport needs eg accessing specialised physiotherapy and occupational therapy from a\nprovider of their choice at a time convenient to them. For the majority of people with\ndisabilities, the NDIS is leading to improved satisfaction with choice and control, both over\nwhat supports are received and where these are obtained. Improvements with choice and\ncontrol became stronger the longer a person was on NDIS, promoting individual\nempowerment and greater access to health services.\n\nThe NDIS has led to modest improvements in the wellbeing of people with disability; this is\nfurther improving with time in the NDIS. Better wellbeing was found to be associated with\nincreased levels of support and independence for people with disabilities. Opportunities for\ngreater social participation, improved skills and developmental progress have also\ncontributed to enhanced wellbeing under the NDIS. People with disabilities often have\nsignificant financial strain due to the need for a range of health services. For some family\nmembers and carers, the NDIS has also reduced the financial stress and strain associated\nwith supporting a person living with disability.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q21(a)","year":2022,"n":21,"part":"(a)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.1"],"img":"2022_Q21_a.png","w":1364,"h":156,"criteria":"• Outlines ONE anaerobic training method appropriate for an athlete who is\n  competing in a high intensity, short duration event                                          3\n• Provides a relevant example\n• Outlines ONE anaerobic training method                                                       2\n• Provides relevant information                                                                1","sample":"Anaerobic interval training can be done by completing sets of 50 m freestyle sprints with a\nmoderate work to rest ratio, eg swimming 20 ´ 50 m freestyle sprints, leaving every 60\nseconds. This will involve the swimmer going as fast as possible, followed by a short rest\nperiod.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q21(b)","year":2022,"n":21,"part":"(b)","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.3","FA2.2.1"],"img":"2022_Q21_b.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Describes the effects of regular anaerobic training on an athlete's\n  performance                                                                                  4\n• Provides a relevant example\n• Describes an effect of anaerobic training on an athlete's performance\n                                                                                               3\n• Provides an example\n• Outlines how anaerobic training can affect an athlete's performance                          2\n• Provides relevant information                                                                1","sample":"A sprinter can participate in regular short-interval running training sessions using parachutes\nfor extra resistance. This involves working at an intensity above 85% of maximum heart rate,\nwith short rest periods, leading to muscle hypertrophy. This will assist the sprinter to\nexperience greater power and explosiveness eg at the start and initial phases of their race,\nas well as maintaining their speed towards the end of a race.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q24(a)","year":2022,"n":24,"part":"(a)","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.4","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2022_Q24_a.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Outlines the extent in Australia of ONE of the listed conditions                             3\n• Demonstrates an understanding of the extent in Australia of ONE of the\n                                                                                               2\n  listed conditions\n• Provides relevant information                                                                1","sample":"There has been an increase in the number of Australians experiencing mental health issues\nsuch as anxiety and depression in the last two years. Australia's young people have the\nhighest prevalence of mental health problems compared to any other age group.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q24(b)","year":2022,"n":24,"part":"(b)","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2022_Q24_b.png","w":1364,"h":116,"criteria":"• Explains how sociocultural determinants can affect ONE group at risk of\n                                                                                               4\n  the selected condition\n• Describes sociocultural determinants and makes links to ONE group at\n                                                                                               3\n  risk of the selected condition\n• Outlines the effect of sociocultural determinant(s) or ONE group at risk of\n                                                                                               2\n  the selected condition\n• Provides relevant information                                                                1","sample":"Sociocultural determinants such as a person's family, friends, culture and the media can\naffect a person's mental health. Elderly people are a group at risk of mental health problems,\neg depression, because they can experience greater isolation or experience grief due to the\nloss of a family member eg spouse. Elderly people may live alone and have minimal\ninteraction with friends, and this can lead to increased periods of isolation causing feelings of\nloneliness and disconnection. This can contribute to mental health problems such as\ndepression.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q25","year":2022,"n":25,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system","Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.2.4","FA1.1.4","FA1.2.7"],"img":"2022_Q25_sa.png","w":1364,"h":313,"criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive analysis of the relationship between\n  increasing funding for early intervention and prevention strategies for\n  cancer and future health care expenditure in Australia                                       8\n• Provides relevant and detailed examples\n• Provides an analysis of the relationship between increasing funding for\n  early intervention and prevention strategies for cancer and future health\n  care expenditure in Australia                                                               6-7\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Explains how increasing the funding for early intervention and prevention\n  strategies for cancer can affect future health care expenditure in Australia                4-5\n• Provides example(s)\n• Outlines features of funding for early intervention and prevention\n  strategies for cancer in Australia\n                                                                                              2-3\nOR\n• Outlines features of health care expenditure in Australia\n• Provides relevant information                                                                1","sample":"The cost of health care services to treat cancer in Australia is substantial and rising due to\nour ageing population. However, there is great potential to reduce this with increased funding\nfor prevention and early intervention and this is evident with strategies for breast cancer. For\nexample, breast screening every two years for women aged over 40 years will help in the\nearly detection of breast cancer, which then increases the options for successful treatment\nand greatly improves the survival rate. If the current funding increased, more services would\nbe available, which means more women could be screened and diagnosed early. This\nmeans more effective treatment options, which may prevent breast cancer from progressing\nwould result in a significant reduction in future health care expenditure for treatment services\nsuch as ongoing chemotherapy, radiation, surgery and palliative care over the next 5-10\nyears of life.\n\nLung cancer is another example where increasing the current funding for early intervention\nand prevention strategies would decrease the future health care expenditure. This can be\nseen through the effects of increased funding for smoking cessation programs, quality\neducation programs for young people and media education campaigns for the broader\ncommunity to stop the uptake and reduce the ongoing use of tobacco. Increased funding for\nthese prevention strategies would reduce the number of Australians developing lung cancer.\nFewer people with lung cancer would then greatly decrease the cost of future health care\nexpenditure to treat people with lung cancer in hospital or outpatient clinics.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q29(a)(i)","year":2022,"n":29,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2022_Q29_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Distinguishes between TWO different types of skin injury\n                                                                                                3\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Demonstrates an understanding of skin injuries                                                2\n• Provides relevant information                                                                 1","sample":"A skin abrasion occurs when the outer layer of skin is removed via a scraping motion, such\nas a cyclist grazing their elbows and knees. Lacerations, also known as cuts, occur when the\nskin is sliced open, resulting in bleeding, with more severe occurrences requiring stitches.\nThis might occur when two rugby league players accidentally clash heads.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q29(a)(ii)","year":2022,"n":29,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2022_Q29_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Provides a detailed explanation of the effects of rest, compression and\n  elevation on the body's inflammatory response following a soft tissue\n  injury                                                                                        5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides a sound explanation of the effects of using rest, compression\n  and elevation on the body's inflammatory response following a soft tissue\n  injury                                                                                        4\n• Provides examples\n• Provides some explanation of the effect(s) of using rest, compression and\n  elevation on the body's inflammatory response following a soft tissue\n  injury                                                                                        3\n• Provides example(s)\n• Outlines ways to manage a soft tissue injury and/or the inflammatory\n                                                                                                2\n  response following a soft tissue injury\n• Provides relevant information                                                                 1","sample":"The inflammatory response phase occurs in the first 24-72 hours after a soft tissue injury. It\nis characterised by an increase in blood flow to the injured site. This brings with it swelling,\nredness, tenderness and pain. As such, incorporating rest, compression and elevation is\nuseful to reduce the effects of the inflammatory response and improve injury recovery time.\nFor example, if a netball player sprains a ligament in their ankle, they will be removed from\nthe court and placed in a comfortable position to stop moving the ankle. Resting like this will\ndecrease blood flow to the injured area, and also decrease pain. Elevation of the injured area\nabove the level of the heart, will further reduce blood flow to the ankle, helping reduce\nswelling. This will be likely to involve the player lying down comfortably with the ankle\npropped up. This can also assist in reducing pain. Applying a compression bandage or brace\nfirmly around the ankle will also help to reduce swelling and decrease tissue damage.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q30(b)","year":2022,"n":30,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.3.2","FA2.2.1","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2022_Q30_b.png","w":1364,"h":120,"criteria":"• Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of types of training,\n  training methods and the considerations for planning a training year\n• Makes well-informed judgements about the extent to which the types of\n  training and training methods used should vary when planning a training                      11-12\n  year for ONE sport\n• Presents a logical and cohesive response\n• Provides a range of relevant examples\n• Demonstrates a detailed understanding of the types of training, training\n  methods and considerations for planning a training year\n• Discusses the extent to which the types of training and training methods\n  used should vary when planning a training year for ONE sport                                 8-10\n• Presents a logical response\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Describes appropriate types of training methods when planning a training\n  year for ONE sport                                                                            5-7\n• Provides examples\n• Outlines how types of training and/or training methods for ONE sport vary\n• Provides example(s)\n                                                                                                3-4\nOR\n• Outlines considerations when planning a training year\n• Provides relevant information                                                                 1-2","sample":"For a team sport such as Australian Rules football, the types of training and training methods\nused should vary considerably across the training year, because the physiological and skill\ndemands placed on players change markedly between the off-season, pre-season and\nin-season phases, even though a small number of training types remain present, at reduced\nvolume, throughout the year.\n\nIn the off-season, the emphasis is on rebuilding a general aerobic base and correcting any\nmuscular imbalances built up over the competitive season. Continuous and fartlek aerobic\ntraining are used to restore cardiovascular fitness, while resistance training using free\nweights targets muscular hypertrophy and addresses strength deficits, since the lower\nintensity and reduced skill focus at this time make it the safest phase to apply high\nresistance loads. Flexibility training, particularly static stretching, is maintained to protect\nthe increased muscle bulk gained through strength work. Skill training is deliberately\nminimal in this phase, reflecting the principle of variety and allowing players to recover\nmentally from the demands of match play.\n\nAs the pre-season begins, training should shift substantially towards anaerobic and\nsport-specific methods. Aerobic interval and circuit training replace purely continuous work\nto better reflect the stop-start, high-intensity nature of AFL, while anaerobic training such as\nHIIT and short-interval running develops the repeat-sprint ability required to chase the ball\nand change direction at match pace. Strength training shifts from hypertrophy towards\npower, incorporating plyometrics to improve leap for marking contests. Skill and tactical\ntraining increases sharply through drills, modified games and games for specific outcomes,\napplying the principle of specificity as the season approaches. This demonstrates that the\nextent of change between phases is large: the whole emphasis of training moves from general\nconditioning to sport-specific power and skill.\n\nOnce the in-season begins, training methods vary again, but now within a much narrower\nband from week to week due to the principle of progressive overload being balanced against\nrecovery. Aerobic and resistance training are maintained only at a maintenance level, using\nshort circuit or interval sessions, so that fitness gained in pre-season is not lost through\nreversibility, but volume is kept low to prevent players carrying fatigue into matches. Skill and\ntactical training becomes the dominant focus, since match execution now matters more than\nfurther physiological adaptation, and sessions are tapered in the days before each game to\nensure players peak for competition.\n\nOverall, therefore, the types of training and training methods used should vary to a large\nextent across the phases of an AFL training year, moving from general aerobic and strength\nwork in the off-season, to anaerobic, power and skill-specific work in the pre-season, to\nmaintenance and tapering in-season. Flexibility and some element of skill training are the\nmethods that vary least, remaining present throughout as a consistent foundation, while\naerobic, anaerobic and strength training vary the most in both volume and method to match\nthe demands of each phase of the training year.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q31(a)(i)","year":2022,"n":31,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2022_Q31_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Outlines TWO social attributes that have contributed to health inequities\n  experienced by a population group in Australia                                                3\n• Provides examples\n• Demonstrates some understanding of social attribute(s) that have\n  contributed to health inequities experienced by a population group in                         2\n  Australia\n• Provides relevant information                                                                 1","sample":"Social exclusion is evident for individuals who are homeless as they can feel disempowered,\nembarrassed or ashamed, and they may disconnect from mainstream society. This can\naffect a homeless person's confidence and ability to access health care services.\nDiscrimination can also contribute to health inequities for people who are homeless as they\nmay be treated differently by prospective employers, making it more difficult to secure\nemployment.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2022-Q31(a)(ii)","year":2022,"n":31,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system","Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.2.1","FA1.1.2"],"img":"2022_Q31_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Provides a detailed explanation of how improving access to services and\n  transport can reduce health inequities experienced by a population group                      5\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides a sound explanation of how improving access to services and\n  transport can reduce health inequities experienced by a population group                      4\n• Provides examples\n• Provides some explanation of how improving access to services and\n  transport can reduce health inequities experienced by a particular\n  population group                                                                              3\n• Provides example(s)\n• Outlines ways access to services and/or transport can be improved for a\n                                                                                                2\n  particular population group\n• Provides relevant information                                                                 1","sample":"To close the inequity gap, people living in geographically remote populations need to be\nprovided with greater access to services, especially health services that focus on\npreventative health. For example, increasing the number of GPs that are available in remote\ncommunities will increase the availability for people to see a GP for preventative health\nscreening for chronic diseases like diabetes and CVD. This improved access will mean that\nrisk factors like being overweight can be identified and addressed early before the condition\ndevelops. Improving transport options, such as shuttle buses to collect people from their\nproperty and transport them to a health facility, can help to increase access to services for\nthis population group. Improving transport increases the use of health facilities for both\nprevention and treatment for conditions. Alternatively, taking the health service to the people\nhelps overcome the significant access barrier and increases participation in health screening\nlike mammograms.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q22","year":2023,"n":22,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians","The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.1.5","FA1.2.7","FA1.2.4"],"img":"2023_Q22_sa.png","w":1364,"h":156,"criteria":"• Demonstrates a thorough understanding of a healthy ageing population\n                                                                                         4\n   and how it affects Australia's health care system\n • Demonstrates a sound understanding of how a healthy ageing\n                                                                                         3\n   population affects Australia's health care system\n • Demonstrates a basic understanding of the effects of a healthy ageing\n                                                                                         2\n   population\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"If Australia's ageing population leads a healthy lifestyle, which includes regular exercise and\nnot smoking, a greater proportion of them will be able to live independently for a longer time,\nincreasing vacancies within nursing homes and hospitals and lowering government\nexpenditure for facilities and services. Conversely, as more Australians over 65 live\nindependently because of healthy ageing, the demand for at-home community care support\nsuch as housekeeping or pre-prepared meals may increase.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q23(a)","year":2023,"n":23,"part":"(a)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.3.4","FA2.2.1","FA2.3.1"],"img":"2023_Q23_a.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Outlines TWO benefits of developing tactics for use in team sports                    3\n • Demonstrates some understanding of developing tactics for use in team\n                                                                                         2\n   sports\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"Developing tactics in team sports enhances players' strategic thinking, allowing them to\nadapt to a changing game situation and gain an advantage over their opponent. Additionally,\nwell-defined tactics promote teamwork and communication among players, emphasising the\nimportance of collaboration in performing set plays in a game.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q24","year":2023,"n":24,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sleep, nutrition and supplements"],"sids":["FA2.4.3","FA2.4.1"],"img":"2023_Q24_sa.png","w":1364,"h":156,"criteria":"• Provides a detailed discussion of the effects of TWO supplements used\n                                                                                         5\n   by athletes to alter their performance\n • Provides a discussion of the effect(s) of TWO supplements used by\n                                                                                         4\n   athletes to alter their performance\n • Outlines the effects of supplement(s) in relation to an athlete's\n                                                                                         3\n   performance\n • Demonstrates a basic understanding of a supplement used by athletes                   2\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"As a stimulant, caffeine can improve the concentration and focus levels of athletes. It may be\nparticularly beneficial in sports such as squash that requires quick thinking and reaction time.\nConversely, too much caffeine can result in heightened physiological arousal which can have\na negative effect on fine motor control in sports such as archery and target shooting.\n\nCreatine supplementation increases the creatine stores in athletes, enabling them to extend\nthe duration of the ATP/PC energy system. This can improve performance in high intensity\nsports such as long jump and sprinting. Creatine supplementation also enables the ATP/PC\nsystem to recover faster, so athletes in an event such as discus can repeat powerful throws\nin a short space of time in competition.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q27","year":2023,"n":27,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.3","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2023_Q27_sa.png","w":1364,"h":195,"criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive analysis of the relationship and implications\n   between ONE physiological adaptation and improved performance                         8\n • Provides detailed examples\n • Provides an analysis of the relationship between ONE physiological\n   adaptation and improved performance                                                  6-7\n • Provides example(s)\n • Provides some explanation of the relationship between ONE\n   physiological adaptation and improved performance                                    4-5\n • Provides an example\n • Demonstrates a basic understanding of a physiological adaptation                     2-3\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"Stroke volume is a physiological adaptation that occurs with training. Aerobic training can\nlead to an increase in stroke volume. The training causes the size of the heart and the\nventricles within it to enlarge. The walls of the ventricles become thicker and hence stronger.\nIt also increases the diastolic filling capacity of the heart. These two changes allow more\noxygenated blood to enter what is now an enlarged heart and the stronger walls allow for this\ngreater volume of blood to be ejected with each heartbeat. Thus, more oxygen is supplied to\nworking muscles leading to improved performance. Increased stroke volume would supply a\nroad cyclist's muscles with more oxygen, enabling them to work at a higher intensity for a\nlonger period of time during a race, which would increase their chances of victory. Similarly,\nan increased stroke volume would enable a rower to maintain a higher stroke rate throughout\na 2000 m race, as the working muscles would receive greater amounts of oxygen and\nnutrients. This would ensure the rower could maintain their form as part of a team and stay\nsynchronised as the race progresses, reflecting the relationship between increased stroke\nvolume and improved performance.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q30(a)(i)","year":2023,"n":30,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2023_Q30_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Outlines TWO classifications of the injury                                             3\n • Demonstrates an understanding of classification(s) of the injury                       2\n • Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"As the injury occurred away from the point of impact and was due to internal forces, it can be\nclassified as indirect. The injury can also be classified as a soft tissue injury as damage has\noccurred to ligaments. Soft tissue injuries are those which occur in any body tissue other\nthan bone or teeth.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q30(a)(ii)","year":2023,"n":30,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2023_Q30_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":83,"criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of the process to determine the nature\n                                                                                          5\n   and extent of the injury, with relevant examples\n • Provides a sound explanation of the process to determine the nature and\n                                                                                          4\n   extent of the injury, with examples\n • Provides some explanation of the process to determine the nature and\n                                                                                          3\n   extent of the injury, with example(s)\n • Demonstrates some understanding of the process and/or the nature\n                                                                                          2\n   and/or extent of an injury\n • Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"To determine the nature and extent of this injury, a procedure known as TOTAPS should be\nfollowed. Talk to the athlete to gather information about the cause, nature, and site of the\ninjury. Questions such as ‘where does it hurt' and ‘did you hear a snap or a crack' are\nappropriate. Next, observe the injured knee and look for any obvious signs of deformity or\nswelling. Touch the knee gently and feel for any deformity or swelling and determine the\nexact location of the injury. Ask the athlete to actively move the knee. Finally, physically\nmobilise the joint (passive movement) using a range of movements to identify any instability\nin the knee joint. Ligament damage would typically be identified at this stage, so a skills test\nsuch as running side to side, would not be performed.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q31(a)(i)","year":2023,"n":31,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.1"],"img":"2023_Q31_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Outlines TWO methods an athlete can use to improve performance for\n                                                                                          3\n   the type of training chosen\n • Demonstrates an understanding of method(s) an athlete can use to\n                                                                                          2\n   improve performance for the type of training chosen\n • Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"A strength training method is weight training. For example, a basketball player would use\nfree/fixed weights to increase power in their legs to enable them to jump higher in retrieving\nthe ball. In addition, a rugby league player would use isometric training such as a bridge hold\nto improve their core strength. This will enable them to maintain superior running form under\nfatigue.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q31(a)(ii)","year":2023,"n":31,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment","Sustained performance and injury","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.1.2","FA2.5.3","FA2.2.3"],"img":"2023_Q31_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of TWO measurements that can be\n   used by an athlete to monitor improved performance for the type of                     5\n   training chosen, with relevant examples\n • Provides a sound explanation of TWO measurements that can be used\n   by an athlete to monitor improved performance for the type of training                 4\n   chosen, with examples\n • Provides some explanation of measurements that can be used by an\n   athlete to monitor improved performance for a type of training, with                   3\n   example(s)\n • Demonstrates some understanding of measurement(s) that can be used\n                                                                                          2\n   by an athlete to monitor improved performance\n • Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"If an Olympic weight lifter is participating in weight training they could complete the 1RM test,\nwhereby they would undertake a single bench press. After a period of training, they would\nengage in the bench press again. If the weight of the 1RM is greater than the first time, they\nhave gained muscular strength, enabling them to improve performance by lifting a heavier\nweight. Additionally, the athlete could also make use of a hand-grip dynamometer to\nmeasure arm strength before and after the implementation of a weight training program,\nwhich would similarly allow the athlete to propel a greater weight above their head.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q31(b)","year":2023,"n":31,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.5"],"img":"2023_Q31_b.png","w":1364,"h":119,"criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive assessment of the consequences for athletes\n   testing positive to performance enhancing drugs\n                                                                                        11-12\n • Presents a logical and cohesive response\n • Uses relevant examples to support response\n • Provides a thorough assessment of the consequences for athletes\n   testing positive to performance enhancing drugs\n                                                                                        8-10\n • Presents a logical response\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Provides a sound assessment of the consequences for athletes testing\n   positive to performance enhancing drugs\n                                                                                         5-7\n • Presents a structured response\n • Provides example(s)\n • Demonstrates an understanding of the consequences associated with\n                                                                                         3-4\n   testing positive to performance enhancing drugs\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1-2","sample":"Athletes who test positive for performance-enhancing drugs face various consequences,\nboth immediate and long-term.\n\n• They may be disqualified from the competition in which they tested positive and may lose\n  any medals, titles or records achieved during that period.\n• Athletics may receive a suspension from their sport, ranging from a few months to several\n  years, depending on the severity of the violation and their previous offences.\n• A tarnished reputation is a significant consequence as it can damage an athlete's career\n  prospects, sponsorships and endorsements.\n• Athletes may face public scrutiny, criticism and a loss of public trust, impacting their\n  personal and professional lives.\n• Testing positive for banned substances can result in financial penalties, including the\n  repayment of prize money or legal costs associated with the investigation.\n• Athletes may experience psychological and emotional stress due to the stigma and shame\n  associated with doping allegations.\n• Their team or country may suffer reputational damage, impacting the support and funding\n  they receive.\n• Athletes may need to undergo rehabilitative programs or therapy to address drug\n  dependencies and regain their physical and mental well-being.\n• In some cases, repeat offenses or involvement in drug taking can lead to lifetime bans\n  from the sport, permanently ending an athlete's career.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2023-Q32(b)","year":2023,"n":32,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system","Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.2.4","FA1.1.2","FA1.2.1"],"img":"2023_Q32_b.png","w":1364,"h":112,"criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive evaluation of how funding has been used to\n   solve health inequities in Australia\n                                                                                        11-12\n • Presents a logical and cohesive response\n • Uses relevant examples to support response\n • Provides a thorough evaluation of how funding has been used to solve\n   health inequities in Australia\n                                                                                        8-10\n • Presents a logical response\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Provides a sound evaluation of how funding has been used to solve\n   health inequities in Australia\n                                                                                         5-7\n • Presents a structured response\n • Provides examples\n • Demonstrates an understanding of how funding has been used to solve\n                                                                                         3-4\n   health inequities in Australia\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1-2","sample":"Funding has been utilised increasingly to positively address health inequities in Australia.\n• The allocation of funds has aimed to improve access to healthcare services for\n  disadvantaged populations.\n• Funding has been directed towards initiatives targeting specific health issues prevalent\n  among marginalised communities.\n• Programs have been implemented to address social determinants of health, such as\n  housing, education and employment.\n• Efforts have been made to reduce barriers to healthcare, including improving\n  infrastructure and increasing healthcare workforce capacity in underserved areas.\n• Funding has supported research and data collection to better understand health\n  disparities and develop evidence-based interventions.\n• Collaboration between governments, healthcare organisations and community groups has\n  been facilitated through funding initiatives.\n• Funding has enabled the implementation of culturally sensitive healthcare services for\n  Indigenous populations.\n• Ongoing monitoring and evaluation of funded projects have been conducted to assess\n  their impact on health inequities.\n• Despite these efforts, challenges remain in achieving equitable health outcomes,\n  indicating the need for sustained funding and continued collaboration.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2024-Q21","year":2024,"n":21,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.3"],"img":"2024_Q21_sa.png","w":1364,"h":195,"criteria":"• Outlines TWO sources of stress that can affect an athlete's performance\n                                                                                         3\n   in a grand final game\n • Demonstrates some understanding of source(s) of stress that can affect\n                                                                                         2\n   an athlete's performance\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"An athlete might experience personal pressure from their own desire to win and accomplish\ntheir goals, given that the grand final is the final game of the season to determine the overall\nwinner. Similarly, social pressure from coaches, family, and supporters who may have victory\nexpectations can cause stress for an athlete.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2024-Q25","year":2024,"n":25,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.5","FA1.2.6"],"img":"2024_Q25_sa.png","w":1364,"h":192,"criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of why there has been an increase in\n   complementary health products OR services in Australia                                5\n • Provides a relevant example\n • Provides a sound explanation of why there has been an increase in\n   complementary health products OR services in Australia                                4\n • Provides an example\n • Demonstrates some understanding of why there has been an increase in\n                                                                                         3\n   complementary health products OR services in Australia\n • Demonstrates a basic understanding of the use of complementary health\n   products OR services\n OR                                                                                      2\n • Provides reason(s) why there has been an increase in the use of health\n   products OR services\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"The credibility of complementary health has grown as health authorities have successfully\ntrialled and experimented with complementary services. Australian consumers perceive\nthese services to be more legitimate as a result of regulatory approval. Private health insurers, for example, now offer extras cover\nrebates for services such as acupuncture and remedial massage, and pharmacies stock an\never-growing range of complementary products alongside conventional medicines. This\ngrowth in mainstream access improves consumer confidence in making informed\ndecisions, as they perceive fewer risks in adding a complementary therapy such as\nremedial massage alongside their oral medication for their lower back pain.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2024-Q27","year":2024,"n":27,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2024_Q27_sa.png","w":1364,"h":459,"criteria":"• Provides a well-informed judgement of the extent to which\n   socioeconomic factors impact the health of ONE chosen group\n   experiencing health inequities                                                        8\n • Provides relevant and detailed examples\n • Provides a sound judgement of the extent to which socioeconomic\n   factors impact the health of ONE chosen group experiencing health\n   inequities                                                                           6-7\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Provides a sound understanding of how socioeconomic factors impact on\n   the health of ONE chosen group experiencing health inequities                        4-5\n • Provides example(s)\n • Demonstrates a basic understanding of socioeconomic factor(s) that\n                                                                                        2-3\n   impact health for a group experiencing health inequities\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"Those who have a lower socioeconomic status (SES) are more likely to drop out of formal\neducation earlier due to disengagement with the education system. This results in students\nmissing vital lessons on health decision-making, for example assessing the dangers of\nvaping. This greatly decreases the extent to which they can develop their health literacy\nskills, reducing their chance of developing lung disease.\n\nFurthermore, the lack of education obtained by people of low SES background leads to a\nreduction in employment and income prospects. Higher paying jobs typically require some\nform of post school education. Without higher incomes, lower SES people cannot afford to\nmake healthy choices, such as purchasing more expensive fresh food items, making the\nextent to which they might become overweight far greater. Therefore, this is evident in their\nincreased morbidity rates of chronic conditions such as heart disease, which will significantly\nreduce their overall quality of life.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2024-Q30(b)","year":2024,"n":30,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury","Training and adaptations","Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.5.4","FA2.2.2","FA2.3.3"],"img":"2024_Q30_b.png","w":1297,"h":110,"criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive evaluation of the use of specific warm-up and\n   psychological readiness procedures to indicate if an athlete is ready to\n   return to play after an injury                                                       11-12\n • Presents a logical and cohesive response\n • Provides a range of relevant examples to support responses\n • Provides a sound evaluation of the use of specific warm-up and\n   psychological readiness procedures to indicate if an athlete is ready to\n   return to play after an injury                                                       8-10\n • Presents a logical response\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Provides some evaluation of the specific warm-up and psychological\n   readiness procedures to indicate if an athlete is ready to return to play\n   after an injury                                                                       5-7\n • Provides a structured response\n • Provides example(s)\n • Demonstrates some understanding of a warm-up and/or psychological\n                                                                                         3-4\n   readiness procedure(s) when returning to play after an injury\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1-2","sample":"There are various specific warm-up and psychological readiness procedures that a coach\ncould use to indicate if an athlete is ready to return to play after an injury.\n• Coaches should conduct a thorough warm-up for athletes returning to play after an injury.\n  Additional time should be spent focusing on the specific body part where the injury\n  occurred to avoid the potential for re-injury. For example, if a soccer player injured their\n  quadricep, the use of heat, a foam roller, massage and additional stretching of the\n  quadricep may be necessary in readiness for the game.\n• Warm-ups could include conditioning and drills that replicate the intensity and demands of\n  the sport and the skills executed to assist with determining the degree of readiness for\n  competition. For example, an NRL player is required to possess a high level of agility. If\n  they sustained a broken ankle, the warm-up may indicate that the injured site is still\n  unable to support the execution of the required movement for the game.\n• Psychological readiness may be determined by the confidence level of an athlete or the\n  fear of overcompensating to avoid reinjury, despite rehabilitation. This is determined\n  through pre and post-surveys in consultation with sport psychologists.\n• Working with a sport psychologist to engage in mental rehearsal and visualisation if the\n  athlete has a fear of returning to play post injury as rehabilitation staff such as doctors and\n  physiotherapists cannot measure this, only physical readiness. They can also assist with\n  managing frustration, disappointment and reducing while prioritising the wellbeing of the\n  athlete.\n• Working with a sports psychologist or professional to discuss the pressures from\n  sponsors, media, family or coaches for an athlete to return to the game earlier than when\n  they are ready which may result in an impeded psychological readiness. This could\n  potentially be identified by the coach or trainer or the athlete themselves.\n• The athlete should be pain free and the use of support bandage or preventative taping\n  may be necessary to stabilise a joint, not only to provide the physical support but also the\n  mental (psychological) assurance that the injury is supported.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2024-Q31(a)(i)","year":2024,"n":31,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.2"],"img":"2024_Q31_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Provides a description of ONE phase of competition that can be included\n                                                                                          3\n   when planning a training year\n • Demonstrates some understanding of ONE phase of competition that\n                                                                                          2\n   can be included when planning a training year\n • Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"The pre-season phase of competition is the period prior to competition where training\nfocuses on developing the fundamentals requirements of the sport, particularly fitness and\nskills. This forms a strong foundation upon which coaches can extend their athletes as the\nseason begins so they can perform at competition standard.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2024-Q31(a)(ii)","year":2024,"n":31,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.3.2","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2024_Q31_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of how an athlete could taper to\n                                                                                          5\n   improve performance for a major sporting event, with relevant examples\n • Provides a sound explanation of how an athlete could taper to improve\n                                                                                          4\n   performance for a major sporting event, with examples\n • Provides some understanding of how an athlete could taper to improve\n                                                                                          3\n   performance for a major sporting event, with example(s)\n • Demonstrates a basic understanding of tapering for a sporting event                    2\n • Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"Tapering involves an athlete progressively reducing their training volume and intensity\n1 to 2 weeks prior to a major sporting event. For example, a half-marathon runner may\nreduce their training intensity from 80% MHR progressively down to 60% MHR; and/or their\nrunning distance from 16 km progressively down to 6 km, within the 2 weeks prior to a major\nevent. This reduction in training allows the body to increase muscle glycogen stores and\nprevent muscle fatigue, allowing for repair and strengthening of muscle and connective\ntissue, resulting in optimal performance.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2024-Q31(b)","year":2024,"n":31,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.3"],"img":"2024_Q31_b.png","w":1297,"h":110,"criteria":"• Provides well-informed judgements of the extent advancements in\n   sporting technology have improved performance\n                                                                                         11-12\n • Presents a logical and cohesive response\n • Provides a range of relevant examples to support responses\n • Provides sound judgements of the extent advancements in sporting\n   technology have improved performance\n                                                                                          8-10\n • Presents a logical response\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Provides some judgement of the extent advancements in sporting\n   technology have improved performance\n                                                                                           5-7\n • Presents a structured response\n • Provides example(s)\n • Demonstrates a basic understanding of how advancements in sporting\n                                                                                           3-4\n   technology have improved performance\n • Provides some relevant information                                                      1-2","sample":"Advancements in sporting technology have profoundly improved accuracy and speed, player\nanalysis and provided high quality feedback to enhance performance. They have also\nassisted referees and umpires to make fair and consistent calls.\n• Swimsuits - technology has seen the advancement from Nylon to Lycra to LZR swimsuits.\n  These advancements have enabled the swimsuit to be form-fitting, have less water\n  absorption, and the latest design significantly reducing drag, resulting in faster\n  performances in the water.\n• Technology advancements in swimming also include the touchpad timing system which\n  eliminated human timing error and provides accuracy of athlete's performance down to\n  the hundredth of a second. This precision has prevented disputes over timing and\n  ensured fair and accurate results. In addition, swimming pool designs advanced to lessen\n  resistance by deepening the pool and designing larger gutters that prevent waves. Both\n  advancements have resulted in improved performance.\n• Equipment such as tennis racquets have advanced from wooden racquets in the 1800s to\n  metal racquets in the 1960s to graphite in the 1980s, to smart racquets in 2014 which\n  included chips in the handle to record power and spin. These advances have improved\n  performance by increasing the racquet lifespan and stiffness, reducing the weight and\n  providing statistical data for players and coaches to pinpoint improvement.\n• Equipment - Hawk-eyed innovations used in cricket, tennis, soccer and golf, to visually\n  track the trajectory of the ball and display a profile of statistics. It provides optical tracking,\n  vision-processing and video review making sport fairer, safer and more engaging. This\n  has had a major impact on referee decisions, eliminating uncertainty of players and\n  spectators about the decision of the match referee. This has allowed for quicker and more\n  accurate calls, allowing the game to progress with accuracy.\n• GPS technology is used by a variety of sports to pinpoint the exact location of athletes on\n  the field, court or ice. This technology provides coaches and team specialists with\n  valuable information about player positioning, speed and distance covered during\n  competition. This has enhanced sporting performance by ensuring that training programs\n  are specific to energy system and components of fitness requirements; and that athlete's\n  diet and recovery programs are optimal for peak performance.\n\n• Virtual reality introduced in the last decade has allowed sports such as rugby to create\n  simulated rugby scenarios such as lineouts and high balls. This technology can improve\n  athletes' mental learning, visualisation and decision-making.\n• Artificial Intelligence (AI) is relatively new and fast growing in the sporting industry. AI is\n  able to analyse large input of data in real time, provide immediate feedback to athletes\n  and coaches and provides comprehensive training programs to improve performance\n  based on the data input. This will allow for quicker progression and advancements in\n  sporting performance.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2024-Q32(b)","year":2024,"n":32,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1"],"img":"2024_Q32_b.png","w":1297,"h":110,"criteria":"• Provides well-informed judgements of the use of health data to\n   determine the extent of inequities for ONE chosen population group\n                                                                                        11-12\n • Presents a logical and cohesive response\n • Provides a range of relevant examples to support responses\n • Provides sound judgements of the use of health data to determine the\n   extent of inequities for ONE chosen population group\n                                                                                        8-10\n • Presents a logical response\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Provides some judgement of the use of health data to determine the\n   extent of inequities for ONE chosen population group\n                                                                                         5-7\n • Presents a structured response\n • Provides example(s)\n • Demonstrates some understanding of the use of health data to\n                                                                                         3-4\n   determine the extent of inequities for ONE chosen population group\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1-2","sample":"Using health data provides an effective means of assessing disparities among Aboriginal and\nTorres Strait Islander communities, offering critical insights into inequities, and guiding\ntailored programs to enhance health outcomes.\n\nHealth Outcomes and Mortality Rates:\n• Health data stresses substantial inequalities in health outcomes and mortality rates\n  between Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and non-Indigenous\n  populations.\n• Elevated rates of chronic diseases, including diabetes, cardiovascular diseases, and\n  respiratory illnesses, contribute to reduced life expectancy among Indigenous Australians.\n• Analysis of health data reveals a higher prevalence of chronic diseases and increased\n  mortality rates stemming from preventable conditions within Aboriginal and Torres Strait\n  Islander communities.\n• Inequities in health outcomes emphasise the imperative for Aboriginal and Torres Strait\n  Islander programs in addressing social determinants of health and systemic barriers to\n  healthcare access.\n\nAccess to Healthcare Services:\n• Analysis of health data highlights variations in healthcare access, such as fewer primary\n  care visits or delays in seeking medical attention.\n• Discrepancies in healthcare access contribute to lower health outcomes and heightened\n  prevalence of preventable illnesses within Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander\n  communities.\n• Evidence from health data highlights lower rates of preventive screenings and\n  immunisations among these populations.\n\nSocial Determinants of Health:\n• Health data underlines the impact of social determinants, such as socioeconomic status,\n  education, housing, and employment, on health outcomes.\n\n• Elevated rates of unemployment, lower educational attainment, and inadequate housing\n  conditions contribute to diminished health outcomes among Indigenous Australians.\n• Alleviating health inequities among Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities\n  necessitates addressing social determinants of health through policy and\n  community-based initiatives.\n\nCultural Competency and Indigenous Health Initiatives:\n• Health data highlights the significance of culturally competent healthcare delivery and the\n  effectiveness of Indigenous-led health initiatives in enhancing health outcomes.\n• Utilising health data to assess disparities among Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander\n  communities, in collaboration with policymakers and healthcare professionals, facilitates\n  the development of targeted programs and policies aimed at reducing disparities and\n  enhancing health outcomes within these communities.\n\nAnswers could also include:\n\n•   Homeless\n•   Incarcerated\n•   Geographically remote\n•   Culturally and linguistically diverse","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q21","year":2025,"n":21,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.4"],"img":"2025_Q21_sa.png","w":1364,"h":426,"criteria":"• Outlines TWO risk factors for ONE of the conditions listed                            3\n • Demonstrates some understanding of a risk factor(s) for ONE of the\n                                                                                         2\n   conditions listed\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"Diabetes risk factors include genetic, lifestyle and environmental influences. A family history\nof diabetes increases susceptibility due to inherited traits. Poor diet, inactivity and weight\ngain contribute to insulin resistance. Environmental factors, like limited access to healthy\nfood or high stress, also play a role in increasing risk.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q22","year":2025,"n":22,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.1"],"img":"2025_Q22_sa.png","w":1364,"h":156,"criteria":"• Explains how infant mortality is used to measure the health status of a\n                                                                                         4\n   population\n • Describes how infant mortality is used to measure the health status of a\n                                                                                         3\n   population\n • Demonstrates an understanding of infant mortality and/or how it is used\n                                                                                         2\n   to measure the health status of a population\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"Infant mortality plays a key role in assessing public health. A low infant mortality rate lifts\nand reflects a population's overall health status, and is linked to a higher average life\nexpectancy for that population - it does not predict the life expectancy of any one\nindividual. This data could inform decisions made by\nprofessionals and policymakers such as the government to show how healthy or unhealthy\nthe nation is. For example, as Australia's infant mortality rate decreases and life expectancy\nincreases, it demonstrates the health of the population improving as less babies are dying in\nthe first year of birth.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q23","year":2025,"n":23,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sleep, nutrition and supplements"],"sids":["FA2.4.3"],"img":"2025_Q23_sa.png","w":1364,"h":156,"criteria":"• Describes the potential effects of caffeine on an athlete's performance               4\n • Describes a potential effect of caffeine on an athlete's performance                  3\n • Outlines the use of caffeine and/or the potential effects on an athlete's\n                                                                                         2\n   performance\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"Caffeine as a form of supplementation could increase an athlete's reaction time, alertness\nand increase energy production. Caffeine could enhance an athlete's focus, allowing them to\ncompete for longer without fatigue. When overconsumed, however, too much caffeine could\nmean the athlete finds their performance is impeded as they could become overstimulated\nand lack control of their movements, resulting in a lower level of performance in sports such\nas golf.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q24","year":2025,"n":24,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians","The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.1.5","FA1.2.7"],"img":"2025_Q24_sa.png","w":1364,"h":194,"criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of the impact an ageing population has\n                                                                                         5\n   on the health service workforce in Australia\n • Provides a sound explanation of the impact an ageing population has on\n                                                                                         4\n   the health service workforce in Australia\n • Describes the impact an ageing population has on the health service\n                                                                                         3\n   workforce in Australia\n • Demonstrates an understanding of an ageing population and/or the\n                                                                                         2\n   health service workforce in Australia\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"An ageing population in Australia impacts the health service workforce by increasing demand\nfor aged care and chronic disease management, leading to workforce shortages and\npressure on staff. This occurs because more people are living longer with chronic conditions,\nsuch as cardiovascular disease requiring ongoing access to complex healthcare services\nover extended periods. Health workers face higher workloads managing conditions like\ndementia and cardiovascular disease, which require specialised skills and prolonged care.\nThese demands often result in burnout, high turnover rates and the need for upskilling,\nhighlighting the importance of recruitment and training to sustain the workforce.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q25(b)","year":2025,"n":25,"part":"(b)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.3.3","FA2.5.2"],"img":"2025_Q25_b.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of how ONE relaxation technique could\n   be used to manage the anxiety of an athlete                                           5\n • Provides a relevant example\n • Explains how ONE relaxation technique could be used to manage the\n   anxiety of an athlete                                                                 4\n • Provides an example\n • Describes how ONE relaxation technique could be used to manage the\n                                                                                         3\n   anxiety of an athlete\n • Outlines a relaxation technique(s) and/or managing anxiety                            2\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"Athletes could use relaxation techniques to rationalise emotions and prevent fear responses\nto reduce anxiety. Progressive muscular relaxation could be used by progressively linearly\ncontracting and relaxing the body's muscles to reduce their tension. For instance, a darts\nplayer may decrease their anxiety by progressively focusing on contracting and relaxing each\nfinger, followed by their hand, forearm, biceps, triceps, and shoulder, reducing the anxious\ntension in this limb ready to throw the dart. This decreased anxiety can reduce the jittery\neffect of the limb to achieve optimum arousal.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q26","year":2025,"n":26,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.2","FA2.2.3","FA2.2.1"],"img":"2025_Q26_sa.png","w":1364,"h":195,"criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive analysis of the relationship between training\n   thresholds and TWO physiological adaptations                                          8\n • Provides relevant examples of aerobic and resistance training\n • Provides an analysis of the relationship between training thresholds and\n   TWO physiological adaptations                                                        6-7\n • Provides examples of aerobic and resistance training\n • Explains the relationship between training threshold(s) and a\n   physiological adaptation(s)                                                          4-5\n • Provides example(s) of aerobic and/or resistance training\n • Outlines features of training threshold(s) and/or physiological\n                                                                                        2-3\n   adaptation(s) and/or types of aerobic or resistance training\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1","sample":"The aerobic training threshold is the point where an athlete's body shifts from primarily using\nfat as fuel to carbohydrates, typically at around 70% of their maximum heart rate. Training at\nthis level improves cardiovascular fitness through adaptations such as increased cardiac\noutput, which strengthens the heart and enhances oxygen delivery to muscles. It also\nimproves lung capacity, making oxygen exchange more efficient. For example, a\nlong-distance runner training at a steady pace for 60 minutes improves their ability to sustain\neffort over extended periods, delaying fatigue during a marathon.\n\nIn resistance training, the threshold refers to the intensity needed to stimulate muscle growth\n(hypertrophy) or strength gains, typically at 70−85% (6-12 repetitions) of an athlete's one-rep\nmax (1 RM) or load. Training at or above this level causes muscular adaptations, such as\nincreased fibre size, strength, and endurance. For example, a weightlifter performing three\nsets of squats at 80% of their 1RM will build muscle size or strength, while incorporating\nlighter weights with higher repetitions will enhance muscular endurance. By targeting these\nthresholds, athletes enhance performance in sports requiring strength and muscular\nendurance.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q30(b)","year":2025,"n":30,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.4"],"img":"2025_Q30_b.png","w":1297,"h":110,"criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive justification of the use of heat and cold and\n   progressive mobilisation for the rehabilitation of a shoulder dislocation\n                                                                                        11-12\n • Presents a logical and cohesive response\n • Provides a range of relevant examples to support responses\n • Provides a sound justification of the use of heat and cold and\n   progressive mobilisation for the rehabilitation of a shoulder dislocation\n                                                                                        8-10\n • Presents a logical response\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Describes the use of heat and/or cold and progressive mobilisation for a\n   shoulder dislocation\n                                                                                         5-7\n • Provides a structured response\n • Provides examples\n • Outlines the use of heat and/or cold and/or progressive mobilisation for\n   injury                                                                                3-4\n • Provides example(s)\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1-2","sample":"Heat\n• Applying a heat pack increases blood flow to the injured area, delivering oxygen and\n  nutrients essential for tissue repair and reducing muscle tension.\n• Heat relaxes the surrounding muscles, improving the shoulder's flexibility and allowing the\n  athlete to perform mobilisation exercises with less pain.\n• Heat therapy can be used before rehabilitation sessions to ‘warm up' the joint, enhancing\n  performance in stretching and strengthening exercises.\n• Techniques such as moist heat (warm towels) or heat pads target deep tissues effectively,\n  reducing stiffness and improving comfort.\nCold\n• Applying an ice pack for 15-20 minutes multiple times a day reduces swelling and\n  inflammation by constricting blood vessels, which limits fluid build-up in the injured area.\n• Cold therapy numbs the area, providing temporary pain relief and making the shoulder\n  more comfortable for rest or rehabilitation exercises.\n• Reducing inflammation early limits secondary tissue damage, allowing for faster recovery\n  and improved long-term outcomes.\n• Cryotherapy can be applied with ice packs, ice baths, or cold compression wraps to\n  maintain consistent cooling and cover larger surface areas of the shoulder.\nProgressive mobilisation\n• Gradually increasing the shoulder's range of motion through controlled movements helps\n   prevent stiffness and muscle atrophy. For example, early-stage pendulum swings and\n   passive stretches gently restore joint mobility without placing stress on the injury.\n• Active movements such as shoulder flexion and abduction improve range of motion and\n   muscle activation as healing progresses, reducing the risk of long-term restrictions.\n• Resistance exercises using light bands or weights enhance muscle strength and joint\n   stability, which are essential for full recovery and injury prevention.\n• Mobilisation ensures scar tissue aligns properly with muscle fibres, avoiding adhesions\n   that limit flexibility and movement.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q31(a)(i)","year":2025,"n":31,"part":"(a)(i)","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.2.1"],"img":"2025_Q31_a_i.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Outlines the suitability of ONE training method for an athlete                         3\n • Demonstrates some understanding of ONE training method for an\n                                                                                          2\n   athlete\n • Provides some relevant information                                                     1","sample":"A 100 m sprinter participating in plyometrics such as box jumps or jumping lunges would\nimprove their power and speed, which is essential for explosive starts and maintaining speed\nduring a sprint. Plyometric exercises would be suitable as they enhance muscle elasticity and\nthe ability to produce rapid, forceful movements, improving acceleration and stride efficiency.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q31(a)(ii)","year":2025,"n":31,"part":"(a)(ii)","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports","Training and adaptations"],"sids":["FA2.3.1","FA2.2.2"],"img":"2025_Q31_a_ii.png","w":1364,"h":117,"criteria":"• Demonstrates a thorough understanding of how an athlete can ensure\n   their training procedures are safe with reference to ONE training method               5\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Demonstrates a sound understanding of how an athlete can ensure their\n   training procedures are safe with reference to ONE training method                     4\n • Provides examples\n • Describes how an athlete can ensure their training procedure(s) are safe\n                                                                                          3\n • Provides example(s)\n • Outlines safe training procedures                                                      2\n • Provides some relevant understanding                                                   1","sample":"Plyometrics is a training method a sprinter could use. To ensure training is safe, a sprinter\nshould focus on proper technique, such as maintaining body positioning and controlled\nmovements to prevent muscle strain. For example, a plyometric lunge should be performed\non soft, stable surfaces to reduce the impact on joints. A thorough warm-up before each\nsession prepares muscles and joints for high-intensity activity and a cool down afterward aids\nrecovery. Gradually increasing the intensity and volume of training ensures the body can\nadapt without the risk of overuse injuries.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q31(b)","year":2025,"n":31,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.1"],"img":"2025_Q31_b.png","w":1297,"h":110,"criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive justification of THREE elements a coach\n   needs to consider when designing a training session for a sport\n                                                                                        11-12\n • Presents a logical and cohesive response\n • Provides a range of relevant examples to support responses\n • Provides a sound justification of THREE elements a coach needs to\n   consider when designing a training session for a sport\n                                                                                        8-10\n • Presents a logical response\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Describes THREE elements a coach needs to consider when designing\n   a training session for a sport\n                                                                                         5-7\n • Provides a structured response\n • Provides examples\n • Outlines elements a coach needs to consider when designing a training\n   session                                                                               3-4\n • Provides example(s)\n • Provides some relevant information                                                    1-2","sample":"Sport = Netball\nWarm-up\n• A well-structured warm-up prepares the body for physical activity, reduces the risk of\n  injury, and improves performance. For under-14 beginners, dynamic movements like\n  jogging, high knees and netball-specific drills (eg side-stepping and pivoting) increase\n  blood flow and loosen muscles while introducing movement patterns used in netball.\nPractice\n• Practice should follow a progression model, starting with basic isolated drills and\n  advancing to more complex, game-like situations. For example, a basic catching drill can\n  evolve into dynamic passing drills with defenders, encouraging skill transfer to real-game\n  scenarios. This builds decision-making and adaptability while reinforcing core techniques.\nConditioning\n• Combining conditioning with netball-specific skills, such as short sprints, defensive\n  shadowing and agility ladder drills, improves fitness while reinforcing the movements\n  required in netball (eg dodging and landing). This helps players build fitness relevant to\n  game demands without overloading young bodies.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"2025-Q32(b)","year":2025,"n":32,"part":"(b)","marks":12,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians","The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.1.2","FA1.1.1","FA1.2.1"],"img":"2025_Q32_b.png","w":1297,"h":110,"criteria":"• Provides well-informed judgements of the extent to which THREE factors\n   that create health inequities can affect ONE population group in Australia\n                                                                                        11-12\n • Presents a logical and cohesive response\n • Provides a range of relevant examples to support responses\n • Provides sound judgements of the extent to which THREE factors that\n   create health inequities can affect ONE population group in Australia\n                                                                                        8-10\n • Presents a logical response\n • Provides relevant examples\n • Describes how factors that create health inequities can affect ONE\n   population group in Australia                                                         5-7\n • Provides examples\n • Outlines a factor(s) that create health inequities for a population group(s)\n   in Australia                                                                          3-4\n • Provides example(s)\n • Provides relevant information                                                         1-2","sample":"Factors\n• Daily living conditions\n• Quality of early years of life\n• Access to services and transport\n• Socioeconomic factors\n• Social attributes, eg social exclusion, discrimination\n• Government policies and priorities, eg health, economic, social.\n\nPopulation Groups\n• Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples\n• Homeless\n• People living with HIV/AIDS\n• Incarcerated\n• Aged\n• Culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds\n• Unemployed\n• Geographically remote populations\n• People with disabilities.","src":"hsc"},{"id":"NEW-SA-001","year":null,"n":1,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.1"],"stem":"Outline how wearable technology can be used to measure and monitor an individual's health.","criteria":"• Outlines clearly how wearable technology measures and monitors health, identifying the data collected and how it is used — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the use of wearable technology to measure or monitor health — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Wearable devices such as smartwatches and continuous glucose monitors measure health data in real time, including heart rate, sleep duration, steps and blood glucose levels. Because the measurement is continuous rather than a single reading taken at a medical appointment, the individual and their health professional can monitor trends over weeks and months. For example, a person with type 2 diabetes using a continuous glucose monitor can see how particular meals and exercise change their blood glucose, and adjust their behaviour before levels become dangerous.","expl":"Tests the 'measuring' and 'monitoring' examples. A top-band response names specific data being measured AND explains the monitoring function — tracking change over time and acting on it. A mid-band response lists devices or data types only ('a smartwatch counts steps') without connecting measurement to any health outcome or decision. Markers should accept any credible Australian example, including continuous glucose monitors, home blood pressure monitors and heart-rhythm alerts, and should not require a named brand.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the relationship between technology and health","aim":"Measuring and monitoring"},{"id":"NEW-SA-002","year":null,"n":2,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.1"],"stem":"Explain how technologies that support early diagnosis can improve the health of Australians. Use ONE Australian example to support your answer.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of how early diagnosis technology improves health, with a relevant and detailed Australian example — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of how early diagnosis technology improves health, with a relevant Australian example — 4\n• Provides some understanding of how early diagnosis technology improves health, with an example — 3\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of early diagnosis technology — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Technology that allows a disease to be found before symptoms appear improves health because treatment given at an early stage is more effective, less invasive and more likely to result in survival. BreastScreen Australia uses digital mammography to screen women aged 50–74 every two years, free of charge. A mammogram can detect a tumour that is only a few millimetres across and cannot be felt, at a point when it is still localised. Women whose breast cancer is detected through screening have a much higher five-year relative survival rate than women diagnosed after the cancer has spread, and they are more likely to be treated with breast-conserving surgery rather than a mastectomy and extended chemotherapy. This also reduces the burden on the healthcare system, because treating early-stage disease costs less than treating advanced disease and requires fewer hospital admissions.","expl":"Targets the 'early diagnosis' example. The distinction between bands is causal reasoning, not example recall: a top-band answer traces the chain from what the technology detects, to the stage at which it is detected, to the improved outcome (survival, less invasive treatment, lower system cost). A mid-band answer describes BreastScreen or bowel screening accurately but stops at 'it finds cancer early, which is good'. Accept BreastScreen Australia, the National Bowel Cancer Screening Program, the National Cervical Screening Program (HPV testing), newborn bloodspot screening or diagnostic imaging. Do not require or reward precise participation or survival statistics.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the relationship between technology and health","aim":"Early diagnosis (screening and diagnostic technology)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-003","year":null,"n":3,"part":"","marks":6,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.1"],"stem":"Assess the impact of precision surgery on individuals and on Australia's healthcare system.","criteria":"• Provides a well-informed judgement of the impact of precision surgery on individuals and on the healthcare system, supported by relevant and detailed examples — 6\n• Provides a sound judgement of the impact of precision surgery on individuals and/or the healthcare system, supported by relevant examples — 4–5\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of precision surgery and its effects, with example(s) — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Precision surgery, including robot-assisted and minimally invasive keyhole procedures, has a strongly positive impact on the individual. Robotic systems allow a surgeon to operate through very small incisions with magnified vision and instruments that filter hand tremor, so in a procedure such as a radical prostatectomy there is less blood loss, less post-operative pain, a lower risk of infection and a shorter hospital stay. The patient returns to work and normal function sooner, improving their physical and mental health and reducing lost income. For the healthcare system the impact is mixed. Shorter lengths of stay free up hospital beds and may reduce readmissions, but the capital cost of a robotic system, its consumables and the specialised training required are very high. Because of this cost, precision surgery is concentrated in large metropolitan hospitals and private hospitals, so people in rural and remote areas, and those without private health insurance, are less likely to access it. Overall, precision surgery clearly improves outcomes for those who receive it, but on balance its high cost means it currently widens the gap in access between population groups rather than improving the health of all Australians equally.","expl":"Assess requires a judgement of value or impact, not a list. A top-band response reaches a defensible overall verdict and sustains it across both parts of the question — individual benefit versus system cost and equity — with at least one concrete procedure. A mid-band response gives accurate benefits and limitations side by side but never weighs them or reaches a conclusion, or addresses only the individual. Markers should credit equity of access, private/public divide and workforce training as legitimate system-level points.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the relationship between technology and health","aim":"Precision surgery"},{"id":"NEW-SA-004","year":null,"n":4,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.1"],"stem":"Discuss the relationship between technology and health in Australia. In your answer, refer to BOTH the benefits and the limitations of health technology.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough discussion of the relationship between technology and health, addressing both benefits and limitations and drawing them together into a coherent judgement — 8\n• Provides relevant and detailed examples\n• Provides a sound discussion of the relationship between technology and health, addressing both benefits and limitations — 6–7\n• Provides relevant examples\n• Provides a sound understanding of ways technology affects health, with limited treatment of limitations — 4–5\n• Provides example(s)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of technology in health care — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Technology has changed almost every stage of health care in Australia, but its relationship with health is not simply positive. Technology allows health to be measured and monitored continuously: wearable devices and continuous glucose monitors give individuals real-time data about their own bodies, supporting self-management of chronic conditions and prompting earlier help-seeking when something changes. It also supports early diagnosis. Digital mammography through BreastScreen Australia and immunochemical testing through the National Bowel Cancer Screening Program detect cancers in people with no symptoms, when survival is far higher, while AI-assisted reading of CT and MRI images can prioritise urgent findings such as stroke so a specialist reviews them sooner. In treatment, precision surgery such as robot-assisted keyhole procedures reduces blood loss, pain and length of stay, and telehealth funded through the MBS lets a patient in a remote town consult a GP or specialist without travelling hundreds of kilometres.\n\nHowever, each of these benefits carries a cost. Health technology is expensive, and rising expenditure on new devices, imaging and treatments is one of the main pressures on Australia's health budget, forcing decisions about what governments will fund through Medicare, the MBS and the PBS. Better detection can also cause harm through over-diagnosis: thyroid cancer incidence has risen steeply since neck ultrasound became widely available, while mortality has barely changed, meaning many people are treated for tumours that would never have made them ill. Access is also unequal. Robotic surgery is concentrated in metropolitan and private hospitals, and telehealth and health apps assume a device, digital literacy and reliable internet — resources that older Australians, people on low incomes and people in rural and remote communities are least likely to have. This digital divide means technology can widen health inequities at the same time as it raises the average standard of care.\n\nOverall, technology has substantially improved the ability of the healthcare system to measure, detect and treat disease, and has improved outcomes for individuals who can access it. Its benefits are not distributed evenly, though, so the relationship between technology and health depends on whether governments fund and locate these technologies in ways that reach the populations with the greatest need.","expl":"This is the anchor extended-response for the content point, and it uses the syllabus's own verb. A top-band response covers all four teaching-support examples (measuring, monitoring, early diagnosis, precision surgery) or a strong subset, and then genuinely discusses — naming at least two distinct limitations from cost, over-diagnosis, equity of access and the digital divide — before drawing them together in a judgement about the relationship as a whole. A mid-band response is typically a well-informed list of technologies with benefits attached and only a token final sentence about cost or access; the limitations are asserted rather than explained. A response that treats technology as uniformly beneficial cannot reach the top band, since the content point requires the relationship to be discussed, not celebrated. Australian institutions (Medicare, the MBS, the PBS, BreastScreen Australia) should be spelled and used correctly; precise current statistics are not required and should not be rewarded over reasoning about trends.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the relationship between technology and health","aim":"The overall technology–health relationship, including cost, over-diagnosis, equity and the digital divide"},{"id":"NEW-SA-005","year":null,"n":5,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.3"],"stem":"Outline Australia's ranking in relation to other OECD countries on TWO measures of health status.","criteria":"• Outlines Australia's ranking relative to other OECD countries on TWO measures of health status, identifying the direction of each ranking — 3\n• Outlines Australia's ranking relative to other OECD countries on ONE measure of health status, OR identifies two measures with limited reference to ranking — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Australia ranks among the highest group of OECD countries for life expectancy at birth, at around 83 years, above the OECD average and above countries such as the United Kingdom and the United States, although still slightly below Japan. In contrast, Australia ranks among the least favourable OECD countries for adult obesity, with around one in three adults obese, far higher than Japan and Korea, where prevalence is below 10%.","expl":"Targets the first sub-bullet directly. A 3-mark response names two distinct measures and states clearly where Australia sits (high, low, above or below the OECD average), ideally with a named comparator country. A 2-mark response typically names two indicators but only characterises one ranking, or describes Australian data without any OECD comparison. Approximate figures are credited but not required — no mark should depend on a precise current statistic.","src":"new","point":"Compare the health status of Australia with that of other OECD countries","aim":"where does Australia rank in relation to other OECD countries?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-006","year":null,"n":6,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.3"],"stem":"Explain why Australia ranks favourably against other OECD countries on some measures of health status but unfavourably on others.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive explanation of the reasons for Australia's contrasting rankings, making clear cause-and-effect links between named factors and named measures, supported by OECD comparison — 5\n• Provides a clear explanation of reasons for Australia's ranking on both a favourable and an unfavourable measure — 4\n• Demonstrates a sound understanding of reasons for Australia's ranking on one or both measures — 3\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of Australia's health status relative to other OECD countries — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Australia ranks near the top of the OECD for life expectancy and below the OECD average for infant mortality and avoidable mortality. This is largely because universal access to care through Medicare and subsidised medicines through the PBS mean cost is less of a barrier to timely treatment than it is in the United States, and because sustained public health action — tobacco control, immunisation, road safety and skin cancer campaigns — has reduced preventable deaths. Australia's daily smoking rate is now among the lowest in the OECD as a direct result of excise increases, plain packaging and graphic health warnings.\n\nHowever, Australia ranks unfavourably for adult obesity, harmful alcohol use and melanoma incidence. Rising obesity reflects an obesogenic environment of energy-dense, heavily marketed food and sedentary work and transport, and unlike the United Kingdom, Australia has not applied a levy on sugar-sweetened beverages. Melanoma incidence is the highest in the world because of high UV exposure, an outdoor lifestyle and a largely fair-skinned population. Australia's favourable national averages also conceal significant inequities, particularly the life expectancy gap for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples.","expl":"Covers sub-bullets two and three. Top band requires genuine causal explanation on both sides of the ledger — why Australia does well AND why it does not — with the factor (universal health care, tobacco control, food environment, UV exposure) linked to the specific indicator. Mid-band responses typically list rankings and factors without connecting a particular factor to a particular measure, or explain only the favourable side. Reference to inequities masked by national averages is a legitimate route to the top band but is not required on its own.","src":"new","point":"Compare the health status of Australia with that of other OECD countries","aim":"why might this be the case?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-007","year":null,"n":7,"part":"","marks":6,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.3"],"stem":"Analyse the data in the table to compare the health status of Australia with that of the other countries shown.","criteria":"• Analyses the data comprehensively, drawing out relationships between indicators and providing well-reasoned explanations for Australia's position relative to the other countries — 6\n• Analyses the data, comparing Australia with the other countries across several indicators with some explanation of the differences — 5\n• Compares Australia with other countries using the data, with limited explanation — 4\n• Describes features of the data relating to Australia and at least one other country — 3\n• Identifies some relevant features of the data — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"The data shows Australia performing above the OECD average on most indicators. Life expectancy of about 83 years exceeds the OECD average and the United Kingdom, and is well above the United States, though marginally below Japan. Infant mortality of about 3 per 1000 live births is also better than the OECD average, indicating effective maternal and child health services and universal access through Medicare.\n\nThe clear exception is adult obesity. At about 31%, Australia is above the OECD average and far above Japan, whose figure of 4% coincides with the highest life expectancy in the table. This suggests dietary patterns and body weight contribute strongly to a country's overall health status, and identifies obesity as Australia's major comparative weakness.\n\nThe United States illustrates that expenditure alone does not determine health status: it spends 17% of GDP, far more than Australia's 10%, yet records the lowest life expectancy, the highest infant mortality and the highest obesity. This is consistent with the absence of universal coverage delaying access to care and with very high obesity driving avoidable deaths.\n\nAustralia's daily smoking rate of about 10% is the lowest in the table and well below the OECD average, reflecting decades of tobacco control. Overall, the data indicates Australia achieves strong outcomes at a moderate level of spending, but that its high obesity prevalence is the indicator most likely to limit future gains.","expl":"Targets the first and third sub-bullets through stimulus interpretation. A top-band response moves beyond restating rows: it identifies the pattern that expenditure and outcomes are not aligned (United States), sets Australia's obesity figure against its otherwise favourable position, and offers reasons drawn from health systems and determinants. A mid-band response compares numbers accurately but treats each indicator in isolation and offers little or no explanation. Responses that merely transcribe the table sit at 2–3 marks. Markers should not penalise students for not quoting the figures exactly.","src":"new","point":"Compare the health status of Australia with that of other OECD countries","aim":"where does Australia rank in relation to other OECD countries? / why do some countries rank higher or lower than Australia?","stim":"<div class=\"stimbox\"><table class=\"stimtable\"><caption>Selected OECD health indicators (approximate recent values)</caption><tr><th>Country</th><th>Life expectancy (years)</th><th>Infant mortality (per 1000)</th><th>Adult obesity (%)</th><th>Daily smoking (%)</th><th>Health expenditure (% of GDP)</th></tr><tr><th>Japan</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">84</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11%</td></tr><tr><th>Australia</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">83</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">31%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">10%</td></tr><tr><th>United Kingdom</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">81</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">28%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">13%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11%</td></tr><tr><th>United States</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">77</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">5</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">42%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">17%</td></tr><tr><th>OECD average</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">81</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">25%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">15%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">9%</td></tr></table></div>"},{"id":"NEW-SA-008","year":null,"n":8,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["Health status of Australians"],"sids":["FA1.1.3"],"stem":"Assess what Australia could learn from other OECD countries in order to improve its health status.","criteria":"• Makes a well-supported judgement about what Australia could learn from other OECD countries, sustained across a range of approaches, with clear links to specific measures of Australia's health status and consideration of the limitations of transferring approaches to the Australian context — 7–8\n• Makes a judgement about approaches used in other OECD countries and their value for Australia, with links to Australia's health status — 5–6\n• Describes approaches used in other OECD countries and their possible application in Australia, with limited judgement — 3–4\n• Provides some relevant information about health in other OECD countries — 1–2","sample":"Australia already ranks near the top of the OECD for life expectancy and near the bottom for daily smoking, so the greatest gains available are in the areas where it ranks poorly — adult obesity, harmful alcohol use and the inequities concealed by national averages.\n\nThe most valuable lesson comes from countries that have acted on the food environment. The United Kingdom's levy on sugar-sweetened beverages, paired with reformulation targets, reduced the sugar content of drinks without relying on individual willpower, and Nordic countries such as Norway and Sweden use simple front-of-pack labelling to guide purchasing. Given that around one in three Australian adults is obese, compared with under 10% in Japan and Korea, structural food policy of this kind would target Australia's weakest indicator far more effectively than further education campaigns. Japan and Korea also demonstrate the protective value of traditional dietary patterns lower in energy density, although diet is culturally embedded and cannot simply be legislated into an Australian population, which limits how directly this can be transferred.\n\nAustralia can also learn from health system design. Countries such as Norway and the Netherlands invest more heavily in primary and preventive care as a share of health expenditure, whereas Australian spending remains weighted towards hospital treatment. Shifting investment towards prevention would address the chronic conditions driving avoidable mortality. Conversely, the United States is an instructive negative example: the highest expenditure in the OECD produces the lowest life expectancy of comparable nations because coverage is not universal, which justifies protecting and strengthening Medicare and the PBS rather than moving towards private financing.\n\nHowever, the value of these lessons is qualified. Australia's geography means rural and remote populations face access barriers that densely populated countries such as Japan and the Netherlands do not, and no OECD comparator shares Australia's history of colonisation and its consequences for the health of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples. Approaches drawn from overseas must therefore be adapted, and improvement in Australia's OECD ranking depends at least as much on closing internal inequities as on adopting foreign policy.\n\nOverall, Australia has most to gain from adopting the structural food and prevention policies of the United Kingdom and the Nordic countries, while recognising that these must be implemented alongside community-controlled and locally adapted responses to Australia's specific inequities.","expl":"Targets the fourth sub-bullet, and requires the comparative reasoning of the first three to support the judgement. A top-band response is defined by judgement plus qualification: it identifies which lessons matter most and why, ties them to a named Australian weakness rather than to health in general, names real OECD comparators and their actual policies, and acknowledges limits on transferability (geography, culture, colonisation, inequity). A mid-band response describes two or three overseas approaches and asserts they would help Australia, without weighing their relative value or their limitations. Responses that list overseas health systems with no evaluative link to Australia's health status cannot exceed 4 marks.","src":"new","point":"Compare the health status of Australia with that of other OECD countries","aim":"what can we learn from other countries that may be applied to the Australian context?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-009","year":null,"n":9,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.2"],"stem":"Outline how assistive technology and 3D-printed prosthetics can improve health outcomes for Australians living with disability.","criteria":"• Sketches in general terms how BOTH assistive technology and 3D-printed prosthetics improve health outcomes for people with disability\n• Supports with relevant example(s) — 3 marks\n• Sketches in general terms how ONE of these technologies improves health outcomes for people with disability, OR provides limited detail about both — 2 marks\n• Provides some relevant information about assistive technology or 3D-printed prosthetics — 1 mark","sample":"Assistive technology includes equipment and software that helps a person with disability carry out everyday tasks more independently, such as a powered wheelchair, screen-reading software or a speech-generating communication device funded through an NDIS plan. By improving mobility and communication, these technologies increase participation in education, work and community life, which supports mental health and social wellbeing as well as physical function.\n\n3D-printed prosthetics allow a limb to be custom-designed and printed at much lower cost and much faster than a traditionally manufactured prosthesis. This is particularly useful for children, who outgrow prostheses quickly, and for people in rural areas where a device can be printed locally rather than fitted in a capital city. Better fit reduces skin breakdown and pain, and improved function supports independence and quality of life.","expl":"This targets the 'assistive technology' Example directly and extends it to 3D printing as a new manufacturing technology. A top response names a specific technology of each type and links it to a health outcome (independence, participation, function, wellbeing) rather than simply describing the device. A mid-band response lists devices without connecting them to health outcomes, or covers only one of the two technologies. Australian anchoring through the NDIS is expected but a response is not penalised for using another accurate Australian context.","src":"new","point":"Investigate new technologies and treatments in the healthcare system","aim":"Assistive technology (NESA Example) and 3D-printed prosthetics"},{"id":"NEW-SA-010","year":null,"n":10,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.2"],"stem":"Explain how a new medicine becomes both available and affordable to Australians.","criteria":"• Provides a clear cause-and-effect account of the regulatory step (TGA registration for safety, quality and efficacy) AND the subsidy step (PBAC assessment of clinical and cost-effectiveness leading to PBS listing)\n• Explains why each step matters for access and affordability, supported with relevant example(s) — 4 marks\n• Sketches in general terms the approval and/or subsidy processes, with limited explanation of why they affect availability and affordability — 2–3 marks\n• Provides some relevant information about the TGA, the PBS or the cost of medicines — 1 mark","sample":"Before a new medicine can be supplied in Australia, the sponsor must submit evidence to the Therapeutic Goods Administration, which evaluates the medicine for safety, quality and efficacy and, if satisfied, enters it on the Australian Register of Therapeutic Goods. This step makes the medicine legally available, but at this point the patient would pay the full price, which for a new targeted cancer therapy can be many thousands of dollars per course. This means availability alone does not create access.\n\nTo make the medicine affordable, the sponsor applies to have it listed on the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme. The Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee assesses whether the medicine is clinically effective compared with existing treatments and whether it is cost-effective at the price sought, and recommends listing to the Minister. Once listed, the Commonwealth pays most of the cost and the patient pays only the PBS co-payment, with a lower rate for concession card holders and further protection through the safety net. A similar role is played by the Medical Services Advisory Committee, which advises on whether a new procedure or technology, such as a new diagnostic test, should be funded through the Medicare Benefits Schedule.\n\nBecause listing depends on a judgement about cost-effectiveness, medicines with a high price and modest benefit may be registered but not subsidised, so some Australians can obtain them only if they can pay privately. This is the point at which the system's cost-effectiveness controls create an equity tension.","expl":"This is the funding and approval sub-strand, which students routinely blur. A top-band response clearly separates the two gates — legal supply (TGA) versus public subsidy (PBAC/PBS) — explains the consequence of each for the individual's out-of-pocket cost, and preferably notes the co-payment and safety net or the MSAC/MBS pathway for procedures. A mid-band response names the TGA and the PBS but treats them as one process, or describes them without explaining the effect on affordability. Naming MSAC is a discriminator at the top of the range, not a requirement.","src":"new","point":"Investigate new technologies and treatments in the healthcare system","aim":"How new treatments get approved and funded in Australia — TGA, PBAC/PBS, MSAC/MBS"},{"id":"NEW-SA-011","year":null,"n":11,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.2"],"stem":"Compare the impact of health apps and artificial intelligence on the delivery of health care in Australia.","criteria":"• Provides a clear comparison of health apps and artificial intelligence, identifying both similarities and differences in their impact on healthcare delivery\n• Draws out points of comparison such as who uses the technology, where it acts in the system, and the benefits and limitations of each\n• Supports with relevant Australian examples — 5 marks\n• Provides characteristics and features of the impact of health apps and artificial intelligence, with some comparison — 3–4 marks\n• Provides some relevant information about health apps and/or artificial intelligence — 1–2 marks","sample":"Health apps and artificial intelligence are similar in that both are digital technologies that shift health care towards earlier detection and more efficient use of clinician time, and both raise the same concerns about data privacy and about widening the gap between those who can and cannot access digital services.\n\nThey differ in who uses them and where they act. Health apps are used by the individual — for example, apps that track physical activity, support smoking cessation, monitor blood glucose or provide mental health support. Their main impact is on prevention and self-management, moving care out of hospitals and into everyday life, which reduces demand on primary and acute services. Their limitation is that quality is variable, only some apps are regulated by the TGA as software-based medical devices, and benefit depends on the user's digital literacy, device and internet access.\n\nArtificial intelligence, by contrast, is mostly used by health professionals and services. It analyses large volumes of data to support diagnosis and planning — for example, algorithms that screen retinal images for diabetic retinopathy or assist radiologists in reading scans, and predictive models used to plan hospital demand. Its impact is on the accuracy and speed of clinical decision-making rather than on individual behaviour. Its limitations are different again: an algorithm reflects the data it was trained on, so it can be less accurate for under-represented groups, and accountability for an incorrect result must remain with the clinician.\n\nOverall, health apps have the broader reach across the population but shallower clinical impact, while artificial intelligence has a narrower point of application but a deeper effect on the quality of clinical care.","expl":"'Compare' requires similarities and differences, not two separate descriptions — that is the single biggest discriminator here. A top-band response establishes explicit points of comparison (user, point of impact in the system, type of benefit, type of limitation) and sustains them across both technologies with Australian examples. A mid-band response gives an accurate account of each technology but leaves the comparison implicit, or offers only one point of similarity. Weak responses list app names without linking them to healthcare delivery.","src":"new","point":"Investigate new technologies and treatments in the healthcare system","aim":"Health apps and artificial intelligence (NESA Examples)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-012","year":null,"n":12,"part":"","marks":7,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.2"],"stem":"Assess the impact of new technologies and treatments on equity of access to health care in Australia. Use the stimulus and your own examples in your answer.","criteria":"• Makes a well-supported judgement about the extent to which new technologies and treatments improve or undermine equity of access\n• Analyses both the benefits of new technologies and treatments and the barriers of cost, geography and digital access, drawing explicitly on the stimulus\n• Integrates a range of relevant examples, including the role of approval and funding processes (TGA, PBAC/PBS, MSAC)\n• Communicates ideas using relevant health terminology in a well-structured response — 6–7 marks\n• Provides characteristics and features of the impact of new technologies and treatments on equity of access\n• Refers to the stimulus and provides relevant examples, with a judgement that may be limited or asserted rather than supported — 4–5 marks\n• Sketches in general terms the benefits and/or limitations of new technologies and treatments in health care\n• May refer to the stimulus — 2–3 marks\n• Provides some relevant information about new technologies or treatments — 1 mark","sample":"New technologies and treatments have clearly improved health outcomes for many Australians, but their benefits are not distributed evenly, and in some cases they widen the very inequities the healthcare system is meant to reduce.\n\nThe positive case is strong. Precision and robotic surgery, as in Extract 1, is less invasive and shortens recovery, freeing hospital beds and returning people to work sooner. Genomic testing allows targeted cancer therapies to be given to the patients whose tumours carry the relevant variant, improving outcomes and avoiding futile treatment. mRNA platforms allowed vaccines to be developed and rolled out at unprecedented speed. Telehealth, assistive technology funded through the NDIS, and 3D-printed prosthetics that can be produced locally and cheaply all extend services to groups that were previously poorly served, particularly people in rural and remote areas and people with disability. My Health Record allows a patient's information to travel with them between providers.\n\nThe equity problem is that access to these advances depends on geography, income and digital capability. Extract 1 shows the geographic dimension: a high-cost robotic system is installed in a metropolitan hospital, so regional patients bear travel and accommodation costs and time away from work to obtain the same benefit — a clear breach of equity of access even though the technology itself is publicly provided. Health apps and artificial intelligence show the digital dimension: the groups with the greatest need, including lower socioeconomic and remote populations, are the least likely to have reliable connectivity, a suitable device or the health literacy to benefit, so a universally available app can still produce unequal gains.\n\nExtract 2 shows the cost dimension and the tension the system must manage. The gene therapy is registered by the TGA, so it is legally available, but at over $1 million per patient it is unaffordable without subsidy. The PBAC's role is to protect the sustainability of the PBS by requiring evidence of clinical and cost-effectiveness, so it has deferred its recommendation. This is defensible stewardship of a finite health budget — every dollar spent on a therapy of uncertain benefit is a dollar unavailable for prevention or primary care that would benefit far more people. But while the decision is deferred, access depends on the ability to pay privately or on a compassionate access program, which is inequitable for the small number of affected families.\n\nOn balance, new technologies and treatments have improved the quality and efficiency of Australian health care, and public funding mechanisms such as the PBS, the MBS and the NDIS do a great deal to convert innovation into equitable access. However, because these technologies are typically expensive and are introduced first in well-resourced metropolitan settings, their benefits reach advantaged groups first. Their impact on equity is therefore positive in the long term but unequal in the short term, and the extent of that inequity depends directly on how quickly and how fairly funding and distribution decisions are made.","expl":"This is the synthesis question for the content point and deliberately forces the equity–cost-effectiveness tension. A top-band response makes and sustains a judgement — typically that the impact is positive overall but unevenly distributed, or that innovation improves outcomes while creating new access barriers — and supports it with both stimulus extracts plus independent examples drawn from across the content point (health apps, AI, assistive technology, genomics, mRNA, 3D printing) and the funding architecture. What separates top from mid band is (i) an explicit, supported judgement rather than a list of pros and cons, and (ii) recognition that the PBAC's cost-effectiveness role is a legitimate protection of the wider population's access, not simply a barrier. Mid-band responses describe benefits and barriers accurately and quote the stimulus but conclude with an unsupported assertion such as 'therefore technology is good for equity'.","src":"new","point":"Investigate new technologies and treatments in the healthcare system","aim":"Equity and cost-effectiveness tension across new technologies and treatments","stim":"<div class=\"stimbox extract\"><div class=\"stimlabel\">STIMULUS</div><p>Extract 1: ‘A robotic surgical system has been installed at a metropolitan hospital. Patients treated with the system go home on average two days earlier, but the cost per procedure is around double that of conventional surgery, and patients from regional areas must travel to access it.’</p><p>Extract 2: ‘A new gene therapy for a rare inherited condition has been registered by the TGA. The listed price is over $1 million per patient. The sponsor has applied for PBS listing; the PBAC has deferred its recommendation pending further evidence of long-term benefit.’</p></div>"},{"id":"NEW-SA-013","year":null,"n":13,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.3"],"stem":"Outline TWO digital health services available in Australia and identify how each supports the management of a person's health information.","criteria":"• Outlines TWO digital health services AND clearly identifies how each supports the electronic management of a person's health information — 3\n• Outlines TWO digital health services with limited link to the management of health information, OR outlines ONE service and its link clearly — 2\n• Identifies a digital health service — 1","sample":"My Health Record is a national online summary of a person's key health information, such as shared health summaries, discharge summaries, pathology and imaging reports and medicines. It allows information entered by one provider to be viewed by another provider treating the same person, supporting continuity of care. Electronic prescriptions allow a prescriber to send a prescription as a token to a patient's phone or email, which the pharmacy scans and dispenses. The prescription and dispensing record are held electronically, reducing lost scripts and giving a more accurate record of the medicines a person is taking.","expl":"Tests the 'what services exist?' sub-bullet. A top response names two genuinely distinct national services (from My Health Record, electronic prescriptions, telehealth MBS items, e-referrals, secure messaging) and states the information-management function of each. Mid-band responses name services but describe only their convenience ('you don't have to go to the doctor') without connecting them to the electronic management of health information.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the impact of digital health on the healthcare system","aim":"what services exist?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-014","year":null,"n":14,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.3"],"stem":"Explain how patient-controlled access and the opt-out model of My Health Record attempt to balance the benefits of sharing health information with individuals' concerns about privacy.","criteria":"• Provides why and/or how patient-controlled access and the opt-out model respond to privacy concerns AND relates this to the benefits of sharing health information — 4\n• Provides some reasons why the controls respond to privacy concerns, with limited reference to the benefits of sharing — 3\n• Sketches in general terms the features of My Health Record and/or privacy concerns — 2\n• Provides relevant information about digital health or privacy — 1","sample":"Sharing health information electronically improves care because a treating clinician who has never met a patient can see their medicines, allergies and recent test results, which reduces duplicated tests and prescribing errors. However, the same feature creates concern about who can see sensitive information and how securely it is held. The opt-out model was chosen so that near-universal participation would be achieved, because a record is only useful if most people have one, while still preserving choice — anyone could decline a record before the deadline and can cancel it at any time. Patient-controlled access then limits the privacy risk within the system: individuals can set access codes so only nominated providers can view their record, remove or hide documents, and receive alerts when their record is accessed. Together these features attempt to keep the population-level benefit of connected information while leaving control of sensitive information with the individual.","expl":"Targets the challenges/opportunities sub-bullet through the privacy lens. A top-band response explains the trade-off in both directions — why opt-out was used (system value depends on scale) and how patient controls mitigate the resulting risk. Mid-band responses list the privacy controls accurately but treat privacy as a one-sided problem, never explaining why sharing was worth pursuing.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the impact of digital health on the healthcare system","aim":"what challenges and opportunities does digital health provide for individuals and organisations? — privacy, patient-controlled access and opt-out"},{"id":"NEW-SA-015","year":null,"n":15,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.3"],"stem":"Compare the challenges digital health presents for individuals with the challenges it presents for healthcare organisations.","criteria":"• Clearly shows similarities and differences between the challenges faced by individuals and those faced by healthcare organisations — 5\n• Supports with relevant examples of digital health services\n• Provides characteristics and features of challenges for both individuals and organisations, with some comparison — 4\n• Describes challenges for individuals and/or organisations with limited comparison — 2–3\n• Provides relevant information about digital health — 1","sample":"For individuals, the main challenges are access and understanding. Older Australians, people in rural and remote areas with poor connectivity, and people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds may lack the devices, internet, digital literacy or English-language health literacy to register for My Health Record, use a telehealth appointment or manage an electronic prescription token. Individuals also carry the concern that sensitive information could be accessed inappropriately or breached.\n\nFor organisations, the challenges are operational. General practices and hospitals must fund and maintain compliant software, train staff and absorb the extra clinician workload of uploading summaries and reconciling information, often during consultations that are not funded for that time. Organisations also face poor interoperability, where a hospital system cannot exchange information reliably with a practice system, and variable data quality when uploads are incomplete or duplicated.\n\nBoth groups face the same underlying issue from different sides: the value of digital health depends on other people participating. A patient's record is of little use if providers do not upload to it, and a practice's investment in secure messaging is wasted if the hospital at the other end does not use a compatible system. Privacy and cyber-security is also a shared challenge, though for individuals it is a risk to personal information while for organisations it is a legal and reputational obligation.","expl":"A top-band answer must actually compare — explicit similarities and differences, not two separate lists. The strongest responses identify the shared 'network effect' problem (participation by others determines the benefit) and distinguish access/literacy challenges for individuals from workload, cost and interoperability challenges for organisations. Mid-band responses describe both groups accurately but leave the comparison implicit.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the impact of digital health on the healthcare system","aim":"what challenges and opportunities does digital health provide for individuals and organisations?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-016","year":null,"n":16,"part":"","marks":6,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.3"],"stem":"Assess the extent to which digital health has been successful in connecting health information across the Australian healthcare system.","criteria":"• Makes a clear judgement about the extent of success in connecting health information, based on both achievements and limitations — 6\n• Supports with relevant examples of digital health services\n• Provides points for and against the success of digital health in connecting health information, with a judgement — 4–5\n• Supports with example(s)\n• Provides characteristics and features of digital health services and/or their limitations — 2–3\n• Provides relevant information about digital health — 1","sample":"Digital health has been substantially but unevenly successful in connecting health information. The clearest successes are where a service is standardised and used at the point of care. Electronic prescriptions now move prescribing and dispensing information between prescriber, patient and pharmacy without paper, and telehealth items in the Medicare Benefits Schedule have normalised consultations at a distance so that a patient's usual doctor can review them without travel. The opt-out model means almost every Australian has a My Health Record, so information such as shared health summaries, discharge summaries, pathology and imaging reports and dispensed medicines can, in principle, follow a patient between providers. This is genuinely valuable in emergency departments and after-hours care, where a clinician who has never met the patient can check allergies and current medicines.\n\nHowever, connection is only as good as what is actually shared. Provider upload has been inconsistent, so many records hold little clinical information, and data quality varies where documents are duplicated, out of date or uploaded in formats that are hard to use. Interoperability between hospital and primary care software remains incomplete, so secure messaging and e-referrals are still not seamless between all providers, and some information continues to move by fax, post or phone. Clinician workload and the time cost of uploading during a consultation reduce uptake further, and the digital divide means older, rural and CALD Australians are less able to engage with the parts of the system that depend on the individual.\n\nOn balance, digital health has succeeded in creating national infrastructure and near-universal registration, and has clearly improved information flow for medicines and for remote consultations, but it has not yet delivered a fully connected system in which any treating clinician reliably sees a complete and current picture of a patient's care.","expl":"The command term requires a judgement of extent, not a balanced list. Top-band responses commit to a position ('substantially but unevenly'), distinguish the parts of the system that are well connected (medicines, telehealth) from those that are not (hospital–primary care interoperability, upload rates), and use accurate Australian service names. Mid-band responses give valid strengths and weaknesses but end without a judgement, or judge success only by registration numbers.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the impact of digital health on the healthcare system","aim":"to what extent has digital health been successful in connecting health information?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-017","year":null,"n":17,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.3"],"stem":"Evaluate the impact of digital health on the Australian healthcare system. In your answer, refer to the stimulus.","criteria":"• Makes a well-reasoned judgement about the impact of digital health on the healthcare system, based on clearly stated criteria — 7–8\n• Analyses both the opportunities and the challenges for individuals and organisations, integrating the stimulus\n• Supports with relevant and accurate examples of digital health services\n• Communicates ideas and information using relevant health terminology in a well-structured response\n• Provides points for and against the impact of digital health, with a judgement — 5–6\n• Refers to the stimulus and supports with examples\n• Provides characteristics and features of digital health and its impact on the healthcare system — 3–4\n• Supports with example(s)\n• Sketches in general terms digital health and/or its impact — 1–2","sample":"Digital health is the electronic management of health information, and its impact on the Australian healthcare system should be judged against three criteria: does it improve the quality and continuity of care, does it use system resources efficiently, and does it distribute those benefits equitably?\n\nOn quality and continuity, the impact has been positive, as Speaker 1 suggests. My Health Record allows shared health summaries, discharge summaries, pathology and imaging reports and dispensed medicines to travel with the patient rather than being locked in one practice's files, which supports safer prescribing and reduces the risk of a clinician acting without knowing a patient's allergies or current medicines. Electronic prescriptions remove lost paper scripts and create an accurate medicines record, e-referrals and secure messaging move information between GPs, specialists and hospitals faster than post or fax, and telehealth items in the Medicare Benefits Schedule have made specialist and GP consultations accessible without travel, which matters most for rural patients and people with mobility limitations.\n\nOn efficiency, the picture is mixed. Avoided duplicate pathology and imaging, fewer avoidable admissions where medicines are reconciled correctly, and reduced travel and transfer costs are real savings for the system. Against this, Speaker 2 identifies the workload cost: uploading and reconciling information consumes clinician time that is not always funded, organisations must pay for compliant software, training and cyber-security, and poor interoperability between hospital and primary care systems means staff often re-enter the same information in more than one place. Where data quality is poor — records with few documents, duplicated or out-of-date information — clinicians may distrust the system and revert to phoning the other provider, which cancels the intended saving.\n\nOn equity, the impact is weakest, and this is the strongest part of Speaker 2's argument. The opt-out model achieved near-universal registration, and patient-controlled access and the ability to cancel a record respond to legitimate privacy concerns, but engagement is not the same as registration. Older Australians, people in remote areas with unreliable connectivity, and people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds are least likely to have the devices, digital literacy or language support to use telehealth, manage a prescription token or review their own record — and these are the groups with the highest burden of chronic disease. Without targeted support, digital health risks widening the gap in health outcomes rather than closing it.\n\nOverall, digital health has had a clearly positive but incomplete impact. It has built national infrastructure that has improved continuity of care and access to consultations, and it has strengthened the management of medicines in particular. However, its benefits are constrained by inconsistent provider uptake, incomplete interoperability, the workload it places on organisations, and a digital divide that excludes the populations who would gain most. Its future impact depends less on further technology than on funding clinician time, achieving genuine interoperability between systems, and investing in digital inclusion.","expl":"This is the full expression of the content point's own command verb. A 7–8 response states criteria for the judgement, uses both speakers as positions to be tested rather than quoted, names real Australian services accurately, and reaches a qualified overall verdict with a forward-looking condition. A 5–6 response gives genuine strengths and weaknesses and mentions the stimulus, but evaluates without criteria and often ends with an unsupported 'therefore digital health is beneficial'. Responses that treat digital health as simply 'technology in hospitals' — robotic surgery, health apps — have drifted into FA1.3.1 and FA1.3.2 and cannot reach the top band.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the impact of digital health on the healthcare system","aim":"what challenges and opportunities does digital health provide for individuals and organisations? — synthesis with 'what is digital health?'","stim":"<div class=\"stimbox extract\"><div class=\"stimlabel\">STIMULUS</div><p>Two statements from a health service planning forum.</p><p>Speaker 1 — 'Digital health has transformed our system. A clinician anywhere in Australia can see a patient's medicines and recent results, and telehealth has brought specialist care to towns that never had it.'</p><p>Speaker 2 — 'The people who most need connected care are the least able to use it, and my staff spend more time on data entry than on patients.'</p></div>"},{"id":"NEW-SA-018","year":null,"n":18,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.4"],"stem":"Outline how big data is used to monitor and respond to communicable disease in Australia.","criteria":"• Provides an outline of how big data is used to monitor communicable disease AND how this informs a public health response — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the use of big data in monitoring communicable disease — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Laboratory and general practice notifications from every state and territory are collated into very large national surveillance datasets, such as the National Notifiable Diseases Surveillance System, and are analysed computationally for unusual increases in cases by time, place and age group. Additional data sources such as wastewater testing and emergency department presentations can be combined with these notifications. Because patterns are detected far earlier than they would be by individual clinicians, health authorities can respond quickly with contact tracing, targeted vaccination and public health messaging in the affected area.","expl":"This targets the 'how is it being used?' sub-bullet through surveillance and outbreak detection. A top-band response does two things: it identifies a genuine large-scale Australian data source and describes the analysis of patterns across it, and it links that analysis to a specific public health action. A mid-band response typically names a data source or says 'data is collected about diseases' without showing that the value lies in computational pattern detection across a very large dataset, or without connecting the monitoring to a response.","src":"new","point":"Examine how big data is shaping the health of Australians","aim":"how is it being used? (disease surveillance and outbreak detection)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-019","year":null,"n":19,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.4"],"stem":"Explain how the use of big data can reduce healthcare spending in Australia.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of how big data can reduce healthcare spending, with relevant examples — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of how big data can reduce healthcare spending, with examples — 4\n• Provides some understanding of how big data can reduce healthcare spending, with example(s) — 3\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of big data and healthcare spending — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Big data reduces healthcare spending mainly by shifting expenditure away from expensive acute treatment towards cheaper prevention and early intervention. Predictive risk models built from linked hospital, MBS and PBS data identify the small proportion of patients who account for a large share of admissions, so that local health districts can offer them community follow-up before they deteriorate; because an avoidable hospital admission costs many times more than community care, preventing even a modest number of admissions saves substantial funds. Big data also reduces spending by improving efficiency — modelling emergency department presentations against influenza notifications and seasonal patterns allows a hospital network to roster staff and open beds ahead of the peak, avoiding costly last-minute agency staffing and ambulance ramping. Analysis of MBS and PBS claims data further identifies low-value or duplicated care, such as unnecessary repeat imaging, so that funding can be redirected to services that produce better health outcomes. Finally, matching patients to treatments through genomic data avoids the cost of prescribing expensive therapies to patients unlikely to respond to them.","expl":"This targets the 'how is it reducing healthcare spending?' sub-bullet. A top-band response explains at least two distinct mechanisms (for example prevention of avoidable admissions, efficiency in resource allocation, elimination of low-value care, avoided ineffective therapy) and makes the cost logic explicit — cheaper care substituted for dearer care. A mid-band response asserts that big data 'saves money' and gives one example without explaining why the saving occurs. Weak responses describe digital health services such as telehealth rather than the analysis of large datasets.","src":"new","point":"Examine how big data is shaping the health of Australians","aim":"how is it reducing healthcare spending? (predictive risk models, hospital demand forecasting, targeted prevention)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-020","year":null,"n":20,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.4"],"stem":"Compare the use of digital health and the use of big data in the Australian healthcare system.","criteria":"• Provides a comparison of the use of digital health and the use of big data in the healthcare system, identifying similarities and differences — 4\n• Provides a sound comparison of the use of digital health and the use of big data — 3\n• Demonstrates an understanding of the use of digital health and/or big data — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Both digital health and big data rely on health information being collected and stored electronically, and both raise the same concerns about the privacy and confidentiality of personal information. However, digital health is the use of digital technologies to deliver and connect care for an individual — telehealth appointments, electronic prescriptions and My Health Record all improve the care of the person in front of the clinician. Big data, by contrast, is the computational analysis of extremely large datasets, such as linked AIHW hospital, MBS and PBS records, to reveal patterns, trends and associations across whole populations, and it is used for planning, research and policy rather than for treating one patient. The two are closely related because digital health systems generate much of the information that big data analysis then draws on.","expl":"This item exists to force the distinction the content point requires. A top-band response states the core difference — individual care delivery versus population-level pattern analysis — uses accurate Australian examples of each, and identifies at least one genuine similarity (electronic collection of information, shared privacy risks, or the fact that digital health feeds big data). A mid-band response describes both concepts accurately but side by side, without an explicit point of comparison. Responses that treat the two terms as synonyms cannot reach the top band.","src":"new","point":"Examine how big data is shaping the health of Australians","aim":"how is it being used? (distinguishing big data from digital health)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-021","year":null,"n":21,"part":"","marks":6,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.4"],"stem":"Examine the measures that need to be taken to ensure the privacy and confidentiality of personal information when big data is used in health.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough examination of the measures needed to protect privacy and confidentiality when big data is used in health, supported by relevant examples — 6\n• Provides a sound examination of the measures needed to protect privacy and confidentiality when big data is used in health, with examples — 4–5\n• Provides some understanding of measures used to protect privacy and confidentiality of health information, with example(s) — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"The first measure is de-identification: names, addresses and Medicare numbers are stripped from records, and detailed variables such as date of birth are reduced to year of birth, before a dataset is made available. De-identification alone is not enough, because rare combinations of variables — for example a person of a particular age with a rare condition in a small rural postcode — can allow re-identification, so datasets must also be assessed for re-identification risk and released only at a level of detail appropriate to the purpose. Second, access must be governed rather than open. In Australia, separation of duties is used so that the agency that links the records does not analyse them, applications are assessed by a data custodian and a human research ethics committee, and researchers work inside secure environments where they can analyse the data but cannot download it. Third, the handling of health information is regulated as sensitive information under the Privacy Act, which requires that information be used only for the purpose for which it was collected or a directly related purpose the person would reasonably expect, that it be securely stored, and that breaches be notified. Fourth, individuals need genuine choice — informed consent where practicable, an accessible opt-out such as that available for My Health Record, and clear public communication about who is using their data and why. Taken together these measures protect the social licence for health data use: if the community loses confidence, people will opt out or withhold information from clinicians, which degrades the very datasets that make big data valuable.","expl":"A top-band response moves beyond listing safeguards to examining why each is necessary and where each falls short — in particular recognising that de-identification does not eliminate re-identification risk, and that legal compliance under the Privacy Act must be accompanied by public trust or social licence if the data supply itself is not to be undermined. A mid-band response lists two or three correct measures (usually de-identification, secure storage, consent) with accurate but undeveloped explanation and no Australian institutional detail. Weak responses simply assert that 'data must be kept private'.","src":"new","point":"Examine how big data is shaping the health of Australians","aim":"what measures need to be taken to ensure privacy and confidentiality of personal information? (de-identification, re-identification risk, consent, governance, the Privacy Act, social licence)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-022","year":null,"n":22,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["Technology and data"],"sids":["FA1.3.4"],"stem":"Examine how big data such as that described above is shaping the health of Australians.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive examination of how big data is shaping the health of Australians, addressing its use, its impact on healthcare spending, its role in treating and managing disease, and the privacy measures required — 7–8\n• Presents a logical and cohesive response supported by relevant examples\n• Provides a sound examination of how big data is shaping the health of Australians, addressing several of these aspects, with examples — 5–6\n• Provides some examination of how big data is used in health, with example(s) — 3–4\n• Provides some relevant information about big data and health — 1–2","sample":"Big data of this scale is reshaping Australian health care first by changing what can be known about the population. Linking hospital, MBS, PBS and death records for more than 20 million people allows patterns to be detected that are invisible in any single dataset — for example, which combinations of medicines, comorbidities and service use precede kidney failure — and these patterns become predictive risk models that identify high-risk patients years before they become unwell. This shifts care towards prevention and early intervention. It also reduces healthcare spending: dialysis is one of the most expensive ongoing treatments in the health system, so delaying or preventing progression to end-stage kidney disease in even a small proportion of patients produces large savings, while forecasting future dialysis demand allows services and workforce to be planned in advance rather than funded reactively at crisis prices. Second, big data is changing how disease is treated and managed. Combining a genomic dataset with clinical records supports precision medicine, where treatment is matched to a patient's genetic profile rather than prescribed on a trial-and-error basis, and it makes clinical trial recruitment far faster and more targeted, because the pool of eligible patients can be identified from existing records instead of through slow manual screening. This accelerates access to new treatments and means trials can be run for rarer conditions that would otherwise never accumulate enough participants. However, these benefits depend entirely on maintaining privacy and confidentiality. Records are de-identified before release, but the richness of linked data means re-identification remains a real risk, so access must be restricted to approved researchers working in a secure environment from which data cannot be extracted, applications must be approved by a data custodian and a human research ethics committee, and the handling of this sensitive information is regulated under the Privacy Act. Individuals also need genuine choice through consent or opt-out arrangements, and transparency about who is using their data. Overall, big data is shaping the health of Australians by making the health system more predictive, more preventive and more personalised, but its continued contribution rests on a social licence that will only hold while the community is confident its information is being used safely and in the public interest.","expl":"This is the synoptic question for the content point and is written around the command verb NESA uses in the syllabus. A top-band response addresses all four sub-bullets and, critically, connects them: it shows that the same features that make linked data valuable (scale, richness, linkage) are the features that create re-identification risk, and it recognises that privacy protection is a condition of the benefits rather than a separate afterthought. It uses the stimulus rather than restating it. A mid-band response describes two or three uses of big data competently — usually prediction and precision medicine — but treats privacy as a brief add-on and does not develop the spending argument beyond asserting that prevention is cheaper. Lower-band responses generalise about technology in health care or drift into digital health services such as telehealth without engaging with large-scale data analysis.","src":"new","point":"Examine how big data is shaping the health of Australians","aim":"all four sub-bullets — how it is used, reducing spending, curing and managing disease, privacy and confidentiality","stim":"<div class=\"stimbox extract\"><div class=\"stimlabel\">CASE STUDY</div><p>A national research program links de-identified hospital admission, MBS, PBS and death records covering more than 20 million Australians, and combines them with a genomic dataset contributed by 100 000 volunteers. The linked data is used to predict which patients are at high risk of chronic kidney disease, to identify and recruit suitable patients into clinical trials of new treatments, and to forecast future demand for dialysis services. Access is granted only to approved researchers working within a secure analysis environment.</p></div>"},{"id":"NEW-SA-023","year":null,"n":23,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.1"],"stem":"Outline TWO key features of the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).","criteria":"• Outlines TWO key features of the Sustainable Development Goals — 3\n• Outlines ONE key feature of the Sustainable Development Goals, OR identifies TWO features — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"The SDGs are a set of 17 goals agreed by United Nations member states in 2015, to be achieved by 2030, which aim to end poverty, reduce inequality and address climate change. A second key feature is that they are universal: unlike the earlier Millennium Development Goals, they apply to developed countries such as Australia as well as developing countries, and each country reports its own progress against agreed targets and indicators through voluntary national reviews.","expl":"This is a straightforward recall question anchored on the content point's verb, 'describe the key features'. It rewards students who can name genuine features — number of goals, the 2015 agreement and 2030 target, universality, indivisibility, measurement by targets and indicators, voluntary national reviews — rather than simply listing the four named goals. A 3-mark response gives two features with enough detail to show what each means; a 2-mark response names two features without developing either, or develops only one. Listing the titles of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 is not a feature of the SDGs and earns at most 1 mark.","src":"new","point":"Describe the key features of Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)","aim":"Key features of the SDGs — what they are, universality, indivisibility, targets and indicators"},{"id":"NEW-SA-024","year":null,"n":24,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.1"],"stem":"Explain how progress towards SDG 4: Quality Education could improve the health status of a population.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of how quality education improves health status, supported by relevant examples — 4\n• Provides a sound explanation of how quality education improves health status — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of a link between education and health — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"SDG 4 seeks inclusive and equitable quality education and lifelong learning for all, including free primary and secondary schooling, equal access for girls, people with disability and other vulnerable groups, and adult literacy. Improving education raises health literacy, so people are better able to understand health information, follow the Australian Dietary Guidelines, use screening programs and navigate services such as Medicare and the PBS. Education also increases employment and income security, which are socioeconomic determinants of health that shape housing, nutrition and access to care. For example, raising Year 12 completion rates among students in rural and remote Australia would be expected over time to improve their employment prospects and lower their exposure to risk behaviours such as smoking, narrowing the health gap between them and people in major cities.","expl":"The question tests the requirement that students can explain why health depends on goals other than SDG 3. A top-band response makes the causal chain explicit — education leads to health literacy and to employment and income, which in turn act as determinants of health behaviour and access to services — and grounds it in an Australian example. A mid-band response asserts that 'educated people are healthier' without showing the mechanism, or describes SDG 4's targets accurately without linking them to health outcomes. Responses that drift into SDG 3 content should not be credited beyond the education–health link they actually establish.","src":"new","point":"Describe the key features of Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)","aim":"SDG 4: Quality Education — why health depends on goals other than SDG 3"},{"id":"NEW-SA-025","year":null,"n":25,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.1"],"stem":"Compare SDG 3: Good Health and Wellbeing with SDG 11: Sustainable Cities and Communities in terms of how each contributes to improving the health of Australians.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough comparison of the contribution of SDG 3 and SDG 11 to the health of Australians, identifying both similarities and differences, supported by relevant examples — 5\n• Provides a sound comparison of the contribution of SDG 3 and SDG 11 to health — 4\n• Provides a limited comparison, OR describes the contribution of each goal separately — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of SDG 3 and/or SDG 11 — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Both goals aim to improve population health and both are universal, applying to Australia as well as to developing countries, and progress in each is measured against agreed targets and indicators. They differ in how they act on health. SDG 3 works directly on health outcomes and health systems: it targets premature death from non-communicable disease, mental health and substance use, road trauma and universal health coverage, which in Australia is supported through Medicare, the PBS and the MBS. SDG 11 works indirectly, through the environments in which people live: adequate and affordable housing, safe and sustainable public transport, and universal access to safe, inclusive green and public space. A council providing shaded parks, footpaths and cycleways makes incidental physical activity easier for a whole community, whereas an SDG 3 measure such as expanding bulk-billed general practice improves early detection and treatment. SDG 3 therefore tends to produce more immediate and measurable health gains, while SDG 11 addresses the determinants that shape health over the longer term, and the two are indivisible — neither alone is sufficient.","expl":"The command term requires similarities and differences to be drawn out in an integrated way, not two separate descriptions. A top-band response identifies at least one genuine similarity (universality, shared aim, measurement by targets and indicators) and sustains a clear point of difference — direct action on health outcomes and services versus action on the built environment as a determinant — with Australian examples for each. A mid-band response describes both goals accurately and correctly but treats them one after the other with only a token comparative sentence. Weak responses reduce SDG 11 to 'clean cities' with no health link.","src":"new","point":"Describe the key features of Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)","aim":"SDG 3: Good Health and Wellbeing and SDG 11: Sustainable Cities and Communities"},{"id":"NEW-SA-026","year":null,"n":26,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.1"],"stem":"Analyse why improving the health of Australians depends on progress towards SDGs 4, 10 and 11, and not on SDG 3 alone.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough analysis of the relationships between SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 and the health of Australians, showing clearly why action on SDG 3 alone is insufficient\n• Provides relevant and detailed examples — 8\n• Provides a sound analysis of the relationships between the SDGs and the health of Australians\n• Provides relevant examples — 6–7\n• Provides a sound understanding of links between the SDGs and the health of Australians\n• Provides example(s) — 4–5\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of the SDG(s) — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"A key feature of the SDGs is that they are integrated and indivisible: progress on one goal depends on progress on the others. SDG 3 acts on health directly, targeting premature death from non-communicable disease, mental health, road trauma and universal health coverage, and in Australia it is supported by Medicare, the PBS and the MBS. However, these services largely respond to illness after it develops, and they cannot alter the conditions that produce it.\n\nSDG 4 shapes health through education. Quality education raises health literacy, so people can interpret health information and use preventive services such as bowel and breast screening, and it improves employment and income, which determine housing quality and food security. Data in Australia's Health consistently shows a gradient in which people with lower educational attainment have higher rates of smoking, obesity and chronic disease, so an expansion of bulk-billed general practice will not close that gap on its own.\n\nSDG 10 addresses the inequalities that underlie this gradient. Its targets include eliminating discriminatory practices, promoting social, economic and political inclusion, and raising the incomes of the lowest-earning households. For Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples, health inequities arise from dispossession, racism and socioeconomic disadvantage; culturally safe services and community-controlled decision-making, which are SDG 10 concerns, are needed for SDG 3 measures to reach the people who most need them.\n\nSDG 11 shapes the environments in which health behaviours occur. Safe and affordable housing, accessible public transport and safe green and public space determine whether physical activity is realistic and whether a person in a rural or outer-suburban area can physically reach a service. A community with no footpaths, poor transport and unaffordable housing will not benefit fully from health promotion messages about physical activity.\n\nBecause the determinants of health sit largely outside the health system, action on SDG 3 alone would treat illness while leaving its causes untouched. Improving the health of Australians therefore requires the four goals to be pursued together, which is precisely what the indivisibility of the SDGs implies.","expl":"This is the synthesis question for the content point and directly targets the requirement that a student can explain why health depends on goals other than SDG 3. A top-band response is organised around the indivisibility of the SDGs, treats each of SDGs 4, 10 and 11 as acting on a determinant of health, shows the limits of a health-services-only approach, and uses detailed Australian examples (Australia's Health data, Medicare and the PBS, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health inequities, rural and remote access). A mid-band response describes what each goal covers and asserts that they are all connected to health without demonstrating the causal relationships or the insufficiency of SDG 3. Markers should not credit health-promotion frameworks substituted for the SDGs, and should not require any specific statistic — trends and relationships are what is being assessed.","src":"new","point":"Describe the key features of Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)","aim":"Key features — indivisible and interconnected; SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 together"},{"id":"NEW-SA-027","year":null,"n":27,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.1"],"stem":"Describe the purpose of a pre-exercise questionnaire used by fitness professionals in Australia.","criteria":"• Provides a sound description of the purpose of a pre-exercise questionnaire, including identifying risk and the need for referral — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the purpose of a pre-exercise questionnaire — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"A pre-exercise questionnaire, such as Stage 1 of the Adult Pre-Exercise Screening System (APSS), collects health information from a client before any exercise or testing begins. It asks whether the client has known cardiovascular, metabolic or renal disease, and whether they have signs or symptoms such as chest pain, dizziness or unusual breathlessness. Its purpose is to identify clients for whom exercise may be unsafe, so that the fitness professional can refer them to a medical practitioner for clearance, and to gather information about medications, injuries and activity history so that any program prescribed is appropriate and safe for that individual.","expl":"This targets straightforward recall of what the questionnaire is for. A mid-band response states only that it 'checks if the client is healthy'. A top-band response names the tool or its content (known disease, signs and symptoms) and links it to a consequence — referral, or safe individualised prescription — rather than treating the form as paperwork.","src":"new","point":"Explain the importance of using a pre-exercise questionnaire and undertaking relevant health screening by exercise and fitness professionals","aim":"Purpose of the pre-exercise questionnaire (APSS Stage 1)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-028","year":null,"n":28,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.1"],"stem":"Explain why health screening must be completed before fitness testing and exercise prescription.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of why screening precedes fitness testing and prescription, clearly distinguishing the two processes — 4\n• Provides a sound explanation of why screening precedes fitness testing and prescription — 3\n• Demonstrates an understanding of the purpose of screening or of fitness testing — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Health screening and fitness testing serve different purposes. Screening establishes whether it is safe for a client to exercise at all, while fitness testing measures components of fitness to provide a baseline for prescription and later evaluation. Screening must come first because fitness tests themselves impose physiological stress — a maximal aerobic test raises heart rate and blood pressure sharply — so an unscreened client with undiagnosed cardiovascular disease could suffer an adverse event during the very test intended to help them. Screening also identifies contraindications, medications and injuries that determine which tests are appropriate; for example, a client with uncontrolled hypertension would be referred to a medical practitioner rather than tested, and a client with a knee reconstruction would be given a submaximal rather than maximal test. Finally, screening information personalises the prescription that follows, so that intensity and mode are matched to the client's risk profile rather than assumed.","expl":"The examinable distinction is safety/eligibility versus measurement/baseline. A mid-band response says screening 'keeps the client safe' without explaining that testing is itself a physiological stressor. A top-band response draws the distinction explicitly, gives a reason why the order matters, and shows how screening information shapes what is subsequently tested and prescribed.","src":"new","point":"Explain the importance of using a pre-exercise questionnaire and undertaking relevant health screening by exercise and fitness professionals","aim":"Screening compared with fitness testing; screening before testing or prescription"},{"id":"NEW-SA-029","year":null,"n":29,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.1"],"stem":"Explain the professional and legal obligations of an exercise professional when screening a new client.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of professional and legal obligations, with clear reference to duty of care, informed consent and referral, supported by relevant examples — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of professional and legal obligations when screening a new client, with example(s) — 4\n• Provides a sound understanding of an obligation such as duty of care or informed consent — 3\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of the responsibilities of an exercise professional — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"An exercise professional owes their client a duty of care, meaning they must take reasonable steps to avoid foreseeable harm. Screening every new client with a tool such as the APSS is one of those reasonable steps, and failing to screen before prescribing exercise could constitute negligence if the client is injured. The professional must also obtain informed consent: the client must be told the purpose of any testing, the procedures involved, the risks and benefits and any alternatives, must have the capacity to agree, and must be free to withdraw at any time. A signed liability waiver does not replace informed consent and does not extinguish duty of care. A third obligation is to work within scope of practice — an exercise professional is not qualified to diagnose or manage disease, so when screening identifies known cardiovascular or metabolic disease, or symptoms such as exertional chest pain, they must refer the client to a medical practitioner for clearance rather than proceeding themselves. Professionals are also obliged to keep accurate and confidential records of screening, consent and any referral, to hold appropriate accreditation and professional indemnity insurance, and to re-screen when a client's health status changes.","expl":"Tests whether students can separate the three strands: duty of care (a legal standard), informed consent (a process, not a waiver), and scope of practice/referral. A mid-band response describes duty of care in general terms only. A top-band response distinguishes consent from a waiver, states that referral follows from scope of practice, and adds concrete professional obligations such as record-keeping, confidentiality, accreditation and re-screening.","src":"new","point":"Explain the importance of using a pre-exercise questionnaire and undertaking relevant health screening by exercise and fitness professionals","aim":"Informed consent, duty of care, referral and professional obligations"},{"id":"NEW-SA-030","year":null,"n":30,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.1"],"stem":"Assess the importance of pre-exercise questionnaires and health screening for exercise and fitness professionals working with older adults and with clients who have known metabolic or cardiovascular disease.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough assessment of the importance of pre-exercise questionnaires and health screening for the specified populations\n• Provides relevant and detailed examples — 8\n• Provides a sound assessment of the importance of pre-exercise questionnaires and health screening for the specified populations\n• Provides relevant examples — 6–7\n• Provides a sound understanding of the use of pre-exercise questionnaires and health screening with these populations\n• Provides example(s) — 4–5\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of pre-exercise screening — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Pre-exercise screening is of greatest importance precisely where the risk of an adverse event and the potential benefit of exercise are both highest, which is the case for older adults and for clients with known metabolic or cardiovascular disease. For an older adult, Stage 1 of the APSS identifies known disease and warning symptoms, while Stage 2 profiles accumulated risk factors such as inactivity, elevated waist girth and high blood pressure. Because ageing is associated with undiagnosed disease, reduced functional capacity, osteoporosis, balance decline and multiple medications, a questionnaire that captures medication use and falls history allows the professional to prescribe safely — for example, choosing a stationary cycle over treadmill running, or including resistance and balance work to reduce falls risk. Screening here also supports participation: identifying that an inactive 68-year-old has no known disease and no symptoms means light to moderate exercise can begin immediately, with medical advice sought only before vigorous exercise, avoiding an unnecessary referral that would act as a barrier.\n\nFor a client with known type 2 diabetes or established cardiovascular disease, screening is not optional. Stage 1 flags known metabolic or cardiovascular disease as requiring medical clearance, and screening distinguishes absolute contraindications, where exercise must not proceed until the condition is medically managed, from relative contraindications, where exercise can proceed with modification and supervision once benefits outweigh risks. Screening also gathers the information that makes prescription safe rather than merely permissible — insulin use and hypoglycaemia risk affect session timing and the need for blood glucose monitoring, while beta blockers blunt the heart rate response and mean intensity must be prescribed using rating of perceived exertion instead of a heart rate target.\n\nScreening is also important professionally. It discharges the exercise professional's duty of care and demonstrates that reasonable steps were taken, it supports genuine informed consent by ensuring the client understands the risks of the activity proposed, and it defines the boundary of scope of practice by identifying when referral to a medical practitioner is required. Its importance does have limits: a self-reported questionnaire depends on the honesty and health literacy of the client, it cannot detect asymptomatic disease, and it is a point-in-time snapshot that must be repeated when health status changes. Overall, however, screening is highly important, because for these populations it is the process that converts exercise from an uncontrolled risk into a personalised, safely prescribed intervention, and this is what allows the substantial health benefits of activity to be realised without exposing the client, or the professional, to unacceptable harm.","expl":"This is the natural 8-mark question for the content point and requires a judgement, not a description. A mid-band response describes what a questionnaire asks and asserts that screening is 'important for safety'. A top-band response makes a sustained judgement of importance, differentiates the two populations rather than treating them as one, uses precise terminology (APSS stages, absolute versus relative contraindication, referral, duty of care, scope of practice), gives concrete prescription consequences such as beta blockers and RPE or hypoglycaemia and session timing, and acknowledges limitations of self-reported screening before reaching a clear overall conclusion. Note for markers: this question also draws on FA1.1.5 healthy ageing content, and a response that legitimately links screening to increased participation for older adults should be credited.","src":"new","point":"Explain the importance of using a pre-exercise questionnaire and undertaking relevant health screening by exercise and fitness professionals","aim":"Screening for specific populations — older adults and known metabolic or cardiovascular disease; contraindications and referral"},{"id":"NEW-SA-031","year":null,"n":31,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.2"],"stem":"Outline how a community can identify its major health issues before developing strategies informed by the Sustainable Development Goals.","criteria":"• Outlines clearly how a community identifies its major health issues, referring to relevant data sources and consultation, and links this to the development of strategies — 3\n• Provides some relevant information about identifying the health issues of a community — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"A community first builds a profile of its health status using epidemiological data — AIHW reports such as Australia's Health, PHIDU social health atlas data and local health district data — to compare local rates of chronic disease, injury and risk behaviours with state and national figures. These data show prevalence and incidence and identify which groups within the community are most affected. This is combined with consultation with residents, local services and community organisations, which identifies issues the data may miss, such as social isolation, food insecurity or lack of safe places to be active. Together these sources allow the community to rank its major health issues and align them with the relevant SDGs before a plan of action is developed.","expl":"Tests the first stage of the FA1.4.2 process — you cannot evaluate strategies without knowing the issue they target. A top-band response names concrete Australian data sources AND includes community consultation, then connects both to setting priorities. A mid-band response lists 'statistics' or 'surveys' generically without naming sources or without the consultation element. Accept any legitimate Australian data source; do not require particular statistics.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the application of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 to inform strategies to improve the health status of a community","aim":"what are the major health issues for a community?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-032","year":null,"n":32,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.2"],"stem":"Describe how the Sustainable Development Goals have been applied in ONE community you have studied, and identify TWO lessons that could be applied to a different community.","criteria":"• Provides a detailed description of how SDGs have been applied in ONE community, with relevant examples, and clearly identifies TWO transferable lessons — 4\n• Provides a sound description of how SDGs have been applied in ONE community, with example(s), and identifies lesson(s) — 3\n• Outlines an application of SDGs in a community OR identifies lesson(s) that could be applied elsewhere — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"Healthy Cities Illawarra applies the SDGs through the WHO Healthy Cities approach. Its work on community gardens, food relief and food literacy addresses SDG 3 by improving nutrition and reducing risk factors for chronic disease, and SDG 4 by building food and health literacy skills. Its partnerships with Wollongong City Council on walkable neighbourhoods, shared paths and access to green space apply SDG 11, while targeting projects at low-income suburbs and at groups such as older residents and people with disability applies SDG 10. Two lessons transferable to another community are, first, that intersectoral partnership between local government, health services and community organisations gives a strategy reach and resources no single agency has; and second, that involving residents in identifying priorities and delivering projects builds local ownership, so activity continues after any particular grant ends.","expl":"Targets the two 'other communities' sub-bullets and NESA's named Example. Top band describes a real application with specific projects mapped to named SDGs and states lessons as generalisable principles (partnership, ownership, local adaptation). Mid band describes the community accurately but leaves the SDGs implicit, or states lessons that simply restate the example rather than abstracting from it. Other legitimate examples (WHO Healthy Cities members, an ACCHO, a Close the Gap initiative, a council active transport plan) are equally acceptable.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the application of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 to inform strategies to improve the health status of a community","aim":"how have these goals been applied in other communities? / what lessons can be drawn and applied to their own community context?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-033","year":null,"n":33,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.2"],"stem":"A coastal town with an ageing population and a high rate of preventable chronic disease has limited public transport, few footpaths and no local allied health services.\n\nExplain the strategies needed to advocate for and improve the health status of this community.","criteria":"• Provides a detailed explanation of strategies needed to advocate for and improve the health status of the community, showing clear cause-and-effect links between the strategies and the community's identified needs\n• Provides relevant examples — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of strategies needed to advocate for and improve the health status of the community\n• Provides examples — 4\n• Outlines strategies to advocate for or improve the health status of the community, with example(s) — 3\n• Identifies strategy(ies) relevant to the community — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"Advocacy is needed because the causes of poor health here sit outside the individual. Residents and local organisations can present local data on falls, chronic disease and service gaps to council and to the local member to argue for footpath construction, seating and shade along key routes, and for community transport — applying SDG 11 so that older residents can move safely and independently, which increases physical activity and reduces social isolation. A second strategy is to lobby the primary health network for outreach allied health and telehealth clinics, applying SDG 3 by improving access to services that manage chronic conditions locally rather than requiring travel. Because low income and lack of transport concentrate the burden on some residents more than others, these strategies must be targeted at those groups, applying SDG 10 so the benefit is equitable. Finally, forming a community reference group that includes older residents applies SDG 4 through peer education and ensures the community owns the plan of action, sustaining it beyond any one funding round.","expl":"The scenario deliberately supplies environmental and access barriers so students must move beyond individual behaviour change. A top-band response distinguishes advocacy (influencing decision-makers) from delivery, names at least two strategies, explains WHY each addresses the stated needs, and links them to specific SDGs. A mid-band response lists sensible strategies — 'build footpaths', 'run education sessions' — without explaining the causal link to the community's health status, or treats every strategy as individual education. Do not require the SDGs to be numbered if the concepts are clearly used.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the application of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 to inform strategies to improve the health status of a community","aim":"what strategies are needed to advocate and improve a community's health status?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-034","year":null,"n":34,"part":"","marks":7,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.2"],"stem":"Assess the effectiveness of the Bayside LGA Active Living Strategy in improving the health status of this community.","criteria":"• Makes a well-reasoned judgement about the effectiveness of the strategy, supported by systematic and accurate use of the data provided\n• Analyses effectiveness in terms of the indicators, equity of benefit and the sustainability of the strategy\n• Presents a logical and cohesive response — 6–7\n• Makes a judgement about the effectiveness of the strategy, using relevant data from the stimulus\n• Refers to more than one aspect of effectiveness, such as outcomes and equity — 4–5\n• Describes changes shown in the data and makes limited judgement about effectiveness — 2–3\n• Provides relevant information about the strategy or the data — 1","sample":"The strategy has been substantially effective in delivering what it set out to build and in shifting behaviour, but its benefit has not been equitable and its continuation is uncertain. The process indicators show strong implementation: separated path grew more than fivefold and canopy cover rose by five percentage points, showing the SDG 11 built-environment commitments were actually delivered rather than only announced. The outcome indicators show this translated into behaviour — active travel to work or study almost doubled and the proportion of adults meeting the physical activity guidelines rose by seven percentage points — and because these indicators were measured against a 2021 baseline the change can reasonably be attributed to the strategy. Sustained increases in physical activity would be expected to reduce the risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and some cancers, improving the community's health status over time, although mortality and life expectancy would not yet register any change.\n\nHowever, effectiveness must also be judged on who benefited. Residents in the most disadvantaged quintile improved by only two percentage points against seven for the LGA overall, so the gap has widened. This suggests the new infrastructure was built where advantaged residents live, or that cost, safety, shift work and lack of equipment remain barriers. Against SDG 10, the strategy has therefore been ineffective, and equity of benefit should be a condition of the next phase.\n\nSustainability is also questionable. Community reference group meetings fell from four a year to one, indicating declining community ownership, and the funding ends in 2026. Infrastructure already built will keep delivering benefit, but the programs, maintenance and advocacy that support its use may not. Overall, the strategy has been effective in changing the environment and improving average physical activity, but only partly effective in improving the health status of the whole community, and it will need renewed funding, targeted delivery in disadvantaged neighbourhoods and restored community involvement to sustain the gains.","expl":"This is the central evaluation item for FA1.4.2. A top-band response does three things a mid-band response does not: it distinguishes process indicators from outcome indicators and comments on what the data can and cannot yet show; it uses the disaggregated row to argue that the aggregate improvement conceals a widening inequity (SDG 10); and it reads the reference-group and funding rows as evidence about community ownership and sustainability. A mid-band response describes each row in turn — 'cycleways went up, activity went up, so the strategy worked' — and offers a judgement without weighing equity or sustainability. Reward students who note that health status measures such as mortality would lag well beyond four years.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the application of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 to inform strategies to improve the health status of a community","aim":"how do you know these strategies may be effective? (indicators, equity of benefit, sustainability, community ownership)","stim":"<div class=\"stimbox\"><table class=\"stimtable\"><caption>Bayside LGA Active Living Strategy &mdash; evaluation indicators, 2021 vs 2025</caption><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>2021</th><th>2025</th></tr><tr><th>Separated cycleway and shared path</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">4 km</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">22 km</td></tr><tr><th>Adults walking or cycling to work/study</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">6%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">11%</td></tr><tr><th>Adults meeting physical activity guidelines</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">48%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">55%</td></tr><tr><th>Adults meeting physical activity guidelines &mdash; most disadvantaged quintile</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">41%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">43%</td></tr><tr><th>Tree canopy cover</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">12%</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">17%</td></tr><tr><th>Community reference group meetings held</th><td style=\"text-align:right\">4 per year</td><td style=\"text-align:right\">1 per year</td></tr></table><p class=\"stimnote\">Strategy funding source: a state grant ending in 2026.</p></div>"},{"id":"NEW-SA-035","year":null,"n":35,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["SDGs and community action"],"sids":["FA1.4.2"],"stem":"Evaluate the application of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 to inform strategies to improve the health status of ONE community. In your answer, refer to how you would judge whether those strategies are working.","criteria":"• Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 and how they inform strategies to improve the health status of a community\n• Makes well-informed judgements about the value and limitations of applying the goals, and about how the effectiveness of the strategies would be judged\n• Presents a logical and cohesive response supported by a range of relevant examples — 7–8\n• Demonstrates a sound understanding of the SDGs and how they inform strategies to improve the health status of a community\n• Makes some judgement about the application of the goals and about how effectiveness would be judged\n• Presents a logical response with relevant examples — 5–6\n• Describes how the SDGs relate to strategies to improve the health status of a community\n• Provides example(s) — 3–4\n• Provides relevant information about the SDGs or about community health strategies — 1–2","sample":"The community I have studied is a Western Sydney suburb with high rates of type 2 diabetes and psychological distress, a large culturally and linguistically diverse population, low tree canopy and poor walkability. Applying the four SDGs produces a far more complete plan of action than any one goal alone.\n\nSDG 3 informs strategies that improve access to care — outreach chronic disease clinics, bulk-billed general practice, mental health support through the primary health network and immunisation catch-up. This is valuable because it addresses existing illness, but on its own it is limited: it treats the consequences of the community's circumstances rather than the circumstances themselves. SDG 4 informs health literacy strategies delivered through schools, TAFE and community language groups, such as cooking programs built on the Australian Dietary Guidelines and peer-led education in first languages. Education has strong long-term value because skills stay with residents, but knowledge alone does not create the food, income or safety conditions needed to act on it. SDG 11 addresses those conditions directly: footpaths, lighting, shade tree planting, safe crossings, community gardens and accessible public transport make physical activity and social connection the easy option, and these environmental changes reach everyone passively rather than only those who volunteer for a program. SDG 10 is the goal that determines whether any of this improves health status across the whole community rather than only for the already advantaged: it requires interpreters, fee waivers, targeted placement of new infrastructure in the most disadvantaged neighbourhoods, and community-controlled delivery, following the model of Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisations and the Close the Gap approach of community ownership.\n\nThe strength of applying the goals together is that they cover services, skills, environments and equity, mirroring the sociocultural and ecological models rather than the biomedical model alone. Healthy Cities Illawarra shows this working in practice through intersectoral partnership between council, health services and residents. The limitations are real, however: the SDGs are global and non-binding, they set no local targets, and their indicators are designed for national reporting, so a community must translate them into locally meaningful measures and secure funding that typically runs for only three to five years.\n\nI would judge these strategies against a baseline established before implementation, using a layered set of indicators: process measures such as kilometres of footpath built and numbers of residents reached; short-term outcome measures such as observed walking counts, food security surveys and self-reported physical activity; and longer-term measures such as prevalence of type 2 diabetes and hospitalisation rates, recognising these lag by years. Critically, every indicator would be disaggregated by income, language spoken at home and age, because a strategy that lifts the community average while leaving the most disadvantaged unchanged has failed the test set by SDG 10. I would also assess reach, whether community members retained decision-making roles, and whether activity continued after the initial funding ceased, since sustainability and community ownership are the strongest predictors that improvement in health status will be maintained.","expl":"This is the full content point and should read as the paper's extended-response item. The discriminator between bands is judgement. A top-band response evaluates — it states the value AND the limitations of applying the SDGs (non-binding, global indicators, short funding cycles, education without enabling conditions), shows how the four goals work together rather than listing them, uses a specific community with concrete Australian examples, and then sets out a defensible evaluation design: baseline, layered process and outcome indicators, disaggregation for equity, reach, community ownership and sustainability. A mid-band response describes what each goal is about and lists plausible strategies with little or no judgement, and answers the second half of the question with 'we would look at whether the statistics improved'. Any community consistent with the glossary definition is acceptable, including a non-geographic community such as an online or cultural community, provided the strategies and indicators genuinely fit it.","src":"new","point":"Evaluate the application of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 to inform strategies to improve the health status of a community","aim":"whole content point — evaluating the application of SDGs 3, 4, 10 and 11 to strategies for a community"},{"id":"NEW-SA-036","year":null,"n":36,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.3"],"stem":"A coach at an athletics club completes a series of exercise assessments with a 17-year-old middle-distance runner at the start of the season.\n\nOutline how the results of these assessments would assist the coach to develop the runner's training program. (3 marks)","criteria":"• Outlines clearly how assessment results assist program development, referring to more than one aspect of program design (eg baseline, identified weaknesses, goals, intensities) — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of how assessment results are used in developing a training program — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"The assessment results give the coach a baseline record of the runner's current aerobic capacity, speed and strength, so that any later change can be measured against a known starting point. Comparing these results with the demands of middle-distance running identifies strengths and weaknesses — for example a strong 1500 m time trial but poor leg strength — allowing the coach to direct training time towards the areas of greatest need. The results also allow the coach to set specific, realistic goals with the runner and to prescribe training intensities, such as heart rate zones drawn from the runner's assessed maximum heart rate.","expl":"Tests whether students can convert 'what testing tells you' into 'what the program then does'. A top response names at least two distinct uses of the data (baseline, weakness identification, goal setting, intensity prescription) and links them to program decisions. A mid-band response describes the tests themselves, or states only that testing shows 'how fit the athlete is', without connecting the result to a programming decision.","src":"new","point":"Explain how exercise assessment can assist in developing training programs","aim":"Baseline data, identifying strengths and weaknesses, and goal setting"},{"id":"NEW-SA-037","year":null,"n":37,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.3"],"stem":"Explain how the coach would use these assessment results to prescribe the intensity of the athlete's aerobic and resistance training sessions. (4 marks)","criteria":"• Provides a clear explanation of how BOTH results are converted into prescribed training intensities, with reasons that relate to the athlete's goal — 4\n• Provides a sound explanation of how the results are used to set training intensity for aerobic and/or resistance work — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of using assessment results to set training intensity — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"The measured maximum heart rate of 198 bpm allows the coach to set aerobic training thresholds as percentages of that figure rather than relying on an age-based estimate. Long continuous rides and runs would be prescribed at roughly 65–75% (about 130–150 bpm) to build the aerobic base needed for an Olympic-distance event, while aerobic interval sessions would be set at around 85% (about 170 bpm) to work near the anaerobic threshold and lift the intensity the athlete can sustain. The 1RM of 90 kg allows resistance loads to be prescribed as a percentage of maximum: because the goal is endurance rather than maximal strength, the coach would prescribe higher-repetition sets at around 60–70% of 1RM, that is roughly 55–65 kg, to develop muscular endurance without adding unnecessary bulk. Prescribing from the athlete's own tested values means the intensity is individualised and can be recalculated as the athlete improves, delivering progressive overload.","expl":"Tests the central mechanism of the content point — a number from a test becoming a prescribed intensity. Top-band responses do the conversion for both results (a percentage of HRmax and a percentage of 1RM) and justify the choice of percentage by reference to the athlete's endurance goal. Mid-band responses state that the results are 'used to set intensity' without translating either figure, or handle only one of the two tests.","src":"new","point":"Explain how exercise assessment can assist in developing training programs","aim":"Setting training thresholds and resistance loads from assessment results","stim":"<div class=\"stimbox extract\"><div class=\"stimlabel\">ASSESSMENT SUMMARY</div><p>Club-level triathlete, aged 20. Maximal treadmill test — maximum heart rate 198 bpm. Gym testing — 1RM back squat 90 kg. Athlete's stated goal: complete an Olympic-distance triathlon.</p></div>"},{"id":"NEW-SA-038","year":null,"n":38,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.3"],"stem":"Explain how re-testing an athlete part-way through a training program allows a coach to apply the principles of training. (5 marks)","criteria":"• Provides a detailed explanation of how re-test data leads to program adjustment, with clear and correct links to named principles of training — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of how re-testing informs program adjustment, with some reference to principles of training — 4\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the use of re-testing in a training program — 3\n• Sketches in general terms the purpose of re-testing — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Re-testing gives the coach objective evidence of whether the adaptations the program was designed to produce are actually occurring. Where a result has improved — for example a 1RM bench press rising from 70 kg to 80 kg — the coach recalculates training loads from the new maximum so that the relative intensity is maintained, which is how progressive overload is delivered across a program. Re-testing also allows training thresholds to be reset: if a re-tested maximum heart rate or lactate threshold has shifted, the heart rates or power outputs prescribed for aerobic sessions must move with it, otherwise the athlete continues working at an intensity that is now too easy to stimulate further adaptation. Where a result has not improved, the coach can diagnose why: an unchanged aerobic score in a program dominated by resistance work points to a failure of specificity, and the coach would redirect training time towards aerobic training. A result that has fallen after illness or an interrupted block shows reversibility, and the coach would rebuild volume gradually rather than resuming the previous load. Re-testing therefore turns the program into a cycle of assess, prescribe, re-assess and adjust, rather than a fixed plan.","expl":"Tests the monitoring and adjustment stage of the assessment chain, and the explicit link to FA2.2.2. Top-band responses use at least two principles accurately (typically progressive overload and training thresholds, plus specificity or reversibility) and show the decision the coach makes in each case. Mid-band responses assert that re-testing 'shows if the athlete has improved' and list principles without demonstrating how a particular result changes a particular part of the program. Naming principles without a programming consequence should not reach the top band.","src":"new","point":"Explain how exercise assessment can assist in developing training programs","aim":"Re-testing to monitor progress and adjust the program; link to principles of training (FA2.2.2)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-039","year":null,"n":39,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.3"],"stem":"Evaluate the contribution that exercise assessment makes to developing an effective training program for this squad. (8 marks)","criteria":"• Makes a well-supported judgement about the contribution of exercise assessment to program development, with detailed reference to the scenario and to how assessment results shape specific programming decisions\n• Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of the relationship between exercise assessment, the principles of training and individualisation within a squad\n• Communicates ideas and information using relevant examples and appropriate terminology — 7–8\n\n• Makes a judgement about the contribution of exercise assessment to program development, with reference to the scenario\n• Demonstrates a sound understanding of how assessment results inform program design\n• Communicates ideas using some relevant examples and terminology — 5–6\n\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the use of exercise assessment in developing a training program\n• Makes limited reference to the scenario — 3–4\n\n• Provides some relevant information about fitness testing or training programs — 1–2","sample":"Exercise assessment makes a substantial contribution to this squad's program because it converts general knowledge of netball's demands into specific, defensible prescriptions. The battery has been chosen to match the demands of the sport: the Yo-Yo test measures the repeated high-intensity running required over four quarters, the vertical jump and 5 m and 20 m sprints measure the explosive power and acceleration needed for rebounds, drives and defensive changes of direction, and the 1RM squat provides the value from which resistance loads can be calculated. Because the tests are sport-specific, the profile they produce shows the coach exactly where the squad's strengths and weaknesses sit relative to what competition demands, and training time can be directed to the areas of greatest need rather than to areas already well developed.\n\nThe assessment results also make the program measurable. Each player has a baseline, so goals can be set that are specific and realistic — for example, a defined increase in Yo-Yo distance by the start of the competition phase — and re-testing mid-program shows whether adaptation is occurring. Where it is, loads are recalculated from the new 1RM and heart rate zones reset from re-tested values, which is how progressive overload and training thresholds are applied in practice; where a result is unchanged, the coach can diagnose a lack of specificity or insufficient overload and adjust the program rather than continuing with a plan that is not working.\n\nThe contribution is greatest in resolving the tension in this scenario between a single squad program and 12 different athletes. The wide spread of results means a uniform prescription would overload some players and understimulate others. Assessment allows the coach to keep the four weekly sessions collective in their skill, tactical and conditioning structure while individualising the intensity within them — each player runs the same interval session but to her own heart rate zone, and each lifts in the same session but at her own percentage of 1RM. Assessment therefore delivers individualisation without fragmenting the squad, and also flags players whose profile suggests an elevated injury risk or a need for modified loading.\n\nThe contribution is not unlimited. Test results are only a snapshot and can be affected by fatigue, motivation, illness or unfamiliarity with the protocol, so a single poor score should not on its own reshape a player's program. Field tests such as the Yo-Yo estimate rather than directly measure aerobic capacity, and no test in this battery captures decision-making, court craft or the ability to execute under pressure, which are decisive in netball. Testing also consumes training time and, for under-19 athletes, published results can create unhelpful comparison between team-mates. On balance, however, exercise assessment is essential rather than optional here: without it the coach would be prescribing intensity by guesswork, could not individualise within a group of this size, and would have no objective basis for judging whether the program is working. Its value is greatest when it is used as an ongoing cycle of assessment, prescription and re-assessment, and when its results are interpreted alongside the coach's observation of performance in match play.","expl":"This is the synoptic question for the content point and deliberately requires the whole chain: baseline, strengths and weaknesses relative to the demands of the sport, threshold and load setting, goal setting, re-testing and adjustment, and individualisation within a group. The distinguishing feature of a top-band response is a sustained judgement — it weighs what assessment genuinely delivers (objective, individualised, measurable prescription) against real limitations (snapshot data, estimation rather than measurement, nothing captured about decision-making or skill) and reaches a defensible conclusion, all anchored in the 12-player squad scenario. A mid-band response describes what each test measures and asserts that testing 'helps the coach plan', with a judgement tacked on at the end and little or no engagement with the problem of one program for 12 different profiles. Responses that simply list the tests, or that describe the principles of training without connecting them to assessment data, should not exceed 3–4.","src":"new","point":"Explain how exercise assessment can assist in developing training programs","aim":"Whole chain — baseline, strengths and weaknesses, thresholds, goals, individualisation within a group, and the limitations of assessment","stim":"<div class=\"stimbox extract\"><div class=\"stimlabel\">SCENARIO</div><p>A state under-19 netball squad of 12 players completes a pre-season testing battery including the Yo-Yo Intermittent Recovery Test, a vertical jump test, a 5 m and 20 m sprint, and 1RM testing for the squat. The results vary widely across the squad. The squad trains together four times per week and the coach must prepare one program for the group.</p></div>"},{"id":"NEW-SA-040","year":null,"n":40,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.1"],"stem":"Outline how the principles of balance and stability can be applied to improve performance in ONE named sport-specific movement.","criteria":"• Provides characteristics and features of how balance and stability principles apply to a named sport-specific movement, referring to base of support and/or centre of gravity and/or line of gravity, and links this to improved performance (3)\n• Provides characteristics of how balance and stability apply to a named movement, with a limited link to performance (2)\n• Provides some relevant information about balance and/or stability (1)","sample":"In a rugby league defensive tackle, the defender lowers the hips and widens the stance immediately before contact. Widening the stance enlarges the base of support and lowering the hips lowers the centre of gravity, so the line of gravity remains well inside the base when the attacking player applies force. This makes the defender more difficult to move off balance, which improves the effectiveness of the tackle and reduces the chance of being knocked off the feet and injured.","expl":"Tests balance and stability as one of the biomechanical principles named in FA2.5.1. A mid-band response names the principles but leaves them abstract ('a low centre of gravity helps you stay balanced'). A top-band response is anchored in a specific named movement and shows the causal chain — the change to the base of support or centre of gravity, the effect on stability, and the resulting performance benefit. Any sport is acceptable provided the mechanics are correct.","src":"new","point":"Explain how biomechanics can be used to develop efficient movements for sustained movement and improved performance","aim":"Balance and stability — base of support, centre of gravity, line of gravity"},{"id":"NEW-SA-041","year":null,"n":41,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.1"],"stem":"Explain how the application of force, including summation of forces, can be used to develop a more efficient throwing or striking movement.","criteria":"• Provides a sound explanation of how force is applied and summed sequentially in a named throwing or striking movement, showing clearly how this produces a more efficient and effective movement (4)\n• Provides an explanation of the application of force and/or summation of forces in a named movement, with some link to efficiency (3)\n• Describes the application of force and/or summation of forces in a movement (2)\n• Provides some relevant information about force (1)","sample":"In a tennis serve, force is applied sequentially from the largest and most proximal body parts to the smallest and most distal. The legs drive against the ground, generating a ground reaction force; the hips then rotate, followed by the trunk, shoulder, elbow and finally the wrist and hand. Each segment begins its contribution as the previous segment reaches maximum velocity, so the velocities add and the racquet head reaches a far higher speed than the arm alone could produce. This means the required racquet speed is generated by the whole body rather than by maximal effort from the shoulder and arm, which lowers the muscular effort and stress on any single joint. The server can therefore repeat the serve at a high standard across a long match with less fatigue and a lower risk of overuse injury to the shoulder.","expl":"Targets force application and summation of forces. A mid-band response lists body parts in order without explaining the timing or why sequencing produces greater velocity. A top-band response makes the mechanism explicit — sequential, correctly timed contributions from proximal to distal, velocities accumulating — and then converts that into an efficiency argument (less effort per repetition, reduced joint stress, sustained quality over time), which is the emphasis of the content point.","src":"new","point":"Explain how biomechanics can be used to develop efficient movements for sustained movement and improved performance","aim":"Force — application of force, summation of forces, Newton's laws in sport-specific movements"},{"id":"NEW-SA-042","year":null,"n":42,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.1"],"stem":"Compare how an understanding of fluid mechanics can be used to improve efficiency in swimming and in cycling.","criteria":"• Provides a detailed comparison of how fluid mechanics is applied to improve efficiency in both swimming and cycling, identifying clear similarities and difference(s), supported by relevant examples (5)\n• Provides a comparison of how fluid mechanics applies to both activities, with some similarities and/or difference(s) and examples (4)\n• Describes how fluid mechanics applies to both activities, or provides a detailed description for one (3)\n• Describes how fluid mechanics applies to one activity (2)\n• Provides some relevant information about drag and/or streamlining (1)","sample":"In both swimming and cycling the athlete moves through a fluid and must overcome drag, so in both cases reducing the frontal surface area presented to the fluid lowers the propulsive force required to hold a given speed, which conserves energy and allows the pace to be sustained. A swimmer holds the hips high, the head in line with the spine and the body flat and elongated, particularly off each wall, so that form drag is minimised; a cyclist adopts a low, tucked position on the drops or aero bars with the back flattened and elbows narrow to achieve the same reduction. The main difference is the density of the fluid and therefore the relative importance of drag. Water is far denser than air, so drag is the dominant resistance at all swimming speeds and even slow swimming demands streamlining, while for a cyclist air resistance only becomes the dominant force at higher speeds, meaning aerodynamic positioning matters most in time trialling and on flat, fast sections rather than when climbing. A further difference is that the swimmer must also manage wave drag at the surface, whereas the cyclist can reduce drag by drafting behind another rider.","expl":"Targets fluid mechanics across two activities and requires genuine comparison, not two separate descriptions. Mid-band responses describe streamlining in each sport side by side. Top-band responses explicitly draw out the shared principle (reducing frontal area lowers drag and therefore energy cost) and at least one substantive difference — most commonly the density of the fluid and the speed at which drag becomes the limiting resistance, or drafting and wave drag. Markers should accept any accurate difference that is properly explained.","src":"new","point":"Explain how biomechanics can be used to develop efficient movements for sustained movement and improved performance","aim":"Fluid mechanics — drag and streamlining in swimming and cycling"},{"id":"NEW-SA-043","year":null,"n":43,"part":"","marks":6,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.1"],"stem":"Analyse how biomechanical analysis of a functional movement can be used to reduce the risk of injury while maintaining performance over a sustained period.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive analysis, drawing out and relating the biomechanical principles evident in a named functional movement, the injury risk they create or reduce, and the consequences for sustaining performance over time\n• Provides relevant examples (6)\n• Provides a sound analysis of the relationship between biomechanical principles in a named functional movement and injury risk and/or sustained performance\n• Provides examples (5)\n• Explains how biomechanical principles apply to a functional movement and relate to injury risk or sustained performance (4)\n• Describes how biomechanical principles apply to a functional movement (3)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of biomechanics and/or injury risk in movement (2)\n• Provides some relevant information (1)","sample":"Repetitive lifting in a warehouse is a functional movement in which biomechanical analysis can substantially reduce injury risk. Video or observational analysis typically shows workers lifting with a flexed spine and the load held away from the body. Mechanically, the trunk acts as a lever with the lumbar spine as the fulcrum; when the load sits away from the body the resistance arm is long, so the torque the back extensors must overcome is large and the compressive force on the intervertebral discs rises sharply. Because a warehouse worker may repeat this hundreds of times per shift, that repeated loading accumulates and the movement becomes a leading cause of overuse soft tissue injury to the lower back. Analysis leads to a corrected technique: the worker widens the stance to increase the base of support, squats to bring the centre of gravity closer to the load, keeps the spine neutral, and holds the load close to the body so the resistance arm is short. This shifts the work to the large, strong muscles of the legs and hips, which are far better suited to producing repeated force than the smaller spinal extensors, and it means the same load is moved with less muscular effort and less joint stress. The consequence for sustained performance is significant. Lower effort per lift slows the rate of fatigue across the shift, and because technique typically deteriorates as fatigue builds, reducing the energy cost of each repetition also protects technique later in the day, when injuries are most likely to occur. Over a longer period, fewer overuse injuries mean fewer days lost and a more consistent work capacity. The same analysis applies to sport-specific movement — for example, correcting an overstriding gait in a distance runner removes a braking ground reaction force at each footstrike, which lowers both the energy cost of running and the repeated impact loading associated with tibial stress injury.","expl":"Targets the injury-reduction half of the glossary definition and the 'functional movements' Example. A mid-band response describes correct technique as a rule ('bend the knees, keep your back straight') without the mechanics. A top-band response explains why the technique works using named principles — lever arms and mechanical advantage, base of support and centre of gravity, force distribution to larger muscle groups — and then relates that mechanism to both injury risk and the capacity to sustain the movement over time, ideally noting the fatigue–technique–injury relationship. Any functional or sport-specific movement is acceptable.","src":"new","point":"Explain how biomechanics can be used to develop efficient movements for sustained movement and improved performance","aim":"Levers and mechanical advantage, balance and stability, force application in functional movements and injury reduction"},{"id":"NEW-SA-044","year":null,"n":44,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.1"],"stem":"Explain how biomechanics can be used to develop efficient movements for sustained movement and improved performance in ONE physical activity of your choice.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive explanation of how a range of biomechanical principles are applied to develop efficient movement in the chosen physical activity, clearly relating efficiency to both sustained movement and improved performance\n• Provides detailed examples\n• Presents a logical and cohesive response (8)\n• Provides a sound explanation of how biomechanical principles are applied to develop efficient movement in the chosen activity, with links to sustained movement and/or improved performance\n• Provides examples (6–7)\n• Describes how biomechanical principles apply to the chosen activity, with some link to efficiency or performance\n• Provides example(s) (4–5)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of one or more biomechanical principles and/or efficient movement (2–3)\n• Provides some relevant information (1)","sample":"Biomechanics is the application of the fundamental principles of physics to human movement, and in a 2000 m rowing event it is used to make each stroke produce more boat speed for less energy, so that a high standard of performance can be held for the full race. The most important application is impulse. Impulse is force multiplied by the time over which it is applied, and it equals the change in momentum of the boat. Analysis of the drive phase shows that a rower who applies force through the full length of the stroke, from a well-compressed catch to a complete finish, generates a greater impulse per stroke than a rower applying a similar peak force through a short, rushed drive. The result is that the same boat speed can be produced at a lower stroke rate, which reduces the number of muscular contractions per minute and slows the accumulation of fatigue across the race. Summation of forces determines how that force is generated. The drive begins with the legs, the largest and strongest muscle group, pressing against the footplate; the trunk then swings back, and finally the arms draw the handle through, each segment contributing as the preceding one reaches maximum velocity. Sequencing the movement in this proximal-to-distal order means the large muscles do most of the work, so the smaller muscles of the arms and shoulders are not overloaded early in the race and are still available for the final sprint. It also reduces the repeated stress on the lumbar spine and the ribs that produces the overuse injuries common in rowers, which matters for performance across a season and not only within one race. Newton's third law explains where the propulsion comes from: the blade pushes against the water and the water pushes back with an equal and opposite force, so technical work focuses on locking the blade in the water at the catch so that force is transmitted to the boat rather than lost in slippage. Fluid mechanics matters as well, since the hull must overcome drag that rises steeply with speed; a crew that keeps the boat level and minimises movement during the recovery phase reduces the boat's check and the drag it must overcome, again lowering the energy cost of each stroke. Finally, balance and stability shape the recovery: the crew must move up the slide together with the centre of gravity kept over the midline of a narrow base of support, because any lateral movement destabilises the hull and wastes energy in corrections. Taken together, these principles improve performance directly by increasing boat speed per stroke, and they support sustained movement by lowering the energy cost and joint stress of each repetition, so technique holds together in the final 500 m when fatigue is greatest.","expl":"This is the full content point and uses NESA's own verb. A mid-band response typically names two principles and defines them accurately but treats them as physics content, without connecting them to the specific movement or to sustaining effort. A top-band response is built around one clearly chosen activity, applies a range of principles (impulse and momentum, summation of forces, Newton's laws, fluid mechanics, balance and stability), and — critically — separates the two outcomes the syllabus names: improved performance (more output per effort) and sustained movement (lower energy cost and joint stress per repetition, so quality is maintained under fatigue). Responses that address only the performance half should be capped in the 6–7 band. Any physical activity, sport-specific movement or functional movement is acceptable.","src":"new","point":"Explain how biomechanics can be used to develop efficient movements for sustained movement and improved performance","aim":"Whole content point — biomechanical principles integrated across a physical activity for efficiency, sustained movement and improved performance"},{"id":"NEW-SA-045","year":null,"n":45,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.3"],"stem":"Outline how a Primary Health Network works with non-government organisations to deliver person-centred healthcare in a local community.","criteria":"• Provides a sound outline of how a Primary Health Network works with non-government organisations to deliver person-centred healthcare — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the relationship between a Primary Health Network and non-government organisations — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Primary Health Networks are Commonwealth-funded organisations that assess the health needs of their region and commission services rather than delivering all of them directly. Where a PHN identifies a gap, such as a shortage of after-hours mental health support, it can fund a non-government organisation already trusted in that community to deliver the service and link it to local general practices. Because the service is planned around needs identified in that particular community and is connected to the person's GP, the individual receives care built around them rather than having to navigate separate, unconnected services.","expl":"Tests the collaboration mechanism at the heart of FA1.2.3 — government funding, non-government delivery. A 3-mark response names the PHN as a government-funded commissioner, names a non-government provider role, and closes the loop by saying why this is person-centred (needs identified locally, care coordinated around the individual). A 2-mark response typically says the PHN 'gives money to charities' without the needs-assessment or the person-centred link.","src":"new","point":"Outline how government and non-government organisations collaborate to provide person-centred healthcare","aim":"Primary Health Networks commissioning services from non-government organisations"},{"id":"NEW-SA-046","year":null,"n":46,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.3"],"stem":"Explain how collaboration between government and non-government organisations supports a person living with a chronic condition. Use an example to support your answer.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation, showing clearly how government and non-government organisations work together to support a person with a chronic condition, supported by a relevant example — 4\n• Provides a sound explanation of how government and non-government organisations support a person with a chronic condition, with some reference to an example — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of government and/or non-government roles in supporting a person with a chronic condition — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"A woman diagnosed with breast cancer is supported by both sectors working together. Government funding means her GP can prepare a chronic disease management plan under the MBS, giving her Medicare-subsidised allied health visits, while her surgery and chemotherapy are delivered through the public hospital and her medicines subsidised through the PBS. Alongside this, the Cancer Council provides a telephone support line, subsidised accommodation when she travels for treatment, and peer support groups, while her hospital social worker refers her to those NGO services as part of discharge planning. The collaboration matters because government funds and delivers the clinical treatment, whereas the NGO fills the practical, emotional and transport gaps that clinical services do not cover. This produces coordinated, person-centred care in which her whole situation, not only her tumour, is managed.","expl":"Targets 'how they collaborate', not 'what each does'. A top-band answer shows the interface between sectors — a referral, a shared care plan, a discharge link — and explains why the combination helps the individual. A mid-band answer lists government services in one paragraph and NGO services in another without ever showing them connecting, which is description rather than explanation.","src":"new","point":"Outline how government and non-government organisations collaborate to provide person-centred healthcare","aim":"Care coordination for chronic and complex conditions across government and non-government providers"},{"id":"NEW-SA-047","year":null,"n":47,"part":"","marks":6,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.3"],"stem":"Analyse how government and non-government organisations collaborate to provide person-centred healthcare for people living in rural and remote Australia.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough analysis, clearly drawing out the relationships between government and non-government organisations and the implications for person-centred healthcare in rural and remote Australia, supported by relevant examples — 6\n• Provides a sound analysis of how government and non-government organisations work together in rural and remote healthcare, with relevant examples — 4–5\n• Describes ways government and non-government organisations provide healthcare in rural and remote areas — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"People in rural and remote Australia face distance, workforce shortages and limited specialist services, so no single provider can deliver care built around the individual and collaboration becomes essential. The Commonwealth funds the Royal Flying Doctor Service, a non-government organisation, to run primary care clinics, telehealth consultations and aeromedical retrieval; the government supplies funding and regulation while the RFDS supplies the aircraft, clinicians and local knowledge, and neither could achieve the coverage alone. Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisations show the same relationship in a different form: they are governed by their own communities but largely government funded, which allows care that is culturally safe and shaped by community priorities rather than imposed from outside. Primary Health Networks sit above this, assessing regional need and commissioning whichever local providers — an ACCHO, a charity, a private allied health group — can best fill the identified gap, so services follow the need rather than the need following the service.\n\nThe implication for the individual is continuity. A man in a remote town with poorly controlled diabetes may see a visiting RFDS GP, have his eyes screened during an outreach visit, receive dietary and podiatry support commissioned by the PHN, and be supported by Diabetes Australia resources, with all of it recorded in My Health Record so each provider sees the same information. Collaboration also has limits: services are funded in short cycles, staff turnover is high and outreach visits may be infrequent, so care can still be fragmented, and the person must be genuinely consulted about the plan or the collaboration coordinates providers rather than serving the patient. On balance, collaboration substantially improves access and cultural safety in rural and remote Australia, but it delivers person-centred care only where the individual's goals actually drive the arrangement.","expl":"A top-band response identifies the relationships between the parties (who funds, who delivers, who commissions), applies them to a named rural or remote context, and draws implications — including limitations — for the individual receiving care. A mid-band response correctly names the RFDS, ACCHOs and PHNs and says what each does, but treats them as a list of separate providers and never analyses the interdependence or its consequences for the person.","src":"new","point":"Outline how government and non-government organisations collaborate to provide person-centred healthcare","aim":"Government–non-government collaboration for rural and remote populations (RFDS, ACCHOs, PHNs)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-048","year":null,"n":48,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.5"],"stem":"Describe how complementary healthcare approaches can be used as a preventative measure.","criteria":"• Provides a sound description of how complementary healthcare approaches can be used as a preventative measure, with reference to an example — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the use of complementary healthcare approaches to prevent ill health — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Complementary approaches are used preventatively when a person has no diagnosed condition but uses them to maintain health and reduce future risk. For example, a person may use a health app to set daily step and sleep goals, supporting physical activity levels and helping to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes. Others use yoga or meditation regularly to manage stress before it affects their mental health, or consult a naturopath for dietary advice aimed at maintaining wellbeing rather than treating an illness.","expl":"Checks that students can separate the preventative sub-bullet from the treatment sub-bullet. A 3-mark response makes the 'before ill health develops' framing explicit and gives a concrete example drawn from the syllabus examples (health apps and websites, naturopathy). A 2-mark response gives an example but slides into describing treatment of an existing condition, losing the preventative distinction.","src":"new","point":"Explain complementary healthcare approaches","aim":"As a preventative measure"},{"id":"NEW-SA-049","year":null,"n":49,"part":"","marks":4,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.5"],"stem":"Explain why it is important for a patient to tell their treating doctor about any complementary health products they are using.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of the importance of disclosing complementary product use to a treating doctor, showing clear reasons and consequences — 4\n• Provides a sound explanation of why disclosure to a treating doctor is important — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding that complementary products may affect medical treatment — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Complementary products contain active ingredients, so they can interact with prescribed medicines. A herbal preparation may increase or reduce the effect of an anticoagulant such as warfarin, raising the risk of bleeding or of a clot, and some supplements affect how the liver clears prescription drugs. If the doctor does not know the product is being taken, an unexpected result may be wrongly attributed to the prescribed medicine and the dose changed unnecessarily. Disclosure also lets the doctor advise on timing, on stopping products before surgery, and on whether the product's claimed benefit is supported by evidence. Because most complementary medicines are bought over the counter without any consultation, and because the TGA does not individually assess the efficacy of listed products, the treating doctor is often the only person able to see the patient's whole treatment picture and keep it safe.","expl":"The distinguishing feature of a top-band answer is causal reasoning about consequences — interaction, masked or misattributed effects, altered dosing, surgical risk — rather than a general statement that 'the doctor should know everything'. Mid-band responses assert that products 'might not mix well' without explaining the mechanism or what could go wrong clinically.","src":"new","point":"Explain complementary healthcare approaches","aim":"As a supplement to other medical treatments — disclosure and interactions"},{"id":"NEW-SA-050","year":null,"n":50,"part":"","marks":6,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.5"],"stem":"Assess the usefulness of health apps and websites as a complementary healthcare approach for Australians.","criteria":"• Makes a clear judgement about the usefulness of health apps and websites as a complementary healthcare approach, supported by thorough reasoning and relevant examples — 6\n• Makes a judgement about the usefulness of health apps and websites, supported by sound reasoning and relevant examples — 4–5\n• Describes features, benefits and/or limitations of health apps and websites — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Health apps and websites are among the most widely used complementary health products in Australia, and their usefulness depends heavily on the quality of the source and how they are used alongside medical care. Their strengths are access and engagement: an app that tracks steps, sleep or food intake gives immediate feedback and can support behaviour change preventatively, while reputable websites such as healthdirect or the Heart Foundation give free, evidence-based information at any hour, which is valuable for people in rural areas or those who cannot easily attend appointments. Used as a supplement to medical treatment, apps can help someone with hypertension log blood pressure readings between appointments, giving the GP better information than recall alone, and My Health Record allows results and medicines to be shared across providers.\n\nThe limitations are significant. There is no requirement that every app or website be accurate, and commercial apps may promote supplements or programs the developer sells, creating a conflict of interest. Only apps making specific therapeutic claims fall under TGA oversight, so most sit outside that regulation, and readings from consumer devices are not diagnostic. Digital literacy and internet access also vary, so the people who could benefit most may use them least, and there is a real risk of self-diagnosis or unnecessary anxiety when symptom-checkers are used in place of a consultation. Overall, health apps and websites are genuinely useful as a complementary approach when the source is credible and the information supports rather than replaces contact with a health professional, but they are of limited and potentially harmful value when used as a substitute for medical advice.","expl":"'Assess' requires a judgement, so the top band is defined by a defensible verdict with the criteria for it made explicit — usefulness is conditional on source credibility and on complementing rather than replacing care. A mid-band answer produces a balanced list of pros and cons but never commits to a judgement, or offers a judgement in the final sentence that is unconnected to the preceding argument.","src":"new","point":"Explain complementary healthcare approaches","aim":"Products and services available — health apps and websites"},{"id":"NEW-SA-051","year":null,"n":51,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.6"],"stem":"Outline how a consumer could assess the credibility of a health website.","criteria":"• Provides a sound outline of several ways a consumer could assess the credibility of a health website — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of ways to judge the credibility of a health website — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"A consumer should check who is responsible for the site and why it exists — government, university and recognised health organisation sites such as healthdirect, the AIHW or the Cancer Council are more credible than sites that exist to sell a product. They should look for the author's qualifications, references to peer-reviewed research and a recent review date, since health advice changes. They should also watch for warning signs, including claims of a cure, advertising or sponsored content mixed with the information, and reliance on testimonials rather than evidence, and confirm the information against a second independent source.","expl":"A 3-mark answer gives multiple distinct criteria — authorship and purpose, evidence and currency, warning signs — rather than one idea restated. Naming an Australian source (healthdirect, AIHW) is a good indicator of a genuinely prepared candidate. A 2-mark answer usually offers only 'check if it is a government site'.","src":"new","point":"Explain the importance of being a critical health consumer","aim":"How do you assess the accuracy and credibility of health information?"},{"id":"NEW-SA-052","year":null,"n":52,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.6"],"stem":"Explain why personal testimonials are not a reliable basis for deciding to use a health product or service. Provide an example to support your answer.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of why testimonials are an unreliable basis for a health decision, showing clear reasoning and supported by a relevant example — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of the limitations of testimonials as a basis for a health decision, with an example — 4\n• Demonstrates some understanding that testimonials are a limited form of evidence — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"A testimonial is one person's account of their own experience, and several features make it unreliable as a basis for a health decision. Testimonials are self-selected: a company publishes the customers who improved and never the ones who did not, so the reader sees a biased sample. There is no comparison group, so improvement cannot be attributed to the product — many conditions such as back pain or a cold improve on their own, and the placebo effect means people often feel better simply from taking action. Testimonials may also be paid or incentivised, as when a social media influencer receives a commission on sales, which creates a conflict of interest.\n\nFor example, a website selling a weight-loss supplement might display twenty before-and-after stories. Those stories say nothing about how many people bought the supplement without result, whether the individuals also changed their diet and exercise, or whether they were paid to appear. A randomised controlled trial published in a peer-reviewed journal would compare the supplement against a placebo across a large group, controlling for these factors, which is why evidence of that kind, rather than testimonials, should guide the decision.","expl":"Top-band responses identify more than one distinct mechanism of unreliability — selection bias, absence of a control, natural improvement or placebo, conflict of interest — and contrast testimonials with a stronger form of evidence. Mid-band responses correctly say a testimonial is 'just one person's opinion' and stop there, without explaining why that undermines the inference or what better evidence would look like.","src":"new","point":"Explain the importance of being a critical health consumer","aim":"How do you know whom to believe? — testimonials versus evidence"},{"id":"NEW-SA-053","year":null,"n":53,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.6"],"stem":"Discuss the importance of being a critical health consumer at a time when health information is increasingly accessed through social media.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough discussion of the importance of being a critical health consumer in a social media environment, considering issues from more than one perspective and drawing out implications, supported by relevant examples — 8\n• Provides a sound discussion of the importance of being a critical health consumer in a social media environment, with relevant examples — 6–7\n• Explains reasons for evaluating health information found on social media — 4–5\n• Describes features of health information on social media and/or ways to evaluate it — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Social media has changed how Australians encounter health information. Advice now arrives unrequested in a feed, from people with no health qualifications, mixed in with entertainment and advertising, which makes the skills of a critical health consumer more important than they were when information came mainly from a GP or a printed pamphlet.\n\nThe central problem is knowing whom to believe. On social media the loudest and most engaging voice is amplified rather than the most accurate, because platforms reward content that provokes a reaction. An influencer promoting a 'detox' program may have hundreds of thousands of followers, a confident manner and a commission on every sale, while a dietitian citing the Australian Dietary Guidelines has a fraction of the reach. A critical consumer therefore asks who is speaking, what qualifications they hold, and what they gain if the advice is followed. Registration can be checked on the AHPRA register, and claims can be tested against independent Australian sources such as healthdirect, the AIHW or the Heart Foundation.\n\nJust as important is knowing what makes evidence strong. Content on social media relies heavily on testimonials and before-and-after images, which are self-selected and uncontrolled, whereas peer-reviewed research and systematic reviews of randomised controlled trials compare a treatment against a control and are checked by other experts before publication. Consumers also need to recognise the persuasive devices used — impressive-sounding proportions drawn from tiny samples, fine print disclosing a weak method, or #ad tags that signal a paid relationship — and to know that a listed complementary medicine has been assessed by the TGA for safety and quality but not individually for effectiveness.\n\nThe consequences of not thinking critically are real. Misinformation can lead people to delay effective treatment, to spend money on products that do nothing, or to take supplements that interact dangerously with prescribed medicines. There are protections: Australian Consumer Law prohibits false and misleading claims and the ACCC can penalise businesses, the TGA regulates therapeutic goods and their advertising, and AHPRA regulates registered practitioners. However, these operate largely after the fact and struggle with overseas sellers and anonymous accounts, so regulation cannot substitute for consumer judgement.\n\nThere is another side. Social media has also widened access to credible health information, allowing government agencies and organisations such as the Cancer Council to reach young people and hard-to-reach groups, and letting patients find peer support they would otherwise lack. The issue is not the platform but the reader's ability to distinguish sources. Being a critical health consumer — checking credentials, identifying conflicts of interest, demanding evidence rather than anecdote, and verifying against an independent source before acting — is what allows a person to gain the benefit of that access while protecting their health and their money.","expl":"An 8-mark 'discuss' must present issues from more than one perspective. The strongest responses treat social media as both a risk and a genuine benefit, integrate all three sub-bullets (whom to believe, what you need to know, how to assess accuracy and credibility), name specific Australian mechanisms (AHPRA, TGA, ACCC/Australian Consumer Law, healthdirect, AIHW) and identify consequences of failing to think critically. A 6–7 response covers the risks well with good examples but is one-sided or omits the regulatory context; a 4–5 response explains why evaluation matters but stays generic, with no Australian institutions and no discussion of limits.","src":"new","point":"Explain the importance of being a critical health consumer","aim":"What do you need to know to make informed decisions? — social media, misinformation and consumer protections"},{"id":"NEW-SA-054","year":null,"n":54,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.7"],"stem":"Describe TWO challenges facing the delivery of healthcare services in rural and remote Australia.","criteria":"• Provides characteristics and features of TWO distinct challenges to healthcare delivery in rural and remote Australia (3)\n• Provides characteristics and features of ONE challenge, and identifies a second, OR describes TWO challenges in limited detail (2)\n• Identifies a challenge facing rural and remote healthcare delivery (1)","sample":"One challenge is maldistribution of the health workforce. Rural and remote communities struggle to recruit and retain GPs, nurses, allied health professionals and specialists, so services depend on visiting or locum staff, continuity of care is broken and some towns have no resident doctor at all. A second challenge is distance and access. Patients often travel hundreds of kilometres for diagnostic imaging, specialist appointments, cancer treatment or maternity services, which adds travel and accommodation costs on top of any out-of-pocket fee. This means people delay or forgo care and present later with more advanced illness, contributing to the poorer health outcomes and lower life expectancy of people living outside major cities.","expl":"This tests the rural and remote sub-strand of FA1.2.7 at recall/description level. A mid-band response names two challenges (for example 'not enough doctors' and 'long distances') without developing them. A top-band response gives the features of each — who is affected, what the service gap actually looks like, and the consequence for the patient or the system — and uses correct terminology such as maldistribution, continuity of care and out-of-pocket cost. Markers should accept other defensible challenges (limited access to bulk-billing, thin allied health and mental health services, reliance on telehealth where connectivity is poor, small hospitals without surgical or maternity capacity).","src":"new","point":"Investigate the current and emerging changes and challenges to the healthcare system","aim":"Rural and remote service delivery"},{"id":"NEW-SA-055","year":null,"n":55,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.7"],"stem":"Explain how the shift towards community and home-based care is changing the function of hospitals in Australia.","criteria":"• Provides a detailed explanation of the shift towards community and home-based care and clearly relates cause and effect to a changed function of hospitals, supported by relevant examples (5)\n• Provides an explanation of how the shift towards community and home-based care affects the function of hospitals, with some supporting detail (4)\n• Describes the shift towards community and home-based care and its effect on hospitals (3)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of community or home-based care OR of the function of hospitals (2)\n• Provides some relevant information (1)","sample":"Australia's healthcare system is moving care out of the hospital and into the community because an ageing population and a rising chronic disease burden have created demand that inpatient beds alone cannot meet. Models such as hospital-in-the-home, virtual care, community nursing, chronic disease management plans through general practice, and home care packages under My Aged Care allow people with conditions like heart failure, COPD or diabetes to be monitored and treated at home. Because much of this ongoing care no longer occurs on a ward, the function of hospitals is changing: they are increasingly concentrated on acute, emergency, surgical and complex specialist care, and on short-stay and day-only procedures, rather than on long inpatient admissions for conditions that can be managed elsewhere. This has consequences in both directions. Reducing potentially preventable hospitalisations frees inpatient and emergency department capacity for genuinely acute demand and reduces expenditure, since a hospital bed day is the most expensive form of care. However, hospitals now also function as coordinators — discharge planning, outreach teams and virtual care hubs are run from the hospital — so the boundary between hospital and community care is blurring rather than disappearing.","expl":"This targets the NESA Example 'function of hospitals' and the shift to community and home-based care. A mid-band response describes the shift and asserts that hospitals become 'less busy'. A top-band response explains the causal chain — demand pressure and cost drive the shift, the shift removes a class of care from the ward, and hospitals therefore specialise in acute and complex care while acquiring a new coordinating role — and supports it with named Australian models (hospital-in-the-home, virtual care, My Aged Care, GP chronic disease management). Reference to reduced potentially preventable hospitalisations is a strong discriminator.","src":"new","point":"Investigate the current and emerging changes and challenges to the healthcare system","aim":"Shift from hospital to community and home-based care; function of hospitals (NESA Example)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-056","year":null,"n":56,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Health","subs":["The healthcare system"],"sids":["FA1.2.7"],"stem":"Assess the impact of privatisation on the Australian healthcare system. In your answer, refer to both equity of access and healthcare expenditure.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive judgement of the impact of privatisation on the Australian healthcare system, clearly addressing both equity of access and expenditure, and weighing benefits against limitations using well-chosen examples (7–8)\n• Provides a judgement of the impact of privatisation, addressing both equity of access and expenditure, with relevant examples; the judgement may be uneven across the two aspects (5–6)\n• Describes impacts of privatisation on access and/or expenditure, with limited judgement (3–4)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of privatisation in the Australian healthcare system (2)\n• Provides some relevant information (1)","sample":"Privatisation refers to the growing role of the private sector in funding and delivering healthcare — the expansion of private hospitals and day surgeries, contracted private providers within public systems, and government support for private health insurance through the rebate and the Medicare Levy Surcharge. Its impact on the Australian healthcare system is mixed. In terms of capacity, privatisation increases the total volume of care available. A large share of elective surgery is now performed in private hospitals, and every hip replacement or cataract procedure done privately is one that does not sit on a public waiting list. Private facilities also absorbed public patients during the COVID-19 pandemic under viability arrangements, which demonstrates that private capacity can be a genuine national asset. In terms of equity of access, however, privatisation creates a two-tiered pattern of care. Access to the faster private pathway depends on the ability to afford premiums, excesses and medical gaps, so people on low incomes, and those in rural and remote areas where private facilities are scarce, remain reliant on the public system and wait longer for the same procedure. This directly conflicts with the equity principle underpinning Medicare, that access should be based on need rather than capacity to pay. Privatisation can also draw specialists and nurses towards better-remunerated private work, worsening workforce shortages in public hospitals. In terms of expenditure, the effect is contested. Private funding shifts some cost from government to individuals and insurers, which relieves pressure on public budgets. But the private health insurance rebate is itself a very large item of government expenditure, and out-of-pocket costs in Australia are high by international standards, so total health expenditure is not necessarily reduced — it is redistributed, with a growing share carried by households. On balance, privatisation increases the capacity and choice available within the healthcare system, but it does so in a way that weakens equity of access and shifts, rather than reduces, cost. Its net contribution therefore depends on how effectively government regulates the sector and protects the capacity of the public system.","expl":"This is an 8-mark judgement question on the NESA Example 'privatisation'. A mid-band response lists advantages and disadvantages of private health care — shorter waits, choice of doctor, cost — without a sustained judgement, and typically addresses access much more strongly than expenditure. A top-band response makes and sustains a judgement, explicitly weighs capacity and choice against equity, recognises that expenditure is redistributed rather than removed (naming the private health insurance rebate as government spending and out-of-pocket costs as household spending), and links back to the equity principle of Medicare. Recognition of the workforce consequence, or of the pandemic viability arrangements, indicates the top band. Markers should not require the specific examples in the sample, only defensible Australian ones.","src":"new","point":"Investigate the current and emerging changes and challenges to the healthcare system","aim":"Privatisation and private-sector growth (NESA Example)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-057","year":null,"n":57,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.2"],"stem":"Describe how the results of a skinfold assessment and a sit-and-reach test could be used by a recreational participant to improve their health.","criteria":"• Provides characteristics and features of how BOTH results could be used by a recreational participant to improve health (3)\n• Provides characteristics and features of how ONE result could be used, with limited reference to the other (2)\n• Identifies a use of a fitness test result for a recreational participant (1)","sample":"A skinfold assessment gives a recreational participant an estimate of body composition and a baseline that can be retested after several weeks. Because it tracks change in body fat directly rather than relying on scale weight, it can show progress even when weight has not changed, which supports adherence and motivation; a high result can also prompt a conversation about the Australian Dietary Guidelines and about increasing physical activity to reduce chronic disease risk. A sit-and-reach result identifies limited flexibility in the lower back and hamstrings. The participant can use this to justify including targeted stretching in their program, aiming to improve range of motion, reduce the risk of injury and make everyday movements such as bending and lifting easier. Repeating both tests provides evidence of improvement and helps the participant set realistic short-term goals.","expl":"This targets two of the named test types in a recreational context. A mid-band response describes what each test measures without saying how the result would be used. A top-band response is explicit about use — baseline, goal setting, motivation, program modification and retesting — and connects the result to a health outcome such as reduced chronic disease risk or injury prevention. Simply naming body fat percentage and hamstring flexibility is not sufficient for full marks.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the use of performance/fitness testing for recreational participants and elite athletes to improve their health, participation and performance","aim":"Skinfolds/body composition and sit-and-reach flexibility testing for recreational participants"},{"id":"NEW-SA-058","year":null,"n":58,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.2"],"stem":"Compare the use of performance/fitness testing by recreational participants with its use by elite athletes.","criteria":"• Provides a detailed comparison, clearly showing similarities and differences in the purpose, protocols and use of results of fitness testing for recreational participants and elite athletes, supported by relevant examples (5)\n• Provides a comparison of fitness testing for recreational participants and elite athletes with some supporting detail (4)\n• Describes fitness testing for recreational participants and for elite athletes, with limited comparison (3)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of fitness testing for one group (2)\n• Provides some relevant information (1)","sample":"Both groups use fitness testing to obtain objective baseline data, to monitor change over time and to guide what their training program should contain, and both may use the same broad components of fitness such as aerobic capacity, strength and flexibility. The purpose, however, differs. For recreational participants, testing is primarily a health screen and a motivational tool — resting blood pressure, waist circumference, skinfolds, a sit-and-reach and a submaximal predicted VO2 max establish that it is safe to begin, identify risk factors and give visible evidence of improvement that supports ongoing participation. For elite athletes, testing is performance diagnostics and selection: it identifies the limiting factor in performance, tracks the effect of a training phase, informs peaking and tapering, and contributes to squad selection and return-to-play decisions. The protocols differ accordingly. Recreational testing favours submaximal, non-invasive, low-cost field tests, such as a predicted 1RM from repetitions to fatigue or the multi-stage beep test. Elite testing is often maximal, invasive and laboratory-based — direct VO2 max with gas analysis, incremental treadmill testing with blood lactate sampling, a Wingate test for anaerobic power — or highly sport-specific in the field, such as the Yo-Yo Intermittent Recovery Test for football. Elite protocols are also repeated far more frequently and compared against normative data for the sport. Finally, the results are used differently: a recreational participant's result adjusts their program and their goals, whereas an elite athlete's result may change their training zones, their position in a squad, or whether they compete at all.","expl":"This is the central discrimination the content point demands. A mid-band response describes each group separately — 'recreational people test to get fit, elite athletes test to perform' — without integrating the comparison, and usually addresses only purpose. A top-band response compares across several explicit dimensions (purpose, protocol and invasiveness, cost and equipment, frequency, how results are used), acknowledges genuine similarities as well as differences, and names specific tests appropriate to each group. Correct use of a NESA Example test (Yo-Yo or Wingate) in the elite half is a strong indicator of the top band.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the use of performance/fitness testing for recreational participants and elite athletes to improve their health, participation and performance","aim":"Recreational versus elite — purpose, protocol, cost and use of results"},{"id":"NEW-SA-059","year":null,"n":59,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Exercise assessment"],"sids":["FA2.1.2"],"stem":"Discuss the use of performance/fitness testing to improve the performance of elite athletes. In your answer, refer to at least TWO specific tests.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive discussion, identifying and clearly explaining both the benefits and the limitations of performance/fitness testing for elite athletes, with sustained reference to at least TWO appropriate named tests and how results are used (7–8)\n• Provides a discussion of the benefits and limitations of performance/fitness testing for elite athletes, referring to TWO named tests with some supporting detail (5–6)\n• Describes the use of performance/fitness testing for elite athletes, with reference to at least one named test; limited discussion of limitations (3–4)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of a fitness test used with athletes (2)\n• Provides some relevant information (1)","sample":"For elite athletes, performance/fitness testing is a diagnostic tool: it identifies the physiological quality that is limiting performance, establishes the training zones and loads that will develop it, and provides objective evidence of whether the training program is working. The tests selected must match the physiological and movement demands of the sport. The Yo-Yo Intermittent Recovery Test is used with team-sport athletes such as footballers because its 20 m shuttles separated by 10 seconds of active recovery reproduce the repeated high-intensity efforts of match play. Distance covered gives a sport-specific index of the athlete's capacity to repeat sprints and to recover between them, so a decline across a season can flag inadequate aerobic conditioning or accumulating fatigue, and an improvement after a pre-season block justifies continuing that training. The Wingate test, by contrast, is a 30-second maximal cycle ergometer effort used with athletes whose events depend on brief maximal power output, such as track sprint cyclists. It yields peak power, mean power and a fatigue index, which lets a coach distinguish an athlete who lacks explosive power from one whose problem is a rapid decline in power, and prescribe accordingly. Other tests extend this diagnostic picture: an incremental treadmill test with blood lactate sampling identifies the lactate threshold that sets an endurance athlete's training intensities, direct VO2 max testing with gas analysis quantifies aerobic ceiling, and agility tests such as the Illinois agility run assess change-of-direction ability in court and field sports. Repeated testing also supports periodisation, guiding peaking and tapering, and provides objective criteria for selection and for return to play after injury. There are limitations. Laboratory testing is expensive, time-consuming and often invasive, and requires specialist staff and facilities of the kind available at the Australian Institute of Sport rather than at a club. Maximal tests are fatiguing, so they must be scheduled carefully or they interfere with the training they are meant to inform. No test reproduces competition exactly — a Wingate result does not account for tactics, skill execution, or psychological factors under pressure — and results can be distorted by illness, hydration, motivation and time of day, so a single score should never be read in isolation. Testing also has consequences for the athlete: when results drive selection, they can create anxiety and encourage athletes to train for the test rather than for the sport. Overall, performance/fitness testing makes a substantial contribution to elite performance when the tests chosen are specific to the demands of the sport, are administered under standardised conditions and are repeated over time, but the results are one source of evidence that must be interpreted alongside competition performance and coach observation.","expl":"This uses the syllabus's own verb, 'discuss', and requires both sides of the argument. A mid-band response explains what two tests measure and asserts that testing 'helps athletes improve', staying descriptive. A top-band response does three things the mid-band does not: it links each named test to the specific physiological quality or movement demand of a sport and to a training decision that follows from the result; it discusses limitations (cost, invasiveness, fatigue cost of maximal testing, day-to-day variability, the gap between test and competition, and the pressure created when results drive selection); and it reaches a sustained overall position. The NESA Examples (Yo-Yo, Wingate) should be credited generously, but any appropriate elite test — direct VO2 max, lactate threshold, 1RM, agility, skinfolds — is acceptable provided its use is explained rather than merely named.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the use of performance/fitness testing for recreational participants and elite athletes to improve their health, participation and performance","aim":"Use of specific tests (Yo yo, Wingate, VO2 max, lactate threshold, agility) to improve elite performance"},{"id":"NEW-SA-060","year":null,"n":60,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.1"],"stem":"Outline TWO health and safety considerations a coach must address when designing a training session for a squad of 20 rugby league players that would be less significant when designing a session for a single 400 m runner.","criteria":"• Outlines TWO health and safety considerations and clearly indicates why each is more significant in a group session than an individual session — 3\n• Outlines TWO health and safety considerations relevant to a group training session, OR outlines ONE with clear reference to the group context — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Supervision ratio: with one coach and 20 players, the coach cannot observe every athlete at once, so activities must be organised into supervised stations with clear rules and a qualified first-aid officer present. With a single 400 m runner the coach observes the athlete continuously.\nCollision and mixed-ability risk: contact and competitive drills bring players of different body sizes and fitness levels together, so drills must be graded and players matched appropriately, and rest and rehydration breaks must be set for the least heat-tolerant player. A solo runner is not exposed to contact and can be loaded precisely.","expl":"Tests the 'health and safety considerations' sub-bullet with the individual-versus-group comparison the content point demands. A top-band response names two distinct considerations AND makes the comparative point explicit (why the consideration is heightened by a squad). A mid-band response lists two generic safety considerations — surfaces, equipment, warm-up — that would apply equally to a lone runner, and so never engages with the comparison.","src":"new","point":"Compare aspects that need to be considered when designing a training session for individual and group sports","aim":"health and safety considerations"},{"id":"NEW-SA-061","year":null,"n":61,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.1"],"stem":"Explain how a coach's approach to skill instruction and practice, and to strategies and tactics, differs between a training session for an individual swimmer and a training session for a netball squad.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of how BOTH skill instruction and practice AND strategies and tactics differ between an individual and a group session, with relevant examples — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of how both aspects differ, with examples — 4\n• Provides some understanding of how these aspects differ between individual and group sessions, with example(s) — 3\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of skill instruction or strategies and tactics in a training session — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"With one swimmer the coach has a one-to-one ratio, so instruction is individualised and continuous: the coach can isolate a single technical fault, such as a dropped elbow in the catch phase, use video from the pool deck and give corrective feedback every lap. Practice is largely closed and repetitive because the race environment is predictable. Strategy work is individual — rehearsing pace per 50 m and turn timing for the athlete's own race plan.\nWith a netball squad the coach-to-athlete ratio is poor, so instruction is delivered to the whole group and then broken into small, position-based groups with one correction focus each while the coach rotates. Practice must be open and game-like — modified and small-sided games — because netball skills are performed under pressure from opponents. Strategies and tactics take up far more session time and must be practised collectively: centre-pass set plays, defensive pressure patterns and the on-court communication needed to adjust them, none of which can be rehearsed by an athlete alone.","expl":"Targets two named sub-bullets at once and rewards the comparison. Top-band responses attribute the difference to a cause — the coach-to-athlete ratio and the open versus closed nature of the skills — and give sport-specific examples for both athletes. Mid-band responses describe what happens in each session accurately but treat the two sports separately, without explaining why the coaching approach must differ.","src":"new","point":"Compare aspects that need to be considered when designing a training session for individual and group sports","aim":"skill instruction and practice; strategies and tactics"},{"id":"NEW-SA-062","year":null,"n":62,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.1"],"stem":"A coach conducts a 90-minute session for a 400 m runner on Monday and a 90-minute session for a netball squad of 14 on Tuesday. Compare the aspects the coach needs to consider when designing each of these sessions.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive comparison of the aspects considered when designing a training session for an individual and a group sport, identifying both similarities and differences — 7–8\n• Presents a logical and cohesive response supported by a range of relevant examples drawn from both sports\n• Provides a sound comparison of the aspects considered when designing sessions for an individual and a group sport — 5–6\n• Presents a logical response with relevant examples\n• Provides some comparison of session design for an individual and a group sport, or describes the aspects for both with limited comparison — 3–4\n• Provides example(s)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of aspects considered when designing a training session — 1–2","sample":"Health and safety: both sessions require a hazard check, appropriate equipment and hydration. For the runner the coach can monitor load precisely and adjust in real time; for the squad the coach must supervise 14 athletes at once, grade contested drills for mixed fitness and body size, and set breaks for the least heat-tolerant player.\nAim: both sessions need a goal-specific aim. The runner's aim can be narrow — develop lactate tolerance through 3 × 300 m at 90% of race pace. The squad's aim must serve the whole group — improve defensive pressure in the goal third — with individual sub-aims layered underneath.\nWarm-up and cool-down: both raise body temperature and finish with active recovery. The runner's warm-up is tailored to their event and previous injury history; the squad's is a shared ball-based circuit that also rehearses passing and footwork patterns, using the warm-up time efficiently for 14 athletes.\nSkill instruction and practice: the runner receives continuous individual feedback on a closed skill. The squad receives one whole-group demonstration, then works in position-based groups on open, game-like tasks while the coach rotates between them.\nConditioning: the runner's conditioning is dictated entirely by the 400 m energy-system profile. The squad's must accommodate positional demands — a goalkeeper's short repeated efforts differ from a centre's high-volume running — so stations are used to individualise within the group.\nStrategies and tactics: the runner rehearses a personal race plan. The squad devotes far more time to shared tactics and to the communication needed to adjust them against opponents.\nReflection and evaluation: the runner uses a training diary and split times with the coach. The squad uses a whole-group debrief and video so the coach can evaluate combinations and shared decision-making as well as individual contribution.\nOverall, the same seven aspects are considered for both, but the coach-to-athlete ratio, the need for group cohesion and communication, and the range of fitness levels within a squad mean each aspect is individualised in the runner's session and managed collectively in the squad session.","expl":"This is the full content point. A top-band response works aspect by aspect across both athletes, drawing out similarities as well as differences, and identifies the underlying cause of the differences (ratio, individualisation versus shared drills, positional demands, cohesion). A mid-band response writes two separate descriptions — one session then the other — leaving the marker to make the comparison, and typically covers only three or four aspects. Note that 'compare' requires similarities as well as differences; markers should not reward difference-only responses at the top band.","src":"new","point":"Compare aspects that need to be considered when designing a training session for individual and group sports","aim":"all seven sub-bullets — whole-session comparison"},{"id":"NEW-SA-063","year":null,"n":63,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.2"],"stem":"Describe ONE sub-phase within the pre-season phase of a yearly training program for a group sport.","criteria":"• Provides a description of ONE sub-phase within the pre-season phase, including its purpose and the training undertaken — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of a sub-phase within the pre-season phase — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"The general preparation sub-phase occurs at the start of the pre-season for a rugby league squad. Its purpose is to rebuild the aerobic base and general strength lost during the off-season, so training volume is high and intensity is comparatively low — long continuous runs, circuit training and hypertrophy-based resistance work. This builds the work capacity the squad needs to tolerate the specific, higher-intensity sub-phase that follows, in which repeat-sprint, contact and skill work under fatigue dominate as round one approaches.","expl":"Directly targets the 'sub-phases' sub-bullet, which is under-examined. A top-band response names a recognised sub-phase (general preparation, specific preparation, pre-competition), states its purpose and describes the training characteristics — the volume and intensity relationship. A mid-band response names the sub-phase and gives a general statement about 'building fitness' without linking it to volume, intensity or its position in the pre-season.","src":"new","point":"Compare a yearly training program for an individual and a group sport","aim":"sub-phases"},{"id":"NEW-SA-064","year":null,"n":64,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.2"],"stem":"Explain why peaking and tapering are planned differently for a swimmer preparing for one national championship and for a netball squad competing in a 20-round season followed by finals.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of why peaking and tapering are planned differently for an individual athlete targeting one event and a group sport with a long season, with relevant examples — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of the difference, with examples — 4\n• Provides some understanding of peaking and tapering in relation to individual and group sports, with example(s) — 3\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of peaking or tapering — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"The swimmer has one date that matters, so the whole macrocycle is built towards it. In the 10–14 days before the championship the swimmer tapers — training volume is cut progressively, for example from 60 km to 20 km per week, while race-pace intensity is maintained in short efforts. This dissipates accumulated fatigue and restores muscle glycogen, so fitness is expressed as a single sharp peak on the day of the final.\nThe netball squad cannot do this. It must perform to a high standard every week for 20 rounds, so the in-season plan aims to hold a performance plateau: conditioning is reduced to maintenance level, matches supply much of the load, and the weekly microcycle is built around recovery after each game. The squad uses short mini-tapers — lighter loads in the two or three days before a crucial fixture — and reserves a fuller taper for the finals series, which is the only point in the season where a true peak is planned. Peaking too early, or tapering fully in round five, would leave the squad flat for the remaining rounds.","expl":"Rewards the comparison at the heart of the content point. Top-band responses explain the mechanism of tapering (volume down, intensity maintained, fatigue dissipated, glycogen restored) AND the structural reason a team cannot use it the same way (a long fixture list requiring sustained performance rather than one peak). Mid-band responses define tapering accurately for the swimmer but treat the netball squad only briefly, or assert that teams 'cannot taper' without explaining the mini-taper and finals strategy.","src":"new","point":"Compare a yearly training program for an individual and a group sport","aim":"peaking and tapering"},{"id":"NEW-SA-065","year":null,"n":65,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.2"],"stem":"A 1500 m runner is targeting the national championships in March. A rugby league team plays a season from March to September. Compare the yearly training program of these two athletes.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive comparison of yearly training programs for an individual and a group sport, addressing phases of competition, changes in volume and intensity, peaking and tapering, and sport-specific attributes — 7–8\n• Presents a logical and cohesive response supported by a range of relevant examples from both sports\n• Provides a sound comparison of the yearly training programs of an individual and a group sport, addressing most of these aspects — 5–6\n• Presents a logical response with relevant examples\n• Provides some comparison of yearly training programs, or describes each program with limited comparison — 3–4\n• Provides example(s)\n• Demonstrates a basic understanding of planning a training year — 1–2","sample":"Both programs are periodised into a 12-month macrocycle divided into mesocycles and weekly microcycles, and both move from high-volume, lower-intensity general work towards lower-volume, higher-intensity specific work as competition approaches.\nPhases: the runner's off-season follows the championships, with an extended base-building pre-season through summer of high-volume aerobic running, then a short, intense in-season of lead-up races culminating in March. The team's off-season runs from September, pre-season occupies the summer with heavy conditioning and contact preparation, and the in-season stretches across seven months, during which volume drops to maintenance level and matches provide the load.\nSub-phases: the runner's pre-season moves from general aerobic preparation to specific preparation with race-pace interval work and then a pre-competition sub-phase of sharpening. The team's pre-season moves from general conditioning to specific repeat-effort and contact work, then to trial matches; in-season the microcycle repeats weekly — recovery, load, tactical preparation, taper into the match.\nPeaking and tapering: the runner constructs the year around a single peak and tapers fully for 10–14 days before the championships. The team must sustain a plateau across 20-plus rounds, using short mini-tapers before each match and a fuller taper into finals in September.\nSport-specific attributes: the runner's plan is driven by aerobic capacity and anaerobic (lactic) power, with the closed skill of running economy and pace judgement refined through repetition. The team's plan must develop aerobic capacity, anaerobic capacity, speed, agility, muscular strength and power, and its skill work must be open and game-like — decision-making under pressure, tackling and positional combinations — with conditioning individualised by position.\nOverall, the same periodisation principles are applied to both, but the length of the competition phase and the breadth of fitness components and skills required by a team sport mean the runner's year is shaped around one peak, while the team's is shaped around sustained weekly performance.","expl":"The full content point, examined as the syllabus frames it. Top-band responses cover all four sub-bullets, use the correct terminology (macrocycle, mesocycle, microcycle, taper, phases) and, critically, identify similarities as well as differences before reaching a comparative conclusion. Mid-band responses typically describe the runner's year well and the team's year thinly, or list phases for both without addressing peaking, tapering or sport-specific attributes. Markers should not award the top band for a difference-only response, and should not require the exact cycle terminology if the substance is accurately described.","src":"new","point":"Compare a yearly training program for an individual and a group sport","aim":"phases of competition; sub-phases; peaking and tapering; sport-specific attributes"},{"id":"NEW-SA-066","year":null,"n":66,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.4"],"stem":"Outline how environmental conditions can influence the tactics used in an individual sport.","criteria":"• Outlines clearly how environmental conditions influence the tactics used in an individual sport, with a relevant sport-specific example — 3\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the influence of environmental conditions on tactics — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"In an individual sport such as tennis, environmental conditions directly shape tactics. On a hot, humid Australian summer day the player will shorten points by attacking early and taking the ball on the rise, because long rallies increase heat storage and fatigue. Wind is also a tactical factor: playing into the wind the player uses higher, deeper margins over the net, while with the wind behind they hit flatter and use the slice serve wide. The surface matters as well — a slow clay court favours patient baseline construction, whereas a fast hard court rewards serve and volley.","expl":"This targets the NESA Example 'environmental conditions' at the low-mark end. A 3-mark response makes a clear cause-and-effect link (condition → tactical adjustment) in a named individual sport; a 2-mark response names conditions such as heat or wind but describes their effect on the body or on performance generally rather than on the tactic chosen. Simply listing conditions earns 1 mark. Markers should accept any defensible individual sport, including surfing, cycling, athletics and golf.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the factors that influence how strategies and tactics are applied to individual and group sports","aim":"Environmental conditions — weather, playing surface and venue"},{"id":"NEW-SA-067","year":null,"n":67,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.4"],"stem":"Explain how a coach uses knowledge of their own group's strengths and weaknesses, and those of the opposition, to apply tactics in a team sport.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of how knowledge of both the group's own strengths and weaknesses and those of the opposition informs the tactics applied, supported by clear sport-specific reasoning — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of how knowledge of strengths and weaknesses influences the tactics applied — 4\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the relationship between strengths, weaknesses and tactics — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"A coach begins by matching tactics to what the group can actually execute. A rugby union side with a dominant, experienced pack and an inexperienced backline will apply tactics that keep the ball forward — driving mauls from lineouts, pick-and-drive phases close to the ruck and territorial kicking — because these use the group's strength and protect its weakness by limiting the number of high-risk wide passes the young backs must make.\n\nThe coach then analyses the opposition through notational analysis and video to locate weaknesses to attack and strengths to neutralise. If the opposition lineout is unreliable under pressure but their outside backs are quick in broken play, the coach will contest the lineout aggressively and kick long rather than short, denying the opposition counter-attacking space. Tactics are therefore the product of the interaction between the two sets of strengths and weaknesses, and the coach must adjust them within the match if a planned match-up is not working — for example, moving a defender onto a dangerous ball-runner who is beating the original match-up.","expl":"Directly addresses the NESA Example 'group strengths and weaknesses'. The discriminator between top and mid band is whether the student handles BOTH sides of the equation — their own group and the opposition — and explains why a tactic follows from the analysis, rather than describing what analysis is. Mid-band responses typically describe video analysis or list team strengths without connecting them to a chosen tactic. Reference to in-match adjustment lifts a response into the top band.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the factors that influence how strategies and tactics are applied to individual and group sports","aim":"Group strengths and weaknesses; opposition match-ups"},{"id":"NEW-SA-068","year":null,"n":68,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Individual and group sports"],"sids":["FA2.3.4"],"stem":"Discuss the factors that influence how strategies and tactics are applied in individual sports compared with group sports. In your answer refer to ONE individual sport and ONE group sport.","criteria":"• Discusses comprehensively a range of factors influencing the application of strategies and tactics, drawing out clear similarities and differences between an individual and a group sport, with sustained sport-specific support and a clear judgement about which factors matter most in each context — 7–8\n• Discusses several factors influencing the application of strategies and tactics in both an individual and a group sport, with relevant examples and some comparison — 5–6\n• Provides characteristics and features of factors that influence strategies and tactics in individual and/or group sports, with limited comparison — 3–4\n• Provides some relevant information — 1–2","sample":"Strategies are the overall plan an athlete or team brings to competition, while tactics are the specific decisions used to carry that plan out during play. In both individual and group sports the same broad factors operate — environmental conditions, the opposition, the match situation, fatigue and injury, the rules, and athlete skill and experience — but they operate very differently.\n\nIn an individual sport such as road cycling, environmental conditions and the athlete's own physiological state dominate. A rider facing a mountain stage in extreme heat plans a conservative pacing strategy with disciplined fluid intake, then adjusts tactically as the race develops — sitting in the bunch to reduce workload, attacking on a gradient that suits their strengths, or conceding time when fatigue signals accumulate. Because the athlete alone executes the plan, tactics can change instantly and are limited mainly by that athlete's skill, experience and physical state. Rules such as those governing drafting or race radios further shape what is possible.\n\nIn a group sport such as rugby league, tactics must be executed collectively, so communication, structure and the strengths and weaknesses of every player constrain what can be applied. The coach designs a strategy from analysis of both squads, then tactics respond to the match situation: leading late in a match a team will slow the play-the-ball, kick early in the count and defend field position, while trailing it will chance repeat sets. Regulations create constraints unique to group sports — a limited interchange bench means a coach must plan the rotation of forwards in advance, and once interchanges are exhausted the only remaining tactical lever is tempo. Playing away from home adds crowd noise that disrupts on-field communication, which affects a team far more than an individual competitor.\n\nOverall, the factors overlap but their weighting differs. In individual sports, environmental conditions and the athlete's own capacity are the strongest influences because there is no one else to compensate. In group sports, the interaction of personnel — match-ups, fatigue across a squad, interchange limits and communication under pressure — is the greater determinant, which is why group sports rely on rehearsed structures that individuals can apply more flexibly.","expl":"This is the whole content point, using its own verb. The top band requires genuine comparison sustained across both contexts and a judgement about the relative weight of factors, not two separate mini-essays. Mid-band responses typically describe factors accurately in one sport and mention the other briefly, or list factors without showing how they change the tactic applied. Markers should look for correct use of the strategy/tactic distinction and for at least three or four distinct factors; responses that treat 'strategy' and 'tactic' as synonyms cannot reach the top band.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the factors that influence how strategies and tactics are applied to individual and group sports","aim":"Integrated — individual vs group sports; skill level, match situation, home vs away, rules"},{"id":"NEW-SA-069","year":null,"n":69,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sleep, nutrition and supplements"],"sids":["FA2.4.2"],"stem":"Outline TWO sleep routines an adolescent athlete could use to improve sleep quality before competition.","criteria":"• Outlines clearly TWO sleep routines and how each improves sleep quality — 3\n• Outlines ONE sleep routine and how it improves sleep quality, OR names two routines with limited explanation — 2\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Firstly, the athlete should keep a consistent bed and wake time, including on weekends. A regular schedule stabilises the body clock so sleep onset becomes faster and total sleep time more reliable, which matters most in the nights leading into competition.\n\nSecondly, the athlete should stop using phones, tablets and gaming screens at least an hour before bed and keep them out of the bedroom. Blue light suppresses melatonin release and the content itself is stimulating, both of which delay sleep onset and shorten total sleep.","expl":"Targets the 'routines' sub-bullet, which is the strand least covered in past papers. A 3-mark response gives two distinct routines AND a mechanism or benefit for each. Naming routines only ('go to bed early, no phones') is a 1–2 mark response. Other acceptable routines include a consistent wind-down (shower, stretching, reading), a cool dark quiet bedroom, avoiding caffeine and energy drinks in the afternoon and evening, avoiding late heavy meals, and a short early-afternoon nap of 20–30 minutes rather than a long late nap.","src":"new","point":"Explain how sleep, nutrition and hydration can be used to reduce fatigue and positively influence movement and injury prevention","aim":"Sleep — routines (sleep hygiene, screens, napping)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-070","year":null,"n":70,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sleep, nutrition and supplements"],"sids":["FA2.4.2"],"stem":"Explain how adequate sleep reduces fatigue and contributes to injury prevention in athletes.","criteria":"• Provides a thorough explanation of how adequate sleep reduces fatigue and prevents injury, showing clear cause-and-effect links to physical and neural recovery — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of how adequate sleep reduces fatigue and prevents injury — 4\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the relationship between sleep, fatigue and injury — 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information — 1","sample":"Adequate sleep — 8 to 10 hours per night for adolescents, and often more for athletes in heavy training — is the period in which the greatest physical restoration occurs. During deep sleep the body releases growth hormone, which drives protein synthesis and the repair of muscle tissue damaged in training, and glycogen stores are replenished. Without enough sleep this repair is incomplete, so the athlete begins the next session already fatigued and accumulates residual fatigue across a training week, which is a recognised contributor to overtraining.\n\nSleep also restores the nervous system. Reaction time, attention and decision making all deteriorate with sleep debt, and motor skills learned in training are consolidated during sleep. An under-slept athlete therefore reacts more slowly, controls landing and change-of-direction mechanics less well and makes poorer decisions in contested situations — all of which increase the likelihood of both acute injuries such as ankle and knee sprains and overuse injuries arising from degraded technique under fatigue. Adequate sleep additionally supports immune function, reducing illness that interrupts training. For these reasons sleep is planned in an athlete's program in the same way as training load and nutrition.","expl":"This is the core of the content point and the strand with no past-paper coverage. Top-band responses give BOTH the physical mechanism (growth hormone, tissue repair, glycogen, immune function) and the neural mechanism (reaction time, motor learning consolidation, decision making) and connect them explicitly to injury risk. Mid-band responses assert that 'sleep helps recovery so you get injured less' without the mechanism, or cover only the physical side. Reference to the 8–10 hour guideline is credited but is not sufficient on its own.","src":"new","point":"Explain how sleep, nutrition and hydration can be used to reduce fatigue and positively influence movement and injury prevention","aim":"Sleep — guidelines and its role in reducing fatigue and preventing injury"},{"id":"NEW-SA-071","year":null,"n":71,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sleep, nutrition and supplements"],"sids":["FA2.4.2"],"stem":"Analyse how the planning and monitoring of sleep, nutrition and hydration can be used to reduce fatigue and prevent injury in an athlete preparing for a major competition.","criteria":"• Analyses comprehensively the relationships between the planning and monitoring of sleep, nutrition and hydration, and the reduction of fatigue and prevention of injury, with clear mechanisms and sustained, relevant examples — 7–8\n• Analyses the relationship between planning and/or monitoring of sleep, nutrition and hydration and reduced fatigue and injury, with relevant examples — 5–6\n• Provides characteristics and features of sleep, nutrition and hydration strategies and their effect on fatigue — 3–4\n• Provides some relevant information — 1–2","sample":"Sleep, nutrition and hydration are recovery variables that can be planned and monitored as deliberately as training load, and each contributes to reducing fatigue and preventing injury.\n\nPlanning begins with guidelines. Sleep is planned around the 8–10 hour adolescent recommendation, with bed and wake times set backwards from training and competition times; if the squad trains at 5.30 am, the athlete must plan an earlier bedtime rather than simply losing two hours of sleep, because repeated short nights create sleep debt that slows reaction time and impairs neuromuscular control, increasing the risk of poor landing and cutting mechanics. Travel is planned in the same way — flying east for a national championship, the athlete shifts bed and wake times towards destination time in the days before departure and seeks morning light on arrival. Nutrition is planned against the Australian Dietary Guidelines and sport-specific requirements: carbohydrate is timed to fuel key sessions and replenished with protein within about an hour of finishing to support glycogen resynthesis and muscle repair, and adequate energy availability prevents the fatigue, hormonal disruption and reduced bone density that raise stress-fracture risk. Hydration is planned pre, during and post session, with fluid volumes based on the athlete's measured sweat losses and electrolyte replacement where sessions are long or held in heat.\n\nMonitoring makes the plan self-correcting. A sleep diary or wearable records bed time, wake time and total sleep time, and a morning wellness questionnaire rating sleep quality, soreness, mood and energy allows a coach to detect accumulating fatigue before performance drops. Body mass measured before and after training estimates fluid loss, and urine colour gives a daily hydration check. Food diaries or a dietitian consultation identify shortfalls such as low iron intake, which is common in adolescent female athletes and produces fatigue that training alone cannot explain.\n\nThe three variables interact, which is why they must be managed together. Dehydration and low carbohydrate availability both accelerate fatigue within a session, and fatigue degrades technique, which is a mechanism for both acute and overuse injury. Poor sleep in turn increases appetite dysregulation and reduces the motivation to follow the nutrition and hydration plan. Monitoring data that show declining sleep, rising body mass loss and falling wellness scores together give the coach grounds to reduce load before injury or overtraining occurs. Planned and monitored properly, these three strategies allow the athlete to arrive at the major competition adapted rather than accumulated with fatigue.","expl":"This question deliberately pulls the two 'Including' sub-bullets that past papers have never tested — planning and monitoring — across all three variables. Top-band responses show the causal chain (poor recovery → fatigue → degraded movement quality → injury), name specific monitoring tools and use Australian references such as the Australian Dietary Guidelines. Mid-band responses describe good sleep, nutrition and hydration practice without distinguishing planning from monitoring, or cover nutrition and hydration well while treating sleep as a single sentence. Markers should reward responses that show the three interacting rather than treating them as three separate paragraphs; an 8-mark response must include sleep substantively.","src":"new","point":"Explain how sleep, nutrition and hydration can be used to reduce fatigue and positively influence movement and injury prevention","aim":"Planning and monitoring across sleep, nutrition and hydration"},{"id":"NEW-SA-072","year":null,"n":72,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.3"],"stem":"Outline how technology can be used to record and monitor an athlete's training.","criteria":"• Outlines how a form of technology is used to record and monitor training\n• Provides a relevant example — 3\n• Outlines a use of technology in recording or monitoring training — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"Wearable GPS and accelerometer units record the distance covered, the time spent in high-speed running and the number of accelerations in each session. This data is uploaded to training-load management software, which compares the athlete's recent workload to their longer-term average, eg a netballer whose weekly load rises sharply is flagged so the coach can reduce volume in the following session and lower injury risk.","expl":"Tests the third sub-bullet of FA2.5.3 at recall/application level. A top response names a specific technology, states what it measures and links that measurement to a training decision. A mid-band response names a device such as a heart rate monitor or smartwatch and says it 'tracks training' without identifying the data or its use. Markers should accept video analysis, heart rate and HRV monitoring, force plates or load-management software as the chosen technology.","src":"new","point":"Examine the role technology can play to improve performance","aim":"recording and monitoring training and performance"},{"id":"NEW-SA-073","year":null,"n":73,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.3"],"stem":"Explain how TWO training innovations can be used to improve an athlete's performance.","criteria":"• Provides a detailed explanation of how TWO training innovations improve performance\n• Makes clear links between the innovation, the adaptation produced and the performance outcome\n• Provides relevant examples — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of how TWO training innovations improve performance\n• Provides relevant example(s) — 4\n• Describes how training innovation(s) can improve performance\n• Provides an example — 3\n• Outlines a training innovation and/or its effect on performance — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"Altitude training in a simulated altitude chamber exposes an athlete to reduced oxygen availability, stimulating the release of erythropoietin and increasing red blood cell and haemoglobin concentration. A 10 000 m runner who sleeps at simulated altitude but trains at sea level maintains training intensity while improving oxygen transport, so more oxygen reaches the working muscles and aerobic power at race pace improves.\n\nBlood flow restriction training applies a cuff to the proximal limb during low-load resistance work. This creates a hypoxic, metabolically stressful environment in the muscle so that hypertrophy and strength gains occur at loads of around 20–30% of maximum. This is valuable for an athlete rehabilitating after knee reconstruction, who can build quadriceps strength without placing high mechanical load through the healing joint, allowing an earlier return to full training.","expl":"Targets the 'training innovations' sub-bullet. The separator between bands is causal reasoning: a top-band response follows the chain from the innovation to the physiological adaptation to the performance or rehabilitation outcome for a named athlete, whereas a mid-band response describes what the innovation involves and asserts that it 'improves fitness' without the adaptation in between. Heat chambers, hypoxic masks, plyometrics with contrast loading and anti-gravity treadmills are also acceptable innovations. Responses that describe equipment advances (eg carbon-plated shoes) rather than training innovations should be capped, as the question specifies innovations in training.","src":"new","point":"Examine the role technology can play to improve performance","aim":"training innovations"},{"id":"NEW-SA-074","year":null,"n":74,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.3"],"stem":"Examine the role technology can play to improve performance.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive examination of the role of technology in improving performance\n• Examines training innovations, equipment advances, and recording and monitoring of training and performance\n• Makes explicit reference to the stimulus and to relevant examples\n• Presents a considered judgement about the extent of technology's contribution, including its limitations — 7–8\n• Provides a thorough examination of the role of technology in improving performance\n• Refers to more than one aspect of technology and to relevant examples\n• Makes some reference to the stimulus and to limitations of technology — 5–6\n• Describes ways technology is used to improve performance\n• Provides example(s) — 3–4\n• Outlines a use of technology in sport — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"Technology contributes to improved performance in three main ways. Training innovations such as altitude and heat chambers and blood flow restriction training allow athletes to generate adaptations — increased haemoglobin concentration, improved thermoregulation, hypertrophy at low mechanical load — that ordinary training cannot produce as efficiently. Equipment advances change the mechanics of performance itself: carbon-plated footwear reduces the metabolic cost of running and has coincided with sharp improvements in distance times, just as full-body polyurethane swimsuits reduced drag until World Aquatics ruled them out of bounds in 2010 because results had come to reflect the suit rather than the swimmer. Recording and monitoring technology, as Statement 1 describes, uses GPS and accelerometer units, force plates, heart rate variability and sleep data to quantify load and readiness so that programs are individualised and injury risk is reduced — an athlete showing a sustained decline in HRV can be unloaded before fatigue becomes injury.\n\nHowever, the contribution of technology is uneven and conditional. Statement 2 identifies the equity issue: the cost of laboratories, wearables and analysis staff means the same technology that lifts institute athletes is unavailable to community and regional athletes, widening the performance gap for reasons unrelated to talent or work. There is also the risk of over-reliance on data, where objective load figures are treated as more trustworthy than an athlete's own report of soreness or fatigue, and where data is collected without the coaching expertise required to interpret it. Governing bodies have had to intervene where equipment advances threatened fair comparison between athletes, which shows that the boundary between improving performance and replacing it is contested rather than settled.\n\nOn balance, technology plays a significant enabling role — it refines training prescription, protects athletes from excessive load and marginally improves the mechanics of performance — but it amplifies existing resource inequalities and cannot substitute for training quality, coaching judgement or athlete ability.","expl":"This is the content point's own verb, applied across all three sub-bullets. A top-band response is structured by the three sub-bullets, uses precise examples (named devices, named adaptations, a named governing-body ruling), engages both stimulus statements, and reaches a qualified judgement that acknowledges cost and equity of access, over-reliance on data and prohibited equipment. A mid-band response lists technologies and their benefits accurately but treats the stimulus as decoration and offers no limitations, leaving the response descriptive rather than examined. Note for markers: this question overlaps 2024 Q31(b) ('To what extent have advancements in sporting technology improved performance?') in subject matter but not in task — the stimulus and the required coverage of all three sub-bullets distinguish it.","src":"new","point":"Examine the role technology can play to improve performance","aim":"training innovations; equipment advances; recording and monitoring training and performance","stim":"<div class=\"stimbox extract\"><div class=\"stimlabel\">STIMULUS</div><p>Two statements. Statement 1 — a sport scientist at the Australian Institute of Sport: 'Everything we do now is measured. Force plates, GPS, sleep and readiness data — we can see an athlete's fatigue before they feel it.' Statement 2 — a coach at a regional athletics club: 'We have a stopwatch and a set of cones. My athletes compete against people who have a laboratory behind them.'</p></div>"},{"id":"NEW-SA-075","year":null,"n":75,"part":"","marks":3,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.5"],"stem":"Describe how the use of analgesics in the management of injury can place an athlete at risk.","criteria":"• Describes how analgesic use in injury management places an athlete at risk\n• Provides a relevant example — 3\n• Outlines a risk associated with the use of analgesics in injury management — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"Analgesics such as anti-inflammatories, and local anaesthetic injections, reduce or remove pain without treating the underlying injury. Because pain is the body's warning signal, the athlete may continue to load the injured area, eg a footballer given a local anaesthetic to play in a final may run and change direction on a damaged ankle ligament, converting a moderate sprain into a season-ending injury and delaying full recovery.","expl":"Targets the injury management half of the content point, which the eight existing short-answer questions in the bank have never addressed directly. A top-band response makes the masking mechanism explicit — pain removed, injury unchanged, protective feedback lost — and applies it to a named scenario. A mid-band response says only that analgesics 'hide the injury' or lists side effects such as gastric irritation without connecting them to further injury. Credit is also available for over-reliance on medication delaying appropriate assessment and rehabilitation.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the impact of drug use on injury management and improving performance","aim":"health implications (injury management)"},{"id":"NEW-SA-076","year":null,"n":76,"part":"","marks":5,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.5"],"stem":"Explain how the drug testing procedures used in Australian sport aim to deter athletes from using prohibited substances.","criteria":"• Provides a detailed explanation of how drug testing procedures deter athletes from using prohibited substances\n• Refers to a range of procedures and links each to its deterrent effect\n• Provides relevant examples — 5\n• Provides a sound explanation of how drug testing procedures deter drug use\n• Refers to relevant procedures and example(s) — 4\n• Describes drug testing procedures used in Australian sport — 3\n• Outlines a drug testing procedure and/or its purpose — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"Sport Integrity Australia administers testing under the World Anti-Doping Code, and each element of the process is designed to raise the likelihood of detection. Out-of-competition testing with no advance notice removes the option of using substances such as anabolic steroids or EPO during a training phase and clearing them before competition; this is enforced through the whereabouts requirement, under which athletes in the registered testing pool nominate a location for a daily 60-minute window, and three missed tests or filing failures in twelve months is itself an anti-doping rule violation. The Athlete Biological Passport further reduces the value of micro-dosing, because abnormal variation in an athlete's own haematological markers over time provides evidence of doping even when no substance is detected in a sample. The principle of strict liability means an athlete is responsible for whatever is found in their sample regardless of intent, which deters careless supplement use, while sanctions of up to four years for a first violation — with loss of results, funding and sponsorship — make the consequences of detection severe enough to outweigh the perceived benefit.","expl":"Covers the 'drug testing' sub-bullet using current Australian arrangements rather than the superseded ASADA framing. The band separator is the explanatory link: a top-band response names procedures (in- and out-of-competition testing, whereabouts, the Biological Passport, strict liability, sanctions) and explains for each why it changes an athlete's calculation, whereas a mid-band response lists the procedures accurately but leaves the deterrent effect implied. Note that 2016 Q30(a) asked for advantages and disadvantages of drug testing and 2021 Q31(a)(ii) asked about introducing testing at a championship — this question is distinct in requiring the mechanism of deterrence.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the impact of drug use on injury management and improving performance","aim":"drug testing"},{"id":"NEW-SA-077","year":null,"n":77,"part":"","marks":8,"topic":"Training","subs":["Sustained performance and injury"],"sids":["FA2.5.5"],"stem":"Discuss the ethical considerations associated with the use of performance enhancing drugs by elite athletes.","criteria":"• Provides a comprehensive discussion of the ethical considerations associated with the use of performance enhancing drugs\n• Presents a range of ethical arguments and their counter-arguments\n• Makes explicit reference to relevant examples\n• Supports the discussion with an informed position — 7–8\n• Provides a thorough discussion of ethical considerations associated with performance enhancing drug use\n• Presents more than one ethical argument and relevant example(s) — 5–6\n• Describes ethical issues associated with performance enhancing drug use\n• Provides example(s) — 3–4\n• Outlines an ethical issue associated with drug use in sport — 2\n• Provides relevant information — 1","sample":"The central ethical objection to performance enhancing drugs is fairness. Sport is premised on comparing athletes competing under equivalent conditions, and an athlete using anabolic steroids or EPO gains an advantage from pharmacology rather than training, which devalues the result for every clean competitor and, in the case of doping that is later exposed, retrospectively distorts an entire era of a sport. Against this it is argued that advantages in sport are already unequal — genetics, altitude chambers, sports science support and national funding all differ — and that prohibition therefore draws an arbitrary line. The counter is that the prohibited list is drawn on three criteria, including harm to health and violation of the spirit of sport, so the line reflects athlete welfare and not just competitive parity.\n\nCoercion is a second consideration. Where doping becomes normalised in a sport, athletes face a choice between using drugs and losing selection, income and career, which is not a free choice at all. This is sharpened for junior and developing athletes influenced by coaches or support staff, and it explains why the Code sanctions coaches and doctors as well as athletes. Athlete welfare weighs heavily here: the health implications of steroids, EPO and stimulants — infertility, hypertension, thrombosis, cardiac arrhythmia — are borne by the athlete alone, often long after the career has ended, and cannot be genuinely consented to under career pressure.\n\nElite athletes are also role models. Adolescents who see doping tolerated at the top of a sport are more likely to accept image- and performance-enhancing drug use themselves, extending the health cost well beyond elite sport. Finally, there are ethical concerns about the testing regime itself: whereabouts requirements intrude substantially on an athlete's private life, and strict liability can sanction an athlete who ingested a contaminated supplement without fault. These are real costs, but they are defensible as the price of a system that protects clean athletes, provided sanctioning takes degree of fault into account.\n\nOn balance, the arguments from fairness, coercion and welfare provide strong ethical grounds for prohibition, while the intrusiveness of testing obliges anti-doping authorities to administer the system proportionately.","expl":"Uses the content point's own verb on the 'ethical considerations' sub-bullet. A top-band response argues rather than lists: it puts each ethical claim against its strongest counter-argument (fairness against the 'inequality already exists' argument, strict liability against contamination), covers at least three of fairness, coercion, role modelling and athlete welfare, and reaches a supported position. A mid-band response asserts that drug use 'is cheating and is unhealthy' with an example or two and does not engage the opposing view. Markers should not require a particular conclusion, only that the position taken is supported by the arguments presented. This differs from 2018 Q31(b) (evaluate the use of PEDs) and 2023 Q31(b) (consequences of testing positive) by confining the task to the ethical dimension.","src":"new","point":"Discuss the impact of drug use on injury management and improving performance","aim":"ethical considerations"}];
